JING — It seems scary, even crazy: talk of a “sea of fire” and an “arc of destruction,” nuclear missiles slamming into distant shores. North Korea, an “isolated state,” as we’re constantly told by media reports, hurls invective at the world while its people, abused, hungry and cold, are led by an apparently well-fed young man, Kim Jong-un, who sits in front of shabby-looking computers running nuclear programs that are going, literally, ballistic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/world/asia/02NorKorRDV/02NorKorRDV-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea: Not Crazy but Very Misunderstood', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carney, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Landler, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CNN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pyongyang (North Korea)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T04:32:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0d38dff-78d8-5e85-b95d-079d44d3ffd1', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0d38dff-78d8-5e85-b95d-079d44d3ffd1'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:15 P.M. (HBO) INTOLERABLE CRUELTY (2003) George Clooney, with his chiseled chin and blazing-white teeth, plays Miles Massey, the crème de la crème of Los Angeles divorce lawyers, in this film from Joel and Ethan Coen. Catherine Zeta-Jones, above with Mr. Clooney, is Marylin, a silken beauty and Miles’s latest client, who becomes mired in a vicious divorce after she catches her adulterous husband (Edward Herrmann) cheating yet again. Billy Bob Thornton is her Texas millionaire beau and Miles’s chief rival for her money-hungry affections. She may be a victim, but she’s no innocent. “The Coens, bless their hearts, are too smart to apologize for avarice — it’s what gave screwball comedies a kick and sends this movie straight to your head,” Elvis Mitchell wrote in The New York Times. “Keep in mind that it’s only a minor rush, but a rush nonetheless.” He added: “It has a Jell-O-shot smoothness that makes you a little dizzy afterward in recollecting how assured and unremittingly smart ‘Intolerable Cruelty’ is. The second word in the title is a little too apt; this movie should pack a license to kill.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T05:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6313aa39-c6d8-5a56-9bf1-c16a8bea1382', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6313aa39-c6d8-5a56-9bf1-c16a8bea1382'}, {'abstract': 'March Madness may be the best three weeks in sports, but baseball’s opening day is the most invigorating 24 hours we have. A celebration of new life, a new season, legitimate hope and a clean slate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/sports/baseball/walking-to-yankee-stadium-for-opening-day.html', 'snippet': 'March Madness may be the best three weeks in sports, but baseball’s opening day is the most i