ne, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T08:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58876535-82a3-574c-8575-544ca0577fb8', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58876535-82a3-574c-8575-544ca0577fb8'}, {'abstract': 'Ron Haviv hoped images of ethnic cleansing in Bosnia in 1992 would stop the killings. They did not, but were recently used as evidence against commanders charged in the genocide.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/photography-in-the-docket-as-evidence/', 'snippet': 'Ron Haviv hoped images of ethnic cleansing in Bosnia in 1992 would stop the killings. They did not, but were recently used as evidence against commanders charged in the genocide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a cool spring day at the beginning of the Bosnian war in 1992, Ron Haviv watched Serbian paramilitary soldiers pull a middle-aged Muslim couple from their home in Bijeljina. Shots rang out, and although members of the Arkan Tigers militia had warned him not to take photographs, Mr. Haviv stepped behind a truck and squeezed off a few frames.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photography in the Docket, as Evidence', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karadzic, Radovan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raznatovic, Zeljko', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Arkan's Tigers", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Croatia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sarajevo (Bosnia and Herzegovina)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yugoslavia', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T09:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9da23b4a-843b-5b82-b91d-064c9e4568d4', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9da23b4a-843b-5b82-b91d-064c9e4568d4'}, {'abstract': 'Why do you think child stars are so prone to public “meltdowns”? Or do you think they are only acting the way any young adult might, except in the very public eye?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/do-you-think-child-stars-have-it-rough/', 'snippet': 'Why do you think child stars are so prone to public “meltdowns”? Or do you think they are only acting the way any young adult might, except in the very public eye?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The public spectacle of child stars self destructing is almost clichéd. Think Britney Spears in her 20s, or the continuing struggles of Lindsay Lohan. But what’s really going on?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Think Child Stars Have It Rough?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T09:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fba6b691-fd1a-5ba5-81f2-40f728c0dc1d', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fba6b691-fd1a-5ba5-81f2-40f728c0dc1d'}, {'abstract': 'In talking about women at work, let’s not forget the role of men at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/how-shared-diaper-duty-could-stimulate-the-economy.html', 'snippet': 'In talking about women at work, let’s not forget the role of men at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I happen to be an educated young woman who loves her job, sometimes gushingly, occasionally annoyingly. And yet, even in this enlightened age, I’ve had two relationships end — at least in large part — thanks to that clammy-palmed discussion in which couples plot hypothetical milestones and life goals. The gentlemen in question said that, somewhere in our semicharted future, they expected me to quit my job. At least for a couple of years, anyway, in order to be the kind of hypothetical mother they wanted to raise their hypothetical kids, if that hypothetical day ever came.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07Economy-t_CA0/mag-07Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'hei