t_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c07d327b-5832-5ec4-b8ef-b1e6d96c238c', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c07d327b-5832-5ec4-b8ef-b1e6d96c238c'}, {'abstract': 'Was the marriage-procreation link invented by desperate social conservatives?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/marriage-procreation-and-historical-amnesia/', 'snippet': 'Was the marriage-procreation link invented by desperate social conservatives?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not surprisingly, my Sunday column raising the possibility that the successes of the gay marriage movement might be having some modest influence on marriage’s overall decline left liberals (and many others) unpersuaded. Kevin Drum has a usefully representative rebuttal, whose three major points I think I’ll take up in separate posts. Here’s the first, which dismisses the idea that the push for same-sex marriage has tended to weaken the legal and cultural connection between marriage and procreation by … blaming social conservatives for injecting that supposed connection into the debate in the first place:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Marriage, Procreation and Historical Amnesia', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Robert P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heritage Foundation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T15:25:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c81e5d5-780e-5a67-97f9-28f8c94626af', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c81e5d5-780e-5a67-97f9-28f8c94626af'}, {'abstract': 'How Civil War prisons contributed to the growth of baseball.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/americas-pastime-behind-bars/', 'snippet': 'How Civil War prisons contributed to the growth of baseball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Civil War prisons were terrible places: captured solders suffered and died by the thousands from malnutrition, disease and exposure to the elements. But in several Northern and Southern prisons, a few fortunate inmates were able to enjoy, for a moment, a lighter side of life: baseball.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Pastime, Behind Bars', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cartwright, Alexander', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-02T15:25:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Kirsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirsch', 'quali