desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abbd07f4-a5c9-5092-b728-0591a3a33695', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abbd07f4-a5c9-5092-b728-0591a3a33695'}, {'abstract': 'The House of Waris has teamed up with the eyewear brand Illesteva to create what might be the single most essential frame of the summer.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/shades-on-house-of-waris-and-illesteva/', 'snippet': 'The House of Waris has teamed up with the eyewear brand Illesteva to create what might be the single most essential frame of the summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Waris Ahluwalia of House of Waris is to jewelry as Daniel Silberman and Jus Ske of Illesteva are to eyewear: they design culty, mostly unisex accessories; pal around with downtown’s creative peerage; and are prone to participating in one-off collaborations. It seems inevitable, then, that the two brands would end up joining forces with one another. A single eyewear style resulting from a single design session, House of Waris for Illesteva’s sunglasses ($250) might be the single most essential frame of the summer. Handmade in France, the 1960s-influenced unisex style comes in five colors, all of which are refreshingly subdued. “They look good on everybody,” says Silberman, who also wears a pair as his prescription eyeglasses. Adds Ahluwalia, “The idea was to help the world look as sexy as possible, even in the glaring sun.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shades On | House of Waris and Illesteva', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyeglasses', 'rank': 1, '