, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Jeffrey D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T01:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3043e1f-b719-5616-a34d-4fc89de1fc47', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3043e1f-b719-5616-a34d-4fc89de1fc47'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s pledge of $100 million is a modest but welcome start to helping us understand how the brain works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/opinion/obama-makes-a-giant-leap-for-brain-research.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s pledge of $100 million is a modest but welcome start to helping us understand how the brain works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama officially announced his new brain research initiative on Tuesday, with a pledge to put $100 million in his 2014 budget to support work at three federal agencies. It is a modest but welcome start for an effort that could transform our understanding of how the brain works and help researchers find new ways to treat and prevent brain disorders like epilepsy and Alzheimer’s. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Frontiers of Brain Research', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Frontiers of Brain Research', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Science Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T01:18:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ade686c9-32b0-547d-bea5-988cd2cf2b6b', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ade686c9-32b0-547d-bea5-988cd2cf2b6b'}, {'abstract': 'In its upfront presentation to advertisers, Bravo explained how it can incorporate brands into scenes from its reality shows.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/02/bravo-discusses-mixing-advertising-with-its-shows/', 'snippet': 'In its upfront presentation to advertisers, Bravo explained how it can incorporate brands into scenes from its reality shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anderson Davis is not among the stars of Bravo series that the channel likes to call “Bravolebrities.” Nonetheless, he was treated like one on Monday night during an episode of “Watch What Happens Live”: he served as the bartender; joked with the host, Andy Cohen; and even took off his shirt to reveal a toned upper body that would not be out of place on one of Bravo’s “Real Housewives” shows.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bravo Discusses Mixing Advertising With Its Shows', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Andy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aspire', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bravo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T01:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf4dbb3b-e3a2-5fd5-83c7-0e0981f5be5e', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf4dbb3b-e3a2-5fd5-83c7-0e0981f5be5e'}, {'abstract': 'Swift approval of the bipartisan bill would send a message to the entire country that some issues must rise above politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/opinion/the-connecticut-gun-control-package.html', 'snippet': 'Swift approval of the bipartisan bill would send a message to the entire country that some issues must rise above politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Connecticut Legislature is scheduled to vote Wednesday on what leaders of both parties call the most far-reaching gun-legislation package in the country. The bill is thorough and bipartisan. Swift approval would send a message to the entire country that, as John McKinney, the Republican State Senate minority leader, observed, some issues must rise above politics. Mr. McKinney represents Newtown, where, in December, a gunman armed with a Bushmaster XM-15 military-style rifle massacred 20 children and six adults at Sandy Hook Elementary School. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Connecticut Gun-Control Package', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': ' The Connecticut Gun-Control Package', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T01:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f54b99ce-611a-5a41-87cd-3b4ac46a4604', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f54b99ce-611a-5a41-87cd-3b4ac46a4604'}, {'abstract': 'The skills of a small, elite group of American soldiers, including Green Berets, are useful in a wide range of tasks if successfully integrated with conventional units.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/world/military-plans-broader-role-for-special-operations.html', 'snippet': 'The skills of a small, elite group of American soldiers, including Green Berets, are useful in a wide range of tasks if successfully integrated with conventional units.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT BRAGG, N.C. — Here at the headquarters of the Army Special Operations Command, planning is well under way for a significantly increased presence in Africa, Asia and Latin America for the Special Forces soldiers with the distinctive green berets who were the first American troops into Afghanistan after the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001. Special Operations forces, which include Green Berets, Navy SEAL teams, the Rangers and specialized aviation units, have historically been a small corner of the military and not always embraced by conventional commanders. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main':