'snippet': 'An environmental coalition on Monday charged that coal and coal dust is polluting the Columbia River Gorge, and pledged to sue mining companies and a railroad company if they do not halt the spills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An environmental coalition on Monday charged that coal and coal dust spilled from railroad hoppers is polluting the scenic Columbia River Gorge, and pledged to sue mining companies and the Burlington Northern Santa Fe Railroad if they do not halt the spills. Representatives of the Sierra Club and other conservation groups said they were invoking a clause in the Clean Water Act that permits lawsuits if those accused of polluting do not remedy environmental problems within 60 days. Each coal train leaves behind 10,000 pounds of loose coal in the gorge containing heavy metals and other harmful chemicals, the groups maintained. But there has been little research on the issue, they added, and the environmental impact is unclear. The groups declined to release their own studies of the contaminants. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oregon: Groups Give Notice of Suit Over Coal Dust', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Northwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oregon: Groups Give Notice of Suit Over Coal Dust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Columbia River (Pacific Northwest)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BNSF Railway', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sierra Club', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T03:32:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/24e1a088-64a2-5447-8d53-82002dd100c3', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24e1a088-64a2-5447-8d53-82002dd100c3'}, {'abstract': 'Harvard’s search of staff e-mail accounts went further than previously disclosed, administrators said Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/education/massachusetts-harvard-admits-deeper-search.html', 'snippet': 'Harvard’s search of staff e-mail accounts went further than previously disclosed, administrators said Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harvard’s search of staff e-mail accounts went further than previously disclosed, administrators said Tuesday. They announced that an outside lawyer would investigate the matter and a task force would review privacy policies. President Drew Gilpin Faust and two deans revealed the developments in a faculty meeting, and the deans apologized. In investigating leaks about a cheating scandal, administrators searched subject lines in the accounts that 16 instructors have as resident deans. On Tuesday, administrators said that with one resident dean, the search included that dean’s personal Harvard account, but no e-mails were opened. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Harvard Admits Deeper Search', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Harvard Admits Deeper Search', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'nam