t': 'Cozy escapes, for the recluse and the foodie alike, abound in Great Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/now-booking-new-beds-in-blighty/', 'snippet': 'Cozy escapes, for the recluse and the foodie alike, abound in Great Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chewton Glen, a palatial Georgian-era resort and spa in New Milton, England, recently built 12 “treehouse suites.” The nomenclature is a bit off the mark because not one of them is actually built into or even adequately surrounded by trees. Rather they appear like stilt homes on the Outer Banks. Still, the interiors are cozy, modern and ample. The living rooms have big half-moon couches upholstered in soft Balmoral tweed, and small wood-burning stoves that are all set up and ready to light. Among Chewton Glen’s more gimmicky offerings are its breakfast and other snack baskets, which you can order any time of day. They are slipped in from outside of the “treehouse” and arrive in a cupboard in the suite’s kitchenette. It’s a romantic and almost courtly service. All of the suites have wraparound terraces and Jacuzzis, with views onto a wide heath that was covered in silvery frost when I visited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Booking | New Beds in Blighty', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T13:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee096df5-8ffa-5d51-b5a5-c0583cab76d1', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee096df5-8ffa-5d51-b5a5-c0583cab76d1'}, {'abstract': 'Delivering video messages to rural farmers in poor countries promises to be an effective method in fighting hunger and malnutrition.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/where-youtube-meets-the-farm/', 'snippet': 'Delivering video messages to rural farmers in poor countries promises to be an effective method in fighting hunger and malnutrition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half of the children in India are chronically malnourished. So imagine the potential benefits if there were a simple way to increase the milk production from cows by, say, a quart per week? Or imagine if there were a better way for small farmers to cultivate rice — the staple food of half the world — one that required no costly inputs, used less water, and substantially increased yields?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where YouTube Meets the Farm', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T13:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3606560c-67f6-5f94-a9a0-4575d773bf89', 'word_count': 1898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3606560c-67f6-5f94-a9a0-4575d773bf89'}, {'abstract': 'Matthew M. Taylor a