ets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T16:54:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By CRAIG O'SHANNESSY", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'SHANNESSY", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a784a785-c32a-53e5-813e-9e5c52781f57', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a784a785-c32a-53e5-813e-9e5c52781f57'}, {'abstract': 'W Hotels plans to open 11 properties in Asia, doubling its footprint in the region.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/expanding-in-asia/', 'snippet': 'W Hotels plans to open 11 properties in Asia, doubling its footprint in the region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re going to Asia, expect to see that glowing W emblem more often. The hotel chain has just opened a hotel in Guangzhou — its first in mainland China — and plans to open 10 more properties in cities including Shanghai (above), Mumbai and Kuala Lumpur, doubling its footprint in Asia.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding in Asia', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hotels: Expanding in Asia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T17:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Brennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/116c5064-9eb5-5911-8cfc-548295475f4d', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/116c5064-9eb5-5911-8cfc-548295475f4d'}, {'abstract': 'In Turin, a new exhibition of works by the pioneering 20th-century designer features prototypes and rare items.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/a-passion-for-jean-prouv/', 'snippet': 'In Turin, a new exhibition of works by the pioneering 20th-century designer features prototypes and rare items.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A Passion for Jean Prouvé,” which opens on Saturday at the Pinacoteca Giovanni e Marella Agnelli\xa0in Turin, Italy, examines the architecture and furniture of Prouvé (1901-84), the pioneering 20th-century designer, from the collections of Laurence and Patrick Seguin. The couple, who own Galerie Patrick Seguin in Paris, which specializes in 20th-century Modernist design, have been collecting Prouvé’s work since the late 1980s. The exhibition, which includes about 40 pieces, features works\xa0—\xa0many of them prototypes or rare items\xa0—\xa0that range in scale from the chair that Prouvé designed for the University of Antony in 1954 to his aluminum Maison Metropole, a 1949 design for rural schools and teacher housing that could be mass produced, although only two of these buildings were ever made. This one has been reassembled (a stop-motion film of the construction can be seen on the Pinacoteca’s Web site) on the famous test track atop the Lingotto, the former Fiat factory that now houses the Pinacoteca.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Passion for Jean Prouvé', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prouve, Jean', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-03T17:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/450724d3-b0f0-5947-86dc-16c4597a16d0', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/450724d3-b0f0-5947-86dc-16c4597a16d0'}, {'abstract': 'By tracking one patient’s immune response, scientists have discovered how a series of mutations led to an antibody that could defeat many H.I.V. strains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/health/aids-vaccine-path-suggested-by-study.html', 'snippet': 'By tracking one patient’s immune response, scientists have discovered how a series of mutations led to an antibody that could defeat many H.I.V. strains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what may be an important step toward a long-elusive AIDS vaccine, American researchers have minutely tracked one person’s powerful immune response to the virus to see how a series of mutations led to an antibody that can defeat many H.I.V. strains.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Possible Path to Vaccine for AIDS Is Suggested', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Possible Path To Vaccine For AIDS Is Suggested', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Viruses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 