ew York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scandal Suggests Narrower Corruption Law Still Survived ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scandal Suggests Narrower Corruption Law Still Survived', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Malcolm A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bruno, Joseph L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T01:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a09bb2c1-e277-5002-8e7a-10d0354e0d52', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a09bb2c1-e277-5002-8e7a-10d0354e0d52'}, {'abstract': 'People often talk about "out of touch" billionaires running for office, but could they be less susceptible to bribes?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2013/04/03/are-rich-politicians-less-corruptible/', 'snippet': 'People often talk about "out of touch" billionaires running for office, but could they be less susceptible to bribes?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Rich Politicians Less Corruptible?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T01:40:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d90a4cf-e2df-5da4-8394-718970d0e685', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d90a4cf-e2df-5da4-8394-718970d0e685'}, {'abstract': 'The tape of an abusive basketball coach is a timely warning not only to Rutgers but to university presidents everywhere of an out-of-control sports culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/opinion/upon-further-review-at-rutgers.html', 'snippet': 'The tape of an abusive basketball coach is a timely warning not only to Rutgers but to university presidents everywhere of an out-of-control sports culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November, an extraordinary videotape showing Rutgers basketball players being physically and verbally abused at practice by their ranting coach, Mike Rice, came into the possession of university officials. With Rutgers intent on becoming a first-tier power in the lucrative world of university athletics, officials at the New Jersey institution foolishly sought to minimize the damage by announcing a three-game suspension of Mr. Rice and a $50,000 fine, while keeping the extent and details of his misbehavior vague. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Upon Further Review at Rutgers', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upon Further Review at Rutgers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Mike Jr (1969- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pernetti, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdock, Eric', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball (College)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T01:40:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb86be6d-10f1-5ff6-97dc-1979f2fa3a2f', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb86be6d-10f1-5ff6-97dc-1979f2fa3a2f'}, {'abstract': 'A convoluted bribery plot over a spot on the New York City mayoral ballot exposes the worst in the state’s political culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/opinion/more-rot-in-new-york-state-goverment.html', 'snippet': 'A convoluted bribery plot over a spot on the New York City mayoral ballot exposes the worst in the state’s political culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The corruption scandal that blew up in Albany this week is the equivalent of a Russian nesting doll: a federal prosecutor accusing a state senator of conspiring with county party bosses and a city councilman to buy a spot on a city ballot and to steer state money to a village mayor. It’s a wonder it didn’t wrap in a local school board and the P.T.A. You could hardly ask for a better all-purpose illustration of the rot that pervades New York State’s political culture.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Rot in Albany', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Rot in Albany', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': 