 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T13:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c736bd3-2b05-5d10-a126-58bfa915741f', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c736bd3-2b05-5d10-a126-58bfa915741f'}, {'abstract': 'Everyone seemed angry on Wednesday — at the Yankees, at Rutgers and at Mark Cuban.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/sports/tough-days-for-yankees-and-knights.html', 'snippet': 'Everyone seemed angry on Wednesday — at the Yankees, at Rutgers and at Mark Cuban.', 'lead_paragraph': "For a day that started with so much promise, Wednesday quickly turned grumpy. It started as the day people would feel some sort of satisfaction at Rutgers dumping its abusive basketball coach, Mike Rice, but then became the dissatisfying one with no retribution for a wishy-washy athletic director and backside-covering president. It started as the day that would be the N.H.L.'s annual player-swapping frenzy — the Canadian national holiday that is the trading deadline — and became the one in which teams desperately tried to find takers for their huge contract mistakes, with varying degrees of failure. And then it became the day in which everyone took potshots at Mark Cuban’s insistence that Brittney Griner could play for the Mavericks.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/sports/ncaabasketball/05leadingpic/05leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/sports/ncaabasketball/05leadingpic/05leadingpic-articleLarge