ques', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunham, Lena', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zizek, Slavoj', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T18:10:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.c. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c415265e-8865-5979-9899-7858325de694', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c415265e-8865-5979-9899-7858325de694'}, {'abstract': 'Once, a rape accuser might have found the word “whore” on a note in her locker and heard accusations about her behavior whispered behind her. Now, this is on social media — and the single good thing about that public ugliness is that it can no longer be ignored.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/why-we-should-thank-twitters-rape-defenders/', 'snippet': 'Once, a rape accuser might have found the word “whore” on a note in her locker and heard accusations about her behavior whispered behind her. Now, this is on social media — and the single good thing about that public ugliness is that it can no lon...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Torrington, Conn., is playing out its own version of what has become the new narrative of the public rape case: as the victims and the accused make their way through the legal process, their defenders and detractors make their way onto social media, and opinions long confined to high school hallways, parent coffee gatherings and local gossip become public property in a very different way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why We Should Thank Twitter’s Rape Defenders', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'v