://interactive/e5b239ea-981a-5fd5-9d6e-fa56d29085a1'}, {'abstract': 'Anita Lo, Dale Talde, Sara Jenkins and other New York chefs recall infested oysters, exploding chickens and kitchen disasters that would make a home cook cry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/new-york-chefs-recall-their-worst-cooking-disasters.html', 'snippet': 'Anita Lo, Dale Talde, Sara Jenkins and other New York chefs recall infested oysters, exploding chickens and kitchen disasters that would make a home cook cry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York chefs recall their worst kitchen disasters.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'True Tales of Hell in the Kitchen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Hair and Hearing Were the Main Victims’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Jessica', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T18:58:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interviews by Jessica Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interviews', 'middlename': 'Jessica', 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecf04d2a-055a-54d0-b241-05a4eb2c6a98', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecf04d2a-055a-54d0-b241-05a4eb2c6a98'}, {'abstract': 'Abe Schoener’s quest to thrill, infuriate and baffle oenophiles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/the-fearless-risk-loving-winemaker.html', 'snippet': 'Abe Schoener’s quest to thrill, infuriate and baffle oenophiles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere in the manicured farmlands of Napa Valley, a 52-year-old winemaker named Abe Schoener stood in a puny and weed-choked tract of land surrounded by 40 gray and contorted barren vines, which he surveyed with paternal satisfaction. “My view when I started leasing this was, It’s 60-year-old-vine sauvignon blanc,” he said, smiling. “How bad could it be?”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07wine5/mag-07wineprof-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fearless, Risk-Loving Winemaker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Mad Czar of Verdelho', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draper, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoener, Abe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scholium Project', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T18:59:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Draper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a52dfef-1cae-5426-8410-19b5966c0098', 'word_count': 2879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a52dfef-1cae-5426-8410-19b5966c0098'}, {'abstract': 'One woman’s crusade to spread the gospel of meat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/the-proper-way-to-eat-a-pig.html', 'snippet': 'One woman’s crusade to spread the gospel of meat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent morning in Portland, Ore., Camas Davis was teaching nine high-school kids how to butcher a pig. A 17-year-old named Mady called dibs on the front trotter, slicing through the skin near the pig’s ankle, then using a hand saw to cut through the bone. Nathan, 15, moved up the leg and worked through the hock, while Karina, 16, eyed the shoulder. Pushing up the sleeves of her red cardigan, she placed her blade between the fifth and sixth ribs, scored the flesh, then gave the knife a long pull, separating the shoulder from the carcass, but leaving intact the coppa — a muscle around the pig’s neck — in case anyone wanted to roast it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 649}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 947, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1082, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07portland1/07portland1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Proper Way to Eat a Pig', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going Whole Hog', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pork', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butchers and Butchering', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T18:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marnie Hanel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f35af2b-487d-5f72-bf1f-752e86af8e53', 'word_count': 2732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f35af2b-487d-5f72-bf1f-752e86af8e53'}, {'abstract': 'Bank of America’s new A.T.M.’s let customers talk to tellers remotely during some hours of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/an-a-t-m-with-a-real-teller-on-the-screen/', 'snippet': 'Bank of America’s new A.T.M.’s let customers talk to tellers remotely during some hours of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bank of America is introducing new automated teller machines that evoke the bridge of the starship Enterprise, where captain and crew spoke remotely with others in outer space.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An A.T.M., With a Real Teller on the Screen', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A.T.M.’s With a Human Touch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Checks and Checking Accounts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:02:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2524b2c0-e8f8-5b26-b942-7e99601d0cdf', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2524b2c0-e8f8-5b26-b942-7e99601d0cdf'}, {'abstract': 'The new venture, which is in Detroit’s Fisher Building, bought the brand name of an early 20th century maker of E.V.’s.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/a-new-electric-car-with-an-old-name/', 'snippet': 'The new venture, which is in Detroit’s Fisher Building, bought the brand name of an early 20th century maker of E.V.’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fledgling Detroit Electric car company introduced its first product, the fully electric SP:01 two-seat roadster, on Wednesday. Like the Tesla Roadster, the SP:01 will be built on a Lotus Elise chassis. Detroit Electric will handle only final assembly of an initial production run of 999 vehicles.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Electric Car With an Old Name', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Electric Car With an Old Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lotus Cars Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:02:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2413f39-e714-5ad3-b666-a78384002380', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2413f39-e714-5ad3-b666-a78384002380'}, {'abstract': 'It takes an army to feed a village. Or, in this case, the Saturday night dinner rush at Harlem’s hottest restaurant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/the-troops-of-the-red-rooster.html', 'snippet': 'It takes an army to feed a village. Or, in this case, the Saturday night dinner rush at Harlem’s hottest restaurant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each dinner service at Red Rooster, on Lenox Avenue and 125th Street, and Ginny’s Supper Club, its subterranean lounge, requires dozens more staff members than an N.F.L. roster. That’s 10 servers, 10 server’s assistants, 6 runners, 2 stockers, 2 baristas, 1 lead host, 2 more hostesses (“one greeter, one seater”), 6 bartenders, 2 bar backs, 3 floor managers, 1 beverage manager, 1 line cook for each of the 4 stations (grill, sauté, fry and garde manger) in each kitchen, 2 expediters, 10 prep cooks, 6 dishwashers, 3 porters, 1 general manager and an assistant general manager. There are 2 reservationists, 2 event planners, 4 overnight cleaners and 3 receivers to check in deliveries. Oh, yeah, they have a chef too: Marcus Samuelsson, back there with the scarf.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1012, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1012, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0/mag-07RedRooster-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Troops of the Red Rooster', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Takes an Army ...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Staley, Willy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samuelsson, Marcus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Rooster (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Ginny's Supper Club (Manhattan, NY)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:03:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Willy Staley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Willy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af933670-e9d3-56b7-bac5-bc39867eed7e', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af933670-e9d3-56b7-bac5-bc39867eed7e'}, {'abstract': 'Prina pays tribute to Rudolph M. Schindler’s built-in furniture in his latest exhibit, now at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/stephen-prina-as-he-remembered-it/', 'snippet': 'Prina pays tribute to Rudolph M. Schindler’s built-in furniture in his latest exhibit, now at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1980s, Stephen Prina was walking on La Brea Avenue in Los Angeles one night with his friend and fellow artist Christopher Williams when they were attracted to a glowing pink form, in a gallery window, that turned out to be a desk by the Modernist architect Rudolph M. Schindler. It was clear that the desk had been a built-in, and the fact that it had been pried from its setting reminded them of an amputated limb. The memory stayed with Prina over the years and, as he said recently, “art comes out of a persistent idea. I couldn’t stop thinking of it and when it started pestering me, I knew I had to do something with it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stephen Prina | ‘As He Remembered It’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8abb060-0e80-50b1-8c8c-84e4b4d90fcf', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8abb060-0e80-50b1-8c8c-84e4b4d90fcf'}, {'abstract': 'The acquisition of Andrew Bynum and the development of Evan Turner have been key issues for Philadelphia, which is nearing playoff elimination this season.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/keeping-score-a-statistical-look-at-the-76ers-drop-off/', 'snippet': 'The acquisition of Andrew Bynum and the development of Evan Turner have been key issues for Philadelphia, which is nearing playoff elimination this season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Philadelphia 76ers were one of the surprise stories of the 2011-12 season. They started off hot, winning 18 of their first 25 games, and ended the season by pushing the Boston Celtics to seven games in the Eastern Conference semifinals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Sixers Went From the Playoffs to a Losing Year', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bynum, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Evan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia 76ers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Kubatko', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kubatko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eed55365-ebfe-5694-aeb3-caa892e0f280', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eed55365-ebfe-5694-aeb3-caa892e0f280'}, {'abstract': 'Sloane Crosley and Logan Hill will be starting the conversation about “Mad Men” as the show’s penultimate season gets under way.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/talking-mad-men-catching-up-before-the-new-season-begins/', 'snippet': 'Sloane Crosley and Logan Hill will be starting the conversation about “Mad Men” as the show’s penultimate season gets under way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Editor’s note: The author Sloane Crosley and the journalist Logan Hill are friends and fellow “Mad Men” obsessives. (There are fans and then there are those who try to find a poster seen in the background of an episode. That’s Ms. Crosley. Mr. Hill once came up with a list of books Don Draper might read.) Each week, they will chat here about all things “Mad Men,” from Don Draper’s affairs to Trudy Campbell’s outfits. In advance of the sixth-season premiere of the show this Sunday, they’re looking back and offering some predictions. Read on, check out Alessandra Stanley’s take and then return Monday morning for their post-premiere analysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "Talking ‘Mad Men': Catching Up Before the New Season Begins", 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:09:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sloane Crosley and Logan Hill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sloane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crosley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Logan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3509fa0a-a333-55b0-b3d8-0e4d9db3015f', 'word_count': 1573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3509fa0a-a333-55b0-b3d8-0e4d9db3015f'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck Hagel gave his first major policy address as secretary of defense.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/the-limits-of-military-power/', 'snippet': 'Chuck Hagel gave his first major policy address as secretary of defense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chuck Hagel gave his first major policy address as secretary of defense and devoted a good amount of space to a fundamental issue: the parameters of conventional military power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Limits of Military Power', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagel, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0151c7f9-6689-5bfd-afde-7e03d48f30ed', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0151c7f9-6689-5bfd-afde-7e03d48f30ed'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Rob Portman’s new support for gay marriage has intensified and quickened debate over the issue in Ohio, his home state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/us/politics/senator-portman-ohio-fallout-shift-same-sex-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Rob Portman’s new support for gay marriage has intensified and quickened debate over the issue in Ohio, his home state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CINCINNATI — No one heckled Senator Rob Portman during his first appearance before thousands of Ohioans since becoming the most prominent elected Republican in the country to endorse same-sex 