arriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gabriel, Trip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portman, Rob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T19:16:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c3a313c-a08d-5b2c-8ecb-a58a5f95b780', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c3a313c-a08d-5b2c-8ecb-a58a5f95b780'}, {'abstract': 'I look and feel fine, yet the rising blood markers and the scan prove me to be ill. These antithetical perspectives split me in two.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/living-with-cancer-truthiness/', 'snippet': 'I look and feel fine, yet the rising blood markers and the scan prove me to be ill. These antithetical perspectives split me in two.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some cancer patients adopt a “don’t ask, don’t tell” policy with their doctors. Either they are more afraid or more brave than I am. I keep on asking, though the telling sometimes jolts me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/health/05well_gubar/05well_gubar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/health/05well_gubar/05well_gubar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credi