er or instrumentation, he reimagines a score’s structural tension in terms of strange personal or social dramas. Two of his most peculiar gifts, peculiar in an artist of considerable musical literacy and compositional sophistication, are his capacities for responding to a score with a raw innocence and for exposing its deep neurological effect. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN/05MARKMORRIS_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Throbbing in Blood and Sinew', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music Throbbing in Blood and Sinew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morris, Mark, Dance Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T21:23:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fef3ea9-3610-5f68-9a38-5cfd14d57765', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fef3ea9-3610-5f68-9a38-5cfd14d57765'}, {'abstract': '“Vice,” on HBO, exposes American audiences to the grit and gore of international conflicts, but the context is minimal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/arts/television/vice-a-new-approach-to-news-on-hbo.html', 'snippet': '“Vice,” on HBO, exposes American audiences to the grit and gore of international conflicts, but the context is minimal.', 'lead_paragraph': '“This is the world through our eyes,” we’re told at the top of “Vice,” the new HBO program produced by the Brooklyn-based Vice Media, a successful purveyor of video, music and clothing that’s really in the business of selling an attitude. The eyes of Vice are male, between late youth and early middle age and self-consciously gonzo in a dilettantish, 21st-century manner that would make Hunter S. Thompson spit up his Chivas. You can picture him doing it during the series’s introduction, as a voice-over states that “Vice” will “expose the absurdity of the modern condition.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/VICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/VICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/VICE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/VICE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/05VICE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/05VICE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VICE_SPAN/05VICE_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They Bring You the World, in a Way', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They Bring You the World, in a Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vice Media Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vice (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T21:24:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7253443c-b260-540a-b5cb-1c37106dd40a', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7253443c-b260-540a-b5cb-1c37106dd40a'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Redford’s “Company You Keep,” which also features Julie Christie and Susan Sarandon, follows 1960s radicals in later life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/movies/robert-redfords-company-you-keep-revisits-radical-60s.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Redford’s “Company You Keep,” which also features Julie Christie and Susan Sarandon, follows 1960s radicals in later life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A seam of melancholy runs through “The Company You Keep,” Robert Redford’s reflective melodrama about political idealism run amok and the wages of youthful folly. For audiences over 50, in particular, this fictional story of homegrown terrorists sprung from the 1960s counterculture should conjure complicated feelings of pride, shame, anger and regret. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering the Side of the ’60s That Wasn’t All Peace and Love', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remembering the Side of the ’60s That Wasn’t All Peace and Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Company You Keep (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Redford, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaBeouf, Shia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sarandon, Susan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-04T21:24:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/88bb85f7-c401-50f5-9075-9c28e70ebd38', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88bb85f7-c401-50f5-9075-9c28e70ebd38'}, {'abstract': 'The Tokyo Skytree, which opened last May, holds the title as the world’s tallest free-standing tower, and it offers a head-clearing perspective on the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/travel/at-the-tokyo-skytree-views-from-the-top.html', 'snippet': 'The Tokyo Skytree, which opened last May, holds the title as the world’s tallest free-standing tower, and it offers a head-clearing perspective on the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blanketed by haze and poised at the juncture of restless tectonic plates, Tokyo is unlikely territory for sky-high observation decks and city-viewing towers. But defiant Tokyoites insist on building them, and then, once they are in place, eagerly get in line to ride up and have a look. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/travel/07HEASUP1_SPAN-sf/07HEASUP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/travel/07HEASUP1_SPAN-sf/07HEASUP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarge