'By Peter Eavis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eavis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d2e90af-0713-5a9d-952b-060e6df4a397', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d2e90af-0713-5a9d-952b-060e6df4a397'}, {'abstract': '“Roman Vishniac Rediscovered,” at the International Center of Photography, does a lot to round out Vishniac’s story, including his photos of European Jewish life on the eve of the Holocaust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/arts/design/roman-vishniac-show-at-international-center-of-photography.html', 'snippet': '“Roman Vishniac Rediscovered,” at the International Center of Photography, does a lot to round out Vishniac’s story, including his photos of European Jewish life on the eve of the Holocaust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eyewitness to history or manipulative mythmaker? The one blurs so easily into the other in socially concerned photojournalism. Separating truth from fantasy in the Depression-era photographs of impoverished Americans by Walker Evans, Dorothea Lange and others is not easy. The question is especially pertinent in the case of Roman Vishniac, who documented the lives of Orthodox Jews living in Eastern European shtetls in the late 1930s.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VISHNIAC1/VISHNIAC1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VISHNIAC1/VISHNIAC1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 598}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/arts/05VISHNIAC1/VISHNIAC1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1021, 'width': 1024, 's