a062a60917dd'}, {'abstract': 'The push to see depression as a necessary and somehow desirable punishment for past sins is as strong as ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/opinion/Krugman-The-Urge-To-Purge.html', 'snippet': 'The push to see depression as a necessary and somehow desirable punishment for past sins is as strong as ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Great Depression struck, many influential people argued that the government shouldn’t even try to limit the damage. According to Herbert Hoover, Andrew Mellon, his Treasury secretary, urged him to “Liquidate labor, liquidate stocks, liquidate the farmers. ... It will purge the rottenness out of the system.” Don’t try to hasten recovery, warned the famous economist Joseph Schumpeter, because “artificial stimulus leaves part of the work of depressions undone.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Urge to Purge', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Urge To Purge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stockman, David A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stockman, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Great Deformation: The Corruption of Capitalism in America (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T00:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e931e574-1cd8-57eb-815a-e9f10f149a68', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e931e574-1cd8-57eb-815a-e9f10f149a68'}, {'abstract': 'The scourge of 24-hour news is now doing to the weather what it did to public affairs and the stock market.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/gales-gone-wild/', 'snippet': 'The scourge of 24-hour news is now doing to the weather what it did to public affairs and the stock market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Things started to go wrong with the weather when the Weather Channel decided on its own last year to give every winter storm a proper name. Snow, no matter if it was simply falling on cedars, and frost, even if it was historically known as Jack, nipping at your nose, were gone. Thereafter came Winter Storm Brutus, Winter Storm Nemo, Winter Storm Saturn — some serious meteorological thugs. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gales Gone Wild', 'kicker': 'Timothy Egan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weather Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weather Underground (Weather Service)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T01:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe18daf1-b28e-561a-b355-f0e0d46776c3', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe18daf1-b28e-561a-b355-f0e0d46776c3'}, {'abstract': 'The Miami rapper Rick Ross sent Twitter messages to his fans and to his sponsors on Thursday apologizing for a lyric in his song “U.O.E.N.O.” that seems to condone rape.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/04/rick-ross-apologizes-for-lyrics/', 'snippet': 'The Miami rapper Rick Ross sent Twitter messages to his fans and to his sponsors on Thursday apologizing for a lyric in his song “U.O.E.N.O.” that seems to condone rape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Miami rapper Rick Ross sent Twitter