p a scheme to steal a valuable cask of whisky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/movies/the-angels-share-is-ken-loachs-new-film-set-in-scotland.html', 'snippet': 'Ken Loach’s new film, “The Angels’ Share,” is about four unemployed Scottish scofflaws who cook up a scheme to steal a valuable cask of whisky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the new comedy “The Angels’ Share” four young unemployed Glaswegians dressed in kilts set out on a journey into the Scottish Highlands. The soundtrack even features the infectious anthem “I’m Gonna Be (500 Miles)” by their countrymen the Proclaimers. But these miscreants could not belong less to the mountains and glens north of the Highland line. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07LOACH1_SPAN/07LOACH1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beneath the Kilt, a Modern Scotland', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beneath the Kilt, a Modern Scotland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whiskey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loach, Ken', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T13:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d690908d-e6b8-5615-950d-ff0bbc1db24e', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d690908d-e6b8-5615-950d-ff0bbc1db24e'}, {'abstract': 'Austerity USA.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/depression-not-ended/', 'snippet': 'Austerity USA.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lousy jobs report. OK, you don’t want to put too much stress on one month’s numbers, yada yada, but it doesn’t look at all good.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Depression, Not Ended', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T13:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9415ef26-8585-5147-ad32-9545d4b50686', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9415ef26-8585-5147-ad32-9545d4b50686'}, {'abstract': 'The mandatory federal budget cuts don’t seem to account for sluggishness in the labor market — at least, not yet.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/sequestration-and-the-jobs-report/', 'snippet': 'The mandatory federal budget cuts don’t seem to account for sluggishness in the labor market — at least, not yet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The March jobs report came in much weaker than expected, with employers adding just 88,000 workers over the course of the month. Did sequestration – the $85 billion in mandatory budget cuts that Congress never managed to unwind, despite promises to the contrary – take a bite? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sequestration and the Jobs Report', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T13:30:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ac13e27-49ad-5c1d-b02f-4b6530f713a8', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ac13e27-49ad-5c1d-b02f-4b6530f713a8'}, {'abstract': 'The new Jackie Robinson biopic, “42,” emphasizes the legendary side of a complicated man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/movies/jackie-robinson-the-hero-in-42.html', 'snippet': 'The new Jackie Robinson biopic, “42,” emphasizes the legendary side of a complicated man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching the movie “42” brings to mind the ending to John Ford’s fine 1962 western, “The Man Who Shot Liberty Valance.” A newspaperman (and the viewer) has learned from James Stewart the truth about who really gunned down the evil Valance years before. But rather than run with the story, the newsman rips it up and flings it into the stove. His reason? “When the legend becomes fact,” he says, “print the legend.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 439, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 439}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1453, 'width': 1984, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07ROBINSON2_SPAN/07ROBINSON2_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Superhero Who Leapt Color Lines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Superhero Who Leapt Color Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Jackie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Helgeland, Brian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T13:46:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c036b868-f8dd-56ff-bb3f-5720a82bdb7e', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c036b868-f8dd-56ff-bb3f-5720a82bdb7e'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a major problem for Turkey’s Kurds that the notion of “Turkishness” is both ill-defined and staunchly defended.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/the-curse-of-ataturk/', 'snippet': 'It’s a major problem for Turkey’s Kurds that the notion of “Turkishness” is both ill-defined and staunchly defended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — “How happy is the one who says ‘I am a Turk,’” said Mustafa Kemal Ataturk, speaking in an emotional finale of a speech in 1933 — a time when Turkey was still trying to forge a national identity out of the ruins of the Ottoman Empire. The notion seemed simple enough: If you think you’re Turkish, then you are.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Curse of Ataturk', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ataturk, Kemal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gul, Abdullah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T13:47:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e8ed850-dbe0-550f-bdd6-190392e99077', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e8ed850-dbe0-550f-bdd6-190392e99077'}, {'abstract': 'Louis C. K. explains, in enthusiastic detail, how he recruited David Lynch to play a memorable character on his FX series, “Louie.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/louis-c-k-and-the-ballad-of-jack-dall/', 'snippet': 'Louis C. K. explains, in enthusiastic detail, how he recruited David Lynch to play a memorable character on his FX series, “Louie.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Louis C. K. gets excited about something, he really gets excited. Case in point: in a recent interview with the Arts & Leisure section of The Times, he expounded on many subjects, including his coming HBO special, “Oh My God,” his work with Woody Allen on “Blue Jasmine” and the number of years he believes he has left in his career. But when the topic of conversation turned to David Lynch, the “Blue Velvet” and “Mulholland Dr.” filmmaker, a certain gleam appeared in his eye. Over the next several minutes, Louis C. K. enthusiastically recounted the story of how he cast Mr. Lynch on “Louie” in the role of Jack Dall, an inscrutable entertainment-industry veteran who trains the Louie character for a fateful audition to replace David Letterman on “Late Show.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-articleLarge.png', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 257}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-jumbo.png', 'height': 261, 'width': 609, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-superJumbo.png', 'height': 261, 'width': 609, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/03/arts/louisck/louisck-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Louis C. K. and the Ballad of Jack Dall', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'C K, Louis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3540c8a8-6ac5-5b21-8845-3cfee7d7d3a0', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3540c8a8-6ac5-5b21-8845-3cfee7d7d3a0'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/what-were-reading-666/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chicago Sun-Times: Roger Ebert on not being able to eat: “From that time I began to replace what I had lost with what I remembered.” — Melissa Clark', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:10:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9372e05b-183c-55c0-988d-a9d90261c756', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9372e05b-183c-55c0-988d-a9d90261c756'}, {'abstract': 'Samuel Fuller’s “China Gate” depicts French incursions in Indochina; “The Sun Shines Bright,” from John Ford, examines racism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/movies/homevideo/new-dvds-china-gate-the-sun-shines-bright.html', 'snippet': 'Samuel Fuller’s “China Gate” depicts French incursions in Indochina; “The Sun Shines Bright,” from John Ford, examines racism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'China Gate', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/arts/07KEHR_SPAN/07KEHR_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Parsing Racial Politics in the ’50s', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parsing Racial Politics in the ’50s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kehr, Dave', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Samuel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'DVD', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Kehr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kehr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/824fc583-3e13-59be-8759-8a7588011553', 'word_count': 1674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/824fc583-3e13-59be-8759-8a7588011553'}, {'abstract': '“The Best of Punk Magazine” is the missing link in the flurry of books published over the past few years chronicling punk music and art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/the-best-of-punk-magazine-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“The Best of Punk Magazine” is the missing link in the flurry of books published over the past few years chronicling punk music and art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1972 I designed Hilly’s Gazette for Hilly Kristal’s eponymous bar before he opened the famed CBGB. I missed the punk scene by about two years because I was a hippie. Punks hated hippies. At least that’s what John Holmstrom told me years after hippiedom and punkdom had run their respective courses. Holmstrom was a co-founder — along with Ged Dunn Jr. and Eddie “Legs” McNeil — as well as an editor, designer, illustrator and letterer of Punk, the magazine that helped put the music on the map. When I was art director of The New York Times Op-Ed page, he showed me his illustration portfolio. The stiff linear crosshatch style was not the right tone for The Times, and I was not impressed by his comics-style hand-lettered magazine. I now realize what a cultural and historical bellwether it was.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 780}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 788, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1647, 'width': 1267, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Heller01/0407-Heller01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Punk’d', 'kicker': 'Visuals', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Punk’d', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heller, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:15:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0c980e3-1e9a-57e0-bd74-dec9d73ca6f0', 'word_count': 1946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0c980e3-1e9a-57e0-bd74-dec9d73ca6f0'}, {'abstract': 'The money is nice, but literary awards often come with ideological baggage and emotional strings attached.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/prize-writing.html', 'snippet': 'The money is nice, but literary awards often come with ideological baggage and emotional strings attached.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, the 133-year-old Library Journal announced the creation of a new annual prize: the Amanda Foreman Award for best acknowledgments. Laugh all you like; it was one of the proudest moments of my life. I knew how much effort had been expended in making those acknowledgments as comprehensive and accurate as possible; but I never thought anyone else would notice.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 930, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Foreman/0407-Foreman-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prize-Writing', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prize-Writing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:17:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Foreman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foreman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df357629-6407-5a64-84de-6dfc57e66a0d', 'word_count': 1327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df357629-6407-5a64-84de-6dfc57e66a0d'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, Italian-Americans balance their aspirations for the future and their nostalgia for Italy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/all-this-talk-of-love-by-christopher-castellani.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, Italian-Americans balance their aspirations for the future and their nostalgia for Italy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in Christopher Castellani’s new novel, “All This Talk of Love,” Frankie Grasso wonders whether he and his family are “at the end of something.” Frankie is a 30-ish graduate student in English literature, still a long way from the end of (for one thing) his dissertation. An answer begins to take shape when his sister, an affluent stay-at-home mom who has never strayed far from their childhood home in Delaware, organizes a family trip to Santa Cecilia, the village in Italy where their parents grew up. For all of the Grassos, the very idea of the trip stirs up thoughts of old losses, ghosts that shadow the present, prompted by the dissonant fact that like many if not most Italian-Americans, Antonio and Mad\xaddalena Grasso enshrined in their children a glowing image of things Italian yet never took them to Italy or even taught them the language. Feeling oneself “at the end of something,” this slyly ambitious novel suggests, may well be the existential condition of being Italian-American. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1376, 'width': 1959, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07RUSSO/07RUSSO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'La Famiglia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'La Famiglia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castellani, Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:18:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c8329a4-71bf-5bd3-916d-220f274dc663', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c8329a4-71bf-5bd3-916d-220f274dc663'}, {'abstract': 'Ira Katznelson examines how Franklin D. Roosevelt won approval for the New Deal, and at what cost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/fear-itself-by-ira-katznelson.html', 'snippet': 'Ira Katznelson examines how Franklin D. Roosevelt won approval for the New Deal, and at what cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ira Katznelson heard his first political argument around the family table a few weeks before the presidential election of 1952. His grandmother started it, as only a grandmother can, by announcing that she wasn’t going to vote. Katz\xadnelson’s parents, who adored Adlai Stevenson, were suitably shocked. But Bubbeh Frima held her ground. “Since Roosevelt,” she insisted, “they are all pygmies.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 735}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1673, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BOYLE/07BOYLE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The President Proposes . . .', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The President Proposes . . .', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katznelson, Ira', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "New Deal (1930's)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:18:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Boyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/743c1579-bf50-5e8d-b4b9-75551a32a65c', 'word_count': 1098, 'uri': 'nyt://article/743c1579-bf50-5e8d-b4b9-75551a32a65c'}, {'abstract': 'Owen King’s hero grapples with his first film and his complicated relationship with his father, a B-movie Lothario.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/double-feature-by-owen-king.html', 'snippet': 'Owen King’s hero grapples with his first film and his complicated relationship with his father, a B-movie Lothario.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Double Feature” is a lame title for a novel that is epic, ambitious and dedicated to the uncontainable. Nor is the jacket of service; it shows a couple of old movie tickets when it needs a scrum of characters, not getting anywhere but being their talkative and irrepressible selves. I’d guess that this novel — Owen King’s first, after an esteemed story collection — was compiled over a period of years, possibly as a series of short stories about a group of characters. The novel is maybe a third too long, chiefly because as he wanders around King can hardly see a place, a face or a chair without embarking on a wordy, if not literary, description of it. This might have been cut by someone (why not King?) who can see that brevity is his best quality. But he may be a tricky man to edit or organize. He should persevere, for when he is good — and that is often enough to make a page turner of this book — he has a captivating energy, a precision and a fondness for people that are rare and that make the reader doubly impatient for him to do what he does best.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1209, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Thomson/0407-Thomson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Stages', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Stages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Owen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:19:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Thomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eb3b719-31d4-5284-bc5e-8b6cfa0c5dc6', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eb3b719-31d4-5284-bc5e-8b6cfa0c5dc6'}, {'abstract': 'An abandoned daughter searches for her mother in Nancy Richler’s novel set in postwar Montreal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/the-imposter-bride-by-nancy-richler.html', 'snippet': 'An abandoned daughter searches for her mother in Nancy Richler’s novel set in postwar Montreal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A family story: My mother’s friend Frika, a Viennese Jew, managed to leave Austria for America after the Anschluss. She knew if she were caught smuggling out the means of subsistence, she would be arrested. So she wore her money on her hat, in the form of a diamond brooch, which — thus flaunted — passed for fake. When the Nazis at the border asked what she was taking with her, she replied, “Only what you see!” And they let her through.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1285}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 479, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1439, 'width': 672, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Kline/0407-Kline-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Book of Ruth', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Book of Ruth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richler, Nancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:19:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Kline', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kline', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0f04b9d-e225-5834-bfcd-6a66e74dab7e', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0f04b9d-e225-5834-bfcd-6a66e74dab7e'}, {'abstract': 'A crisis at Rutgers, trouble at Auburn, problems with the Pac-12 —and then there was N.C.A.A. President Mark Emmert’s news conference.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/leading-off-college-sports-really-bad-day/', 'snippet': 'A crisis at Rutgers, trouble at Auburn, problems with the Pac-12 —and then there was N.C.A.A. President Mark Emmert’s news conference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you are among those who love college sports in all their flawed glory, don’t want the system changed no matter how rotten it has become, Thursday was not a good day for you. In fact, one spin through the news cycle might have sent you crawling under the couch cushions. Of course, you probably found some college sports administrators hiding there, so it could get crowded pretty quickly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/sports/ncaabasketball/06leadingpic/06leadingpic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: College Sports’ Really Bad Day', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NCAA Basketball Championships (Men)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barchi, Robert L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chizik, Gene', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmert, Mark', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pernetti, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Mike Jr (1969- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Ray', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ware, Kevin', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Auburn University', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pacific-12 Conference', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Louisville', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:20:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44fea365-04e5-58eb-9fbd-0eea6cfc1910', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44fea365-04e5-58eb-9fbd-0eea6cfc1910'}, {'abstract': 'To fight World War II, Roosevelt needed support from legislators who wanted to keep out Europe’s Jews.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/fdr-and-the-jews-by-richard-breitman-and-allan-j-lichtman.html', 'snippet': 'To fight World War II, Roosevelt needed support from legislators who wanted to keep out Europe’s Jews.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Franklin Roosevelt enjoyed the overwhelming support of American Jews during his presidency, and the reasons are clear. In his three-plus terms from 1933 to 1945, he led the war against Hitler, supported a Jewish homeland in Palestine, appointed a Jew to the Supreme Court, chose another to be his secretary of the Treasury and surrounded himself with Jewish advisers who helped shape the laws that revolutionized the role of government in American life — what some critics sneeringly called the “Jew Deal.” Then, of course, there was Eleanor Roosevelt, whose concern for minorities fused the bond even tighter. When Roose\xadvelt died in 1945, the Rabbinical Assembly of America described him, almost supernaturally, as an “immortal leader of humanity and a peerless servant of God.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1198, 'width': 1005, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07OSHINSKY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '. . . Congress Disposes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '. . . Congress Disposes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breitman, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lichtman, Allan J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Oshinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oshinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/459864ab-1964-5479-94ac-d510bfffa377', 'word_count': 1687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/459864ab-1964-5479-94ac-d510bfffa377'}, {'abstract': 'Part of what made the discovery of gorillas so sensational in the Victorian era was that they were “too similar to humans for comfort.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Part of what made the discovery of gorillas so sensational in the Victorian era was that they were “too similar to humans for comfort.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his review of Monte Reel’s “Between Man and Beast” this week, David Quammen notes that part of what made the discovery of gorillas so sensational in the Victorian era was that they were “too similar to humans for comfort.” As for whether attitudes have changed much, Quammen — the author of a biography of Charles Darwin, among other books — isn’t so sure; in an e-mail, he cited a recent Gallup poll that found 46 percent of Americans “decline to accept the reality that humans have evolved from ancestral primates.” His most recent book, “Spillover,” is about human diseases that come to us from animals, and he arrives at the terrifying conclusion that such outbreaks are likely to become more frequent and more worrisome. “The next big one might well prove devastating,” he said. Still, if there is one salubrious thing “about these insalubrious afflictions, such as Ebola and West Nile and AIDS and the influenzas,” it’s that “they remind us, by spilling over from nonhuman animals to humans, that we humans are just another animal.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 930, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 930}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1322, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Upfront/0407-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a09d318b-d81d-5d28-b9f7-689c8802e50f', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a09d318b-d81d-5d28-b9f7-689c8802e50f'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography of Hugo Chávez can serve as a political obituary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/comandante-hugo-chavezs-venezuela-by-rory-carroll.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography of Hugo Chávez can serve as a political obituary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On March 5, Hugo Chávez’s extraordinary good luck ran out at last. After decades of evading an endless string of opponents — from personal poverty to the United States government — Venezuela’s democratically elected strongman finally succumbed to the one enemy he couldn’t defeat. Even his ending was vintage Chávez. The perpetual showman spent the last months of his life ruling Venezuela Oz-like, by remote control from his sickbed (he even missed his own inauguration on Jan. 10). All of which makes this the perfect time for the release of Rory Carroll’s “Comandante: Hugo Chávez’s Venezuela,” a sort of political obituary for the self-declared savior of Venezuela — a charismatic champion of the poor who, during his 14 years in office, ground one of Latin America’s richest states into the dust.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1273, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07TEPPERMAN/07TEPPERMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Death of a Strongman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death of a Strongman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tepperman, Jonathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venezuela', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:22:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Tepperman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tepperman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f156d29c-a479-5994-996b-d12252abd60c', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f156d29c-a479-5994-996b-d12252abd60c'}, {'abstract': 'Marie Arana’s biography assesses the man who led Latin America’s independence movement against Spanish rule.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/bolivar-by-marie-arana.html', 'snippet': 'Marie Arana’s biography assesses the man who led Latin America’s independence movement against Spanish rule.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simón Bolívar, the Liberator, was the George Washington of South America, or so he was regarded by sundry eminences in the United States during a large part of his extraordinary career. Grandeur was his nature. He aroused adulation. He dealt a few preliminary military defeats to the Spanish imperial army, and in 1813, he entered victoriously, for the moment, into Caracas on a chariot drawn by white-gowned daughters of the leading families, as if he were a god or a Caesar. And he assumed the magnificent title of “Liberator and Dictator.” The chariot and the daughters deviated, it is true, from the austere Washington style, and the dictatorial honorific has hovered a little ominously over his reputation ever since, as you can see by reading Marie Arana’s admiring and rueful biography, “Bolívar.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 626}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 983, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1965, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07BERMAN/07BERMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Founding Father', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Founding Father', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arana, Marie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:22:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c16463f-8ddf-5529-91ee-0f0c807cbac5', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c16463f-8ddf-5529-91ee-0f0c807cbac5'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIDDLE C,  by William H. Gass. (Knopf, $28.95.) From its opening notes, this unquiet bildungsroman about a family of Austrian emigrants — fakes and fraudsters who have cast themselves as Jews — is designed to detonate its middling title.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editor's Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:23:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9e6d64a-b292-5dc6-b5a1-96d49497656b', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9e6d64a-b292-5dc6-b5a1-96d49497656b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE IDEA FACTORY: Bell Labs and the Great Age of American Innovation,  by Jon Gertner. (Penguin, $17.) Bell Labs, the onetime research and development wing of AT&T, was behind many of the technological transformations that define modern life, including the transistor, lasers and cellular telephony. Gertner’s incisive history is filled with inspiring lessons and asks a critical question: What causes innovation?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/69d09696-8567-5d17-8a1a-3945b45d7378', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69d09696-8567-5d17-8a1a-3945b45d7378'}, {'abstract': 'Point-and-click adventure games for mobile devices range from the simple and light-hearted to surreal and complex.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/app-smart-extra-more-point-and-click-adventure-games-to-try/', 'snippet': 'Point-and-click adventure games for mobile devices range from the simple and light-hearted to surreal and complex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My App Smart column this week was about point-and-click adventure games. These apps build on a long tradition of computer-based adventures. Great graphics and touch screens on today’s tablets and smartphones make these games much more fun to play than before. The idea is that you interact with items in the game’s various scenes by tapping on the graphics, solving puzzles as the game’s story progresses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'App Smart Extra: More Point-and-Click Adventure Games to Try', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Evernote Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:24:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kit Eaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bf5b946-bd65-5ac1-9220-47ec82ee22f0', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bf5b946-bd65-5ac1-9220-47ec82ee22f0'}, {'abstract': 'The March numbers fuel the narrative that the unemployment rate is coming down not because the economy is getting better, but because people are giving up looking for jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/the-shrinking-ranks-of-the-working/', 'snippet': 'The March numbers fuel the narrative that the unemployment rate is coming down not because the economy is getting better, but because people are giving up looking for jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just when you thought it might be safe for the Fed to begin to think about taking its foot off the accelerator, along comes a jobs report that makes the employment picture look much less rosy than we had thought.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Shrinking Ranks of the Working', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:25:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/637caf16-9471-5091-8b8c-0e8fe94720d3', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/637caf16-9471-5091-8b8c-0e8fe94720d3'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Girls of Atomic City” — at No. 14 on the hardcover nonfiction list — Denise Kiernan explains the prominent role young women played in the Manhattan Project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Girls of Atomic City” — at No. 14 on the hardcover nonfiction list — Denise Kiernan explains the prominent role young women played in the Manhattan Project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NUCLEAR FAMILY: The town of Oak Ridge, Tenn., didn’t exist before World War II, but by mid-1945 it had 75,000 residents and used more electricity than New York City. Why? Because, as Denise Kiernan explains in “The Girls of Atomic City” — new at No. 14 on the hardcover nonfiction list — that’s where the government set up the Manhattan Project’s top secret uranium enriching facilities, staffed almost entirely by young women who didn’t learn what they were working on until the first bomb fell on Hiroshima.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07list-img/07list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 474, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07list-img/07list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 474, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07list-img/07list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07list-img/07list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07list-img/07list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:26:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02c05e56-5cdb-5a62-8985-5d31d3d9c4f4', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02c05e56-5cdb-5a62-8985-5d31d3d9c4f4'}, {'abstract': 'Tamas Dobozy’s stories unfold between war-torn Hungary and the émigré circles of present-day North America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/siege-13-by-tamas-dobozy.html', 'snippet': 'Tamas Dobozy’s stories unfold between war-torn Hungary and the émigré circles of present-day North America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are you experiencing any of the following: (1) A thirst for \xadpalinka? (2) A yearning for Budapest, though the closest you’ve ever come is the Hungarian Pastry Shop in Manhattan? (3) An urge to deploy diacritical marks over every other vowel? If so, you may be suffering from Hungarophilia. My own case dates to my mid-20s, when I discovered Peter Nadas’s novel “A Book of Memories.” Its high-Modernist aspirations were just one of the ways this contemporary masterpiece seemed to issue from a more distinguished past. Fascinated, I began reading every living Hungarian novelist I could find in English: Imre Kertesz, Laszlo Krasznahorkai. . . . By the time I got to Peter Esterhazy’s “Celestial Harmonies,” I, too, was dreaming of “palaces with swirling towers and flapping banners gazing at their reflections in the mirror of the lake.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1349, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/0407-Hallberg/0407-Hallberg-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ghosts of Budapest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ghosts of Budapest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hallberg, Garth Risk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dobozy, Tamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:27:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garth Risk Hallberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garth', 'middlename': 'Risk', 'lastname': 'Hallberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e4622dc-d978-5b4c-b4a0-0b7b41e9db2c', 'word_count': 1379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e4622dc-d978-5b4c-b4a0-0b7b41e9db2c'}, {'abstract': 'This book presents the life of Muhammad for Western readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/the-first-muslim-by-lesley-hazleton.html', 'snippet': 'This book presents the life of Muhammad for Western readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In today’s febrile cultural and religious climate, what project could be more fraught than writing a biography of Muhammad? The worldwide protests at “The Innocence of Muslims,” 14 minutes of trashy provocation posted on YouTube, are a terrible reminder to the would-be biographer that the life story of the prophet of Islam is not material about which one is free to have a “take.” Lesley Hazleton’s “First Muslim” is a book written by a white woman of dual American and British citizenship, published in America more than a decade after the 9/11 attacks. For many believers it is already — even before it is read, if it is read at all — an object of suspicion, something to be defended against, in case it should turn out to be yet another insult, another cruel parody of a story such an author has no business telling.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 626}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1963, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/books/review/07KUNZRU/07KUNZRU-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Messenger and the Message', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Messenger and the Message', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hazleton, Lesley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T14:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kunzru', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kunzru', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea5f383e-b5ec-5be7-bf0b-84c51c48cc6b', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea5f383e-b5ec-5be7-bf0b-84c51c48cc6b'}, {'abstract': 'Mike Piazza recalls his career: his achievements, his feuds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/long-shot-mike-piazzas-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'Mike Piazza recalls his career: his achievements, his feuds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a lifelong Mets fan (you can tell by a photo of me at age 10 dressed as Keith Hernandez for Halloween, and my current aura of despair), I’d hoped Mike Piazza — one of few genuine heroes on the team post-’80s — would be voted into the Baseball Hall of Fame earlier this year, his first time on the ballot. Alas, he fell 98 votes short of the 75 percent required for induction, most likely passed over because of a suspicion that he, like some other power hitters of his generation, took steroids — a rumor addressed at length in his strategically timed memoir, “Long Shot.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim