b': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T15:48:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f930b509-96fb-5970-8894-d275c14a1952', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f930b509-96fb-5970-8894-d275c14a1952'}, {'abstract': 'A quandary at the Cambodian border.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/should-you-tip-an-immigration-officer.html', 'snippet': 'A quandary at the Cambodian border.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My family and I traveled through a number of countries in Southeast Asia. Upon leaving Cambodia, the immigration officer asked me in a pleasant but direct voice, “Do you have a tip for me?” I pretended not to understand the nature of his question. He then repeated his request. With some apprehension, I gave him $5. Should I have given the immigration officer the “tip” and, by extension, help to perpetuate the nation’s insidious corruption? Should I have reported the incident? (Given the pernicious nature of Cambodian “justice,” I have genuine concerns that the officer’s punishment would not fit the pettiness of the offense.) LEON WEAVER, REDDING, CONN.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capt