rticle/c716285d-c9a8-5db9-8f88-b578d6ece407', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c716285d-c9a8-5db9-8f88-b578d6ece407'}, {'abstract': 'How much does it cost to have a beautiful yard?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/making-your-own-backyard-paradise.html', 'snippet': 'How much does it cost to have a beautiful yard?', 'lead_paragraph': 'How much does it cost to have a beautiful yard? We asked Mark Word of Mark Word Design, a landscape and construction company in Austin, Tex., to look at photographs of a home in Dallas (above) and give us outdoor-makeover options from least to most expensive. “Before I started the design, I made a choice to make this more of an outdoor room,” he said. “I imagined this was for a family with kids and that they’d be using it all the time.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0/mag-07KeyBackyard-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Your Own Backyard Paradise', 'kicker': 'Key', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yes, Please, In My Backyard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lawns', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Word, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dallas (Tex)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T15:48:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c8071e2-082d-5948-b4a1-f5f8a3fe68d6', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c8071e2-082d-5948-b4a1-f5f8a3fe68d6'}, {'abstract': 'The founder of Zynga on games, management and his relationship with Mark Zuckerberg.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/mark-pincus-thinks-angry-birds-wont-hurt-your-kids.html', 'snippet': 'The founder of Zynga on games, management and his relationship with Mark Zuckerberg.', 'lead_paragraph': ' You are the founder and C.E.O. of Zynga, a company responsible for addictive games like FarmVille and the forthcoming Draw Something 2. You know, every time I see my 4-year-old son playing Angry Birds on my phone, I imagine that his little brain is rotting.  I can commiserate because I have twin 2 ½- year-olds, and they’ve mastered the iPod Touch. I don’t think it’s rotting their brains, if you compare what it’s replacing — TV. My kids are mastering puzzles, it’s challenging their thinking. I want to ultimately reward them with screen-time minutes for chores and achievements.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07talk/07talk-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 383, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07talk/07talk-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1380, 'width': 516, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07talk/07talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07talk/07talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07talk/07talk-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark Pincus Thinks Angry Birds Won’t Hurt Your Kids', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "‘I Don't Think it's Rotting Their Brains’", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldman, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zynga Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pincus, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Angry Birds (Video Game)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'FarmVille (Video Game)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Draw Something 2 (Video Game)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T15:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/97496d20-71e4-5bb5-a7f2-4d49f5260df6', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97496d20-71e4-5bb5-a7f2-4d49f5260df6'}, {'abstract': 'More liabilities, but more assets too.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/hedge-fund-america/', 'snippet': 'More liabilities, but more assets too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Still thinking about debt; and it seems to me that there’s an important aspect of the story that isn’t getting any play, namely, the extent to which US debt has in effect been issued to buy assets overseas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hedge Fund America', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T15:49:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/906a9104-c31d-5e2d-83d8-7b44190af846', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/906a9104-c31d-5e2d-83d8-7b44190af846'}, {'abstract': 'Not everybody can get you a giant weeping Norway Spruce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/magazine/how-and-where-to-buy-extraordinary-trees.html', 'snippet': 'Not everybody can get you a giant weeping Norway Spruce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ordinary trees offer shade, privacy, visual interest. Extraordinary trees — rare, oddly shaped or just plain huge — provide something else altogether. They can serve as sculptural centerpieces to a yard or perfectly complement architectural details. Most tree nurseries keep some odd ducks in their inventory, but a few, including the three listed below, specialize in unusual specimens they either grow themselves or purchase during regular scouting missions of public and private land. As a general rule, the more unusual the tree, the higher the price. But as Todd Forrest of the New York Botanical Garden says: “Planting a tree transforms the landscape. And planting a cool tree adds value to your home.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07keytree-slide-WJHA/07keytree-slide-WJHA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07keytree-slide-WJHA/07keytree-slide-WJHA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/magazine/07keytree-slide-WJHA/07keytree-slide-WJHA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'captio