the capital of the Confederacy, but by early 1863 it was a miserable place to be. With the Union controlling much of Virginia’s Tidewater and coastal North Carolina, inland cities like Richmond were starving. Fields in central Virginia and the Shenandoah Valley went unattended as soldiers were kept at their posts. The rail system and the James River canal were overtaxed trying to provision and equip the men on the front. What food did make it through was sold at sky-high prices by speculators and merchants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Richmond Bread Riot', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T16:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71e2c687-b2b6-56a5-ae4c-4600d40bfba5', 'word_count': 1778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71e2c687-b2b6-56a5-ae4c-4600d40bfba5'}, {'abstract': 'A response to the more determinist arguments for why marriage has declined.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/marriage-and-historical-inevitability/', 'snippet': 'A response to the more determinist arguments for why marriage has declined.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And now to finish up my triptych of replies to Kevin Drum on marriage. Here’s his conclusion:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Marriage and Historical Inevitability', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T16:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b90af4fb-b253-524b-90eb-21ae6ced33ee', 'word_count': 998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b90af4fb-b253-524b-90eb-21ae6ced33ee'}, {'abstract': 'The earnings yield on stocks is now higher than the investment yield on junk bonds. Either stocks are cheap or junk bonds are expensive. Or both.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/based-on-relative-yields-stocks-look-cheap/', 'snippet': 'The earnings yield on stocks is now higher than the investment yield on junk bonds. Either stocks are cheap or junk bonds are expensive. Or both.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Off the Charts column this week looks at the popularity of junk bonds. Since 1999, they have outperformed stocks during both bull and bear markets. Issuance of such bonds has risen to record levels around the world, and yields are at historic lows. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Based on Relative Yields, Stocks Look Cheap', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Junk Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T16:03:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris