d in part by the author’s experience in Panama, where he still lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'R. M. Koster’s novel “The Prince” is one of those cult classics that is hard to classify, and so is its author. He is an American writing in English, but he writes from and about Latin America, his home for more than 50 years, in the fantastical style associated with Gabriel García Márquez and other Spanish-language writers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/SUB-06KOSTER/SUB-06KOSTER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American in Panama Reflects on His Cult Classic, Now Reissued', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An American In Panama, 41 Years Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Panama', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T17:12:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd395b45-89ad-529b-9ad3-627d428918b1', 'word_count': 1294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd395b45-89ad-529b-9ad3-627d428918b1'}, {'abstract': 'Former Gov. Mark Sanford secured the Republican nomination and will face Elizabeth Colbert Busch in the race for South Carolina’s First Congressional District, which is solidly Republican. But this election is not typical.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/why-sanford-vs-colbert-busch-could-be-competitive/', 'snippet': 'Former Gov. Mark Sanford secured the Republican nomination and will face Elizabeth Colbert Busch in the race for South Carolina’s First Congressional District, which is solidly Republican. But this election is not typical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former Gov. Mark Sanford won a runoff election this week, securing the Republican nomination for South Carolina’s First Congressional District. He will face the Democratic nominee, Elizabeth Colbert Busch, in a May 7 general election. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Sanford vs. Colbert Busch Could Be Competitive', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None,