e754c', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2419d948-6f34-5661-a9c6-8b6a156e754c'}, {'abstract': 'In “Mr. TOL E. RAncE” the choreographer Camille A. Brown explores African-American humor and the persistence of black stereotypes in popular culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/06/arts/dance/camille-a-browns-mr-tol-e-rance-at-the-kitchen.html', 'snippet': 'In “Mr. TOL E. RAncE” the choreographer Camille A. Brown explores African-American humor and the persistence of black stereotypes in popular culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The choreographer Camille A. Brown begins “Mr. TOL E. RAncE,” a look at race, stereotypes and the way humor plays into them, with a video montage of numbers from minstrel shows. During the screening her dancers, wearing suspenders, gray suits and caps, quietly assemble onto a darkened stage as if in mourning. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1256, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/06/arts/06CAMILLE/06CAMILLE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Examining a Humor Born of Many Struggles', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Examining a Humor Born of Many Struggles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Camille A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T19:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0116e34d-617f-5ce3-bebc-50ddcc0a7721', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0116e34d-617f-5ce3-bebc-50ddcc0a7721'}, {'abstract': 'Daphne Javitch, designer of the lingerie line TEN, is inspired by romantic movies and books, especially this title by James Salter.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/off-the-shelf-light-years/', 'snippet': 'Daphne Javitch, designer of the lingerie line TEN, is inspired by romantic movies and books, especially this title by James Salter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Shelf | ‘Light Years’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T19:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yuri Chong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yuri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/642d8cf2-c916-5eda-bbec-89ffe5d3bf7d', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/642d8cf2-c916-5eda-bbec-89ffe5d3bf7d'}, {'abstract': 'A furniture collection designed for Knoll by OMA that blurs the lines between working and living space makes its debut.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/paradigm-shift-tools-for-life/', 'snippet': 'A furniture collection designed for Knoll by OMA that blurs the lines between working and living space makes its debut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After its sneak preview in January at the fall 2013 Prada men’s fashion shows, “Tools for Life,” the furniture collection designed for Knoll by OMA, the architecture office that was co-founded by Rem Koolhaas, will make its official debut next week during the Salone Internazionale del Mobile in Milan. The collection, which will be on view April 9 and 10 at Prada’s exhibition space at Via Fogazzaro 36, consists of pieces that address the increasingly blurred boundaries between working and living space by being kinetic rather than static. As Koolhaas said, his goal was to create furniture “that performs in very precise but also in completely unpredictable ways” and indeed, flexibility is the key here. A massive, heavily engineered piece called the 04 Counter consists of three horizontal bars that stack like a wall, but which can also pivot and cantilever, transforming the piece from room divider to “piazza,” as Benjamin Pardo, Knoll’s design director, called it, adding, “Once people discover that the object moves, they want to engage with it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paradigm Shift | Tools for Life', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koolhaas, Rem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T19:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e54d53b6-aa69-547d-bd41-930b8c4d7b22', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e54d53b6-aa69-547d-bd41-930b8c4d7b22'}, {'abstract': 'It’s easy to say Bear Bryant’s tactics in 1954 would be universally unaccepted today. Then we see how the Rutgers coach firing played out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/06/sports/what-has-changed-since-the-junction-boys.html', 'snippet': 'It’s easy to say Bear Bryant’s tactics in 1954 would be universally unaccepted today. Then we see how the Rutgers coach firing played out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The death of Jack Pardee on Monday and the firing of the Rutgers men’s basketball coach Mike Rice on Wednesday bring us to a compelling intersection of coaching norms and excessive behavior. They also remind us that the good old days weren’t all that good.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'captio