ld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f44badc-ca6c-514d-91a8-692b8e2b4aa6', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f44badc-ca6c-514d-91a8-692b8e2b4aa6'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Novogratz, a principal at the Fortress Investment Group and former Princeton wrestler, is trying to get wresting restored to the Olympics after the International Olympic Committee voted to eliminate the sport from the Games starting in 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/wrestlings-private-equity-champion/', 'snippet': 'Michael Novogratz, a principal at the Fortress Investment Group and former Princeton wrestler, is trying to get wresting restored to the Olympics after the International Olympic Committee voted to eliminate the sport from the Games starting in 202...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Novogratz, a principal at the Fortress Investment Group and former Princeton wrestler, is trying to score an emergency takedown. Since the International Olympic Committee voted to eliminate wrestling starting in 2020, Mr. Novogratz has been working the phones from a war room inside Fortress’s 46th-floor headquarters in Midtown Manhattan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wrestling’s Private Equity Champion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wrestling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortress Investment Group L.L.C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T20:46:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynnley Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynnley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3af67af-4006-5dcd-a5d3-68806ce4a47b', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3af67af-4006-5dcd-a5d3-68806ce4a47b'}, {'abstract': 'Osamu Namba, Subaru’s design head, made a surprise appearance at the New York International Auto Show to talk about the WRX concept and Subaru’s new design language.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/the-new-wrx-concept-shows-off-subarus-new-design-language/', 'snippet': 'Osamu Namba, Subaru’s design head, made a surprise appearance at the New York International Auto Show to talk about the WRX concept and Subaru’s new design language.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Osamu Namba, the\xa0Subaru design chief, made a surprise appearance at the New York International Auto Show last week for the unveiling of the new WRX concept. In an interview at the company’s stand, he said the car presented the company’s new design language, parts of which were seen in the Viziv concept shown at the Geneva auto show in March. As the most extreme of Subaru designs, the WRX offers the company a chance to render the new vocabulary in striking form.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New WRX Concept Shows Off Subaru’s New Design Language', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York International Auto Show', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Subaru', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T20:46:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d122b861-7874-55c5-bb30-71dc3cd467d5', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d122b861-7874-55c5-bb30-71dc3cd467d5'}, {'abstract': 'In the six years since her marriage to one of the yoga world’s luminaries, Colleen Saidman Yee has become a star in her own right.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/fashion/colleen-saidman-yee-the-first-lady-of-yoga.html', 'snippet': 'In the six years since her marriage to one of the yoga world’s luminaries, Colleen Saidman Yee has become a star in her own right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vaguely indicating the lower torso, the word “core” has been rippling (as it were) through American fitness programs for about a decade. But the yogini Colleen Saidman Yee likes to be more specific.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/fashion/07YEE_SPAN/07YEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/07/fashion/07YEE_SPAN/07YEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/fashion/07YEE_SPAN/07YEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/07/fashion/07YEE_SPAN/07YEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1300, 'wi