rotection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guarantees and Warranties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Used Cars', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-05T20:54:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b09d94c4-5405-57b9-81b8-e1e51867e6f0', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b09d94c4-5405-57b9-81b8-e1e51867e6f0'}, {'abstract': 'The office of the chief medical examiner is sifting through 590 cubic yards of material excavated from around the World Trade Center in search of fragments that may be human remains.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/05/the-search-for-911-remains-goes-on/', 'snippet': 'The office of the chief medical examiner is sifting through 590 cubic yards of material excavated from around the World Trade Center in search of fragments that may be human remains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Update, May 17: The office of the chief medical examiner is sifting through 590 cubic yards of material excavated from around the World Trade Center in search of fragments that may be human remains. On Thursday, two such fragments were recovered from the Vehicle Security Center area. That brought the total to 78 since work began on April 1; 44 from the C-Grid area outside the World Financial Center, 34 from the Vehicle Security Center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/04/blo