': 'Kohlberg Kravis Roberts & Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group LP', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-06T17:56:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pradnya Joshi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pradnya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joshi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cbb7d60-8a8d-5fc1-b3c3-597e5d006588', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cbb7d60-8a8d-5fc1-b3c3-597e5d006588'}, {'abstract': 'Why blacks who had fled to Canada returned to fight for the Union.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/06/african-north-americans-and-the-war/', 'snippet': 'Why blacks who had fled to Canada returned to fight for the Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Peter Riggs was not like most soldiers. A private in the 54th Massachusetts regiment, his race would have stood out first to contemporary observers: Riggs was black, and his unit was the first to be organized in the North, after President Lincoln and the Union Army acquiesced to expanding the power of the Emancipation Proclamation with African-Americans fighting as soldiers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'African North Americans and the War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Underground Railroad (19th Century)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-06T18:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Arenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6f10c7e-b929-5ae2-8f83-40e17dc54912', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6f10c7e-b929-5ae2-8f83-40e17dc54912'}, {'abstract': 'As writers, we want the other version of our life, the one that thrills us.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/06/how-memoirists-mold-the-truth/', 'snippet': 'As writers, we want the other version of our life, the one that thrills us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wake of horrible family rows, short of leaving my father, my mother would do something that shook the foundations of my childhood. She would step into our living room and, spurred by rage, spite and last-ditch efforts to restore order in her life, she would single-handedly move all the furniture around. The sofa was relegated to the place held by an imposing bookcase. The bookcase would be shunted over to a corner that belonged to an unassuming cabinet, now demoted to another corner where it sat in the dark under the terrified gaze of two leather armchairs squatting in the maelstrom.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Memoirists Mold the Truth', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Memoirists Mold the Truth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-06T18:02:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byli