tion_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Ramsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/747918fd-615a-5c20-8992-83ac53896eb2', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/747918fd-615a-5c20-8992-83ac53896eb2'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years after he became Major League Baseball’s first designated hitter, the Yankees’ Ron Blomberg reflects on the position and what it has meant for the game.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/06/the-first-d-h-reflects-on-how-the-position-changed-the-game/', 'snippet': 'Forty years after he became Major League Baseball’s first designated hitter, the Yankees’ Ron Blomberg reflects on the position and what it has meant for the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On April 6, 1973, at Fenway Park, Ron Blomberg became the first player in major league history to start in a game then take a position on the bench instead of on the field. Blomberg, a .293 hitter during a career spent mostly with the Yankees, was the first designated hitter. Blomberg was drafted first over all in 1967, by the Yankees, after being a standout in baseball, basketball and track in high school in Georgia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The First D.H. Reflects on How the Position Changed the Game', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blomberg, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-06T22:54:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Brescia', 'person':