 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New-York Historical Society', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T15:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b824a89-90ba-55e3-b3d4-aa8d1e7fcf54', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b824a89-90ba-55e3-b3d4-aa8d1e7fcf54'}, {'abstract': 'What a start-up is worth depends on why the prospective buyer wants it, but the prices still sometimes seem random or even nonsensical.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/how-deal-makers-put-a-value-on-start-ups-disruptions/', 'snippet': 'What a start-up is worth depends on why the prospective buyer wants it, but the prices still sometimes seem random or even nonsensical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a vision of how suitors decide how much to offer for a start-up they want to buy. Several executives go into a conference room. Each scribbles a number on a piece of paper and places it in a hat. Then the chief executive pulls out a number, and there it is.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/technology/08bits-disrupt/08bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/08/technology/08bits-disrupt/08bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/technology/08bits-disrupt/08bits-disrupt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 1