for a Dream” and other movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the movie scores of Clint Mansell, Minimalism can hold booby traps. His music is suffused with melancholy foreboding and shadowy spaces, counting down to what might be an elegy or a cataclysm.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1243, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/09/arts/music/09mansell/09mansell-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Film Music, With Just a Hint of Films', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film Music, With a Taste Of the Films', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mansell, Clint', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T21:59:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f8f27dd-8188-55b9-b932-65504a75bfe5', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f8f27dd-8188-55b9-b932-65504a75bfe5'}, {'abstract': 'The return of Janet R. Bender.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/sweetest-part-of-a-cake/', 'snippet': 'The return of Janet R. Bender.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — The last time we saw Janet Bender, she was trying to include us in some email by CCing us (Across Lite, Solution, blog), and today she is back to check on our OPERATING SYSTEMs. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sweetest Part of a Cake', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d21c8fd-968e-500c-a03b-0f038272ffd1', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d21c8fd-968e-500c-a03b-0f038272ffd1'}, {'abstract': 'The publisher Other Criteria will issue a comprehensive catalog of the paintings this spring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/arts/design/a-catalog-will-detail-hirsts-spot-paintings.html', 'snippet': 'The publisher Other Criteria will issue a comprehensive catalog of the paintings this spring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Damien Hirst’s whimsical work evokes amusement, fascination and annoyance among art lovers, seemingly in equal measure. His spot paintings, which he began in 1986, have particularly been lightning rods. These panels of bright-hued spots are usually arranged in columns on canvases of different shapes, and his fans have paid as much as $3.4 million for them. Last year the Gagosian Gallery mounted an exhibition, “The Complete Spot Paintings 1986-2011,” at 11 galleries in eight cities. But the show’s expansive title notwithstanding, it presented only 331 works — a fraction of the spot-painting output of Mr. Hirst. The full scope of Mr. Hirst’s fascination with colored dots will become clear this spring, when his publisher, Other Criteria, issues a comprehensive catalog of the paintings: a 1,000-page illustrated book listing every work. Jason Beard, the director of Other Criteria, told The Art Newspaper that there are about 1,400 of the works. Mr. Hirst painted the first 25 himself; the others have been mainly entrusted to his assistants. Other Criteria’s catalog already offers for sale a number of items derived from the paintings, including magnets, a T-shirt, cuff links and a spot garbage bin.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Catalog Will Detail Hirst’s Spot Paintings', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Catalog Will Detail Hirst’s Spot Paintings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Damien', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:06:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/74e1f78d-7a5f-5d8f-831f-879f8894b701', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74e1f78d-7a5f-5d8f-831f-879f8894b701'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Whitaker will write a biography of Bill Cosby, to be published by Simon & Schuster in the fall of 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/books/a-bill-cosby-biography.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Whitaker will write a biography of Bill Cosby, to be published by Simon & Schuster in the fall of 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Whitaker, the former editor of Newsweek and the former executive vice president and managing editor of CNN Worldwide, will write a biography of Bill Cosby, to be published by Simon & Schuster in the fall of 2014. The book, which was announced in Publisher’s Marketplace on Friday, will be Mr. Whitaker’s second for the house. His “My Long Trip Home: A Family Memoir” was published in 2011 and explored the experiences of his parents, an interracial couple, and how they shaped his life. Mr. Cosby has published several books of his own, including “Fatherhood” (1986), “Childhood” (1991) and most recently, “I Didn’t Ask to Be Born (but I’m Glad I Was)” (2011).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bill Cosby Biography', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bill Cosby Biography', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cosby, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitaker, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:06:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/403877bc-8f1a-59df-a339-419169e6c670', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403877bc-8f1a-59df-a339-419169e6c670'}, {'abstract': 'Beyoncé and Jay-Z spent part of last week in Havana, celebrating their fifth wedding anniversary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/arts/music/questions-over-cuba-trip-of-beyonce-and-jay-z.html', 'snippet': 'Beyoncé and Jay-Z spent part of last week in Havana, celebrating their fifth wedding anniversary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beyoncé</strong> and Jay-Z spent part of last week in Havana, celebrating their fifth wedding anniversary by touring historical landmarks and speaking with what Agence France-Presse described as hundreds of Cuban well-wishers. The Havana Times reported that the couple also visited a dance troupe, Danza Contemporánea de Cuba, the singer Haila Mompié and the children’s theater group La Colmenita, and met with students and teachers at the Superior Art Institute. They may soon be explaining the trip to members of Congress, or facing a prison sentence and a fine. Treasury Department regulations prohibit Americans from traveling to Cuba “unless authorized by a general or specific license.” Now two Republican members of the House of Representatives from Florida — which has a large population of Cuban refugees and where feelings about Cuba are acute — have raised questions about the visit. The representatives, Ileana Ros-Lehtinen and Mario Diaz-Balart, have written to Adam J. Szubin, the director of the Office of Foreign Assets Control at the Treasury Department, asking about the nature of the license the couple received for their visit. “Cuba’s tourism industry is wholly state-controlled,” Ms. Ros-Lehtinen and Mr. Diaz-Balart wrote, “therefore, U.S. dollars spent on Cuban tourism directly fund the machinery of oppression that brutally represses the Cuban people.” Penalties for violating the travel ban can include a 10-year prison sentence and a $250,000 fine. Although thousands of Americans find their way to Cuba every year, usually by way of another country, few are prosecuted.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions Over Cuba Trip of Beyoncé and Jay-Z', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Questions Over Cuba Trip Of Beyoncé and Jay-Z', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay-Z', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Beyonce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:07:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbdde696-e104-5891-bf8d-49d14e4e1370', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbdde696-e104-5891-bf8d-49d14e4e1370'}, {'abstract': 'Daybees lets people fine-tune searches for things to do, using keywords, location or time and date.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/technology/08iht-search08.html', 'snippet': 'Daybees lets people fine-tune searches for things to do, using keywords, location or time and date.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Staying around town over the weekend and your dinner plans have fallen through? Turning to the printed page might not turn up many attractive alternatives, unless you live in a big city like New York, London or Paris and have access to magazines like Time Out or Pariscope. A Google search could present the opposite problem: too many events in too many places at too many times.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeking to Outdo Google in Searching for Events', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeking to Outdo Google\r\nIn Searching for Events', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pfanner, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Daybees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:10:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2f26c4c-8b3a-5ac3-bccd-1d8c6fe70b50', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2f26c4c-8b3a-5ac3-bccd-1d8c6fe70b50'}, {'abstract': 'Reports include retail sales for March; and Alcoa, JPMorgan Chase and Wells Fargo are among the companies set to release quarterly earnings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/business/economic-reports-for-the-week-of-april-8.html', 'snippet': 'Reports include retail sales for March; and Alcoa, JPMorgan Chase and Wells Fargo are among the companies set to release quarterly earnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ECONOMIC REPORTS Data to be released include wholesale trade for February (Tuesday); weekly jobless claims and import prices for March (Thursday); and retail sales for March, the Producer Price Index for March, the Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan consumer sentiment index for April and business inventories for February (Friday).', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Reports for the Week Ahead', 'kicker': 'Looking Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Economic Reports for the Week Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:15:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cfe8c20-c864-57b6-a2e1-6c1a2fc2a77a', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cfe8c20-c864-57b6-a2e1-6c1a2fc2a77a'}, {'abstract': 'Columbia University held a symposium that mainly focused on the promise of Big Data technology, but concerns were raised that the technology was racing ahead of society’s understanding of it.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/the-potential-and-the-risks-of-data-science/', 'snippet': 'Columbia University held a symposium that mainly focused on the promise of Big Data technology, but concerns were raised that the technology was racing ahead of society’s understanding of it.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Columbia University held a daylong symposium on Friday as a kind of brainy coming-out party for its new Institute for Data Sciences and Engineering. The institute is a collection of interdisciplinary centers including ones for cybersecurity, financial analytics, health analytics, new media and smart cities. It points to the direction universities will have to take if the bundle of technologies called Big Data\xa0— new data and artificial intelligence tools\xa0— really are to transform industries, as its champions predict.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Potential and the Risks of Data Science', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pentland, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bloomberg LP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a8e4a07-18d2-5521-abeb-27ca2ea1b123', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a8e4a07-18d2-5521-abeb-27ca2ea1b123'}, {'abstract': 'Using a contentious medium to try to shape its future.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/contest-seeks-to-honor-anti-advertising-ads/', 'snippet': 'Using a contentious medium to try to shape its future.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Shoot the first anti-advertising ad” is the pitch for a contest sponsored by Adblock Plus, the popular software that whites out Web site advertisements. The winning 30- to 90-second video submission, Adblock Plus says, will receive a prize of class time or video equipment worth $5,000 from a film institute in the Philippines.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Contest Seeks to Honor Anti-Advertising Ads', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Contest Seeks to Honor Anti-Advertising Ads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:25:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f566fc9-5198-516a-8eea-39eaa1511a4b', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f566fc9-5198-516a-8eea-39eaa1511a4b'}, {'abstract': 'Next, Portugal.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/just-say-nao/', 'snippet': 'Next, Portugal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Update: yes, I know about the tilda, but could not persuade the software to show it. The tilda, I’ve always said, means that there’s an “n” at the end that isn’t written but which you don’t pronounce — if you’ve ever heard Portuguese, you’ll understand what I mean.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just Say Nao', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:28:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fbb7d05-8640-54d4-b93f-4a131c308db6', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fbb7d05-8640-54d4-b93f-4a131c308db6'}, {'abstract': 'When shoppers buy two Clarins items at Macy’s, Clarins will donate $1 to an antihunger group, the equivalent of paying for 10 school meals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/business/media/a-cosmetics-freebie-with-a-cause-from-clarins.html', 'snippet': 'When shoppers buy two Clarins items at Macy’s, Clarins will donate $1 to an antihunger group, the equivalent of paying for 10 school meals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR decades, cosmetic companies have embraced gift-with-purchase promotions, where consumers who spend a given amount are rewarded with a free assortment of travel-size products and a cosmetic bag or tote bag. So popular are gift-with-purchase enticements that some consumers are loath to buy cosmetics without the freebies, and Web sites like GWP Addict track the offers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 738}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1666, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/08/business/adco/adco-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cosmetics Freebie With a Cause', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cosmetics Freebie With a Cause', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newman, Andrew Adam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Macy's Inc", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clarins SA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-07T22:34:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Adam Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Adam', 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11b39a48-a05a-5262-9361-4d5ce55b812c', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11b39a48-a05a-5262-9361-4d5ce55b812c'}, {'abstract': 'A big part of what Roger Ebert left behind was a remarkable template for how a lone journalist can become something much more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/business/media/roger-eberts-legacy-as-a-relentless-empire-builder.html', 'snippet': 'A big part of what Roger Ebert left behind was a remarkable template for how a lone journalist can become something much more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At journalism conferences and online, media strivers talk over and over about becoming their own brand, hoping that some magical combination of tweets, video spots, appearances and, yes, even actual written articles, will help their name come to mean something. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/business/media/carr-new-headshot/carr-new-headshot-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/12/business/media/carr-new-headshot/carr-new-headshot-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/business/media/carr-new-headshot/carr-new-headshot-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/business/media/carr-new-headshot/carr-new-headshot-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/0