'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69d228d7-cc56-5aa2-9c13-cabf55c1c6b6', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69d228d7-cc56-5aa2-9c13-cabf55c1c6b6'}, {'abstract': 'Russia’s xenophobic officialdom can’t keep out of anything, not even a spelling contest.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/dictation-partial-dictatorship/', 'snippet': 'Russia’s xenophobic officialdom can’t keep out of anything, not even a spelling contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Sometimes something totally geeky suddenly catches on and becomes truly popular. This is what happened with an event called Totalny Diktant (Total Dictation). Back in 2004, a group of Russian-grammar enthusiasts in the city of Novosibirsk invited other proud spellers and punctuators to join them for an afternoon of taking dictation. Each participant’s text is corrected and the number of mistakes compared with the average from all texts. For the first five years the event drew an average of 200 people each spring. Then it went big. And then it went terribly wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Total Dictation, Partial Dictatorship', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-08T09:58:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2605fa19-3ac7-56a5-a120-1173f125992b', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2605fa19-3ac7-56a5-a120-1173f125992b'}, {'abstract': 'A Q. and A. with Erik Stover, the chief operating officer of the reborn New York Cosmos.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/q-and-a-with-the-cosmos-erik-stover/', 'snippet': 'A Q. and A. with Erik Stover, the chief operating officer of the reborn New York Cosmos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New New York Cosmos begin play in the newish North American Soccer League in August, playing their home games at Hofstra University in Hempstead, N.Y., on Long Island. On Monday, the club announced plans to train at Mitchel Field Athletic Complex in Uniondale, N.Y. Erik Stover, a former executive with the Red Bulls of Major League Soccer, joined the Cosmos over the winter and recently spoke with a contributor to the Goal blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/04/sports/soccer/cosmoss/cosmoss-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/04/sports/soccer/cosmoss/cosmoss-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/04/sports/soccer/cosmoss/cosmoss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/04/sports/soccer/cosmoss/cosmoss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With the Cosmos’ Erik Stover', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Cosmos (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North American Soccer League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-08T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24079b9d-fb2e-56d4-a070-1431c4cd1030', 'word_count': 1550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24079b9d-fb2e-56d4-a070-1431c4cd1030'}, {'abstract': 'The United States leads the world in battery research, but skilled manufacturing labor lags. Battery University seeks to change that with education.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/battery-university-aims-to-train-a-workforce-for-next-generation-energy-storage/', 'snippet': 'The United States leads the world in battery research, but skilled manufacturing labor lags. Battery University seeks to change that with education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, San Jose State University is offering the first classes in what it calls “battery university,” a series in its professional development program intended to train a work force for the next generation of battery makers. The curriculum is being developed by more than 100 experts from the emerging battery storage sector — mostly from the San Francisco Bay Area — and will focus not only on developing the technology, but also on ways to make it cost-effective and realistically usable in electric cars, renewable energy or smartphones and laptops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Battery University’ Aims to Train a Work Force for Next-Generation Energy Storage', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'A123 Systems Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LG Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Jose State University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-08T10:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brita Belli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88b50c03-2248-5b96-907f-51ea9ec85f05', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88b50c03-2248-5b96-907f-51ea9ec85f05'}, {'abstract': 'Many Americans favor elimination of the cap on taxable Social Security earnings in order to protect retirement benefits, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/the-peoples-choice-for-the-peoples-pension/', 'snippet': 'Many Americans favor elimination of the cap on taxable Social Security earnings in order to protect retirement benefits, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economi