'nyt://article/e137b2ee-97d3-56fe-a27f-bec7d60c2121'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at Margaret Thatcher’s mutual, and mutually advantageous, admiration society with Mikhail Gorbachev in the final years of the Cold War.', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/08/maggie-and-gorby/', 'snippet': 'A look back at Margaret Thatcher’s mutual, and mutually advantageous, admiration society with Mikhail Gorbachev in the final years of the Cold War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the evening of March 31, 1987, viewers in the Soviet Union turned on their TV sets and watched (I’d bet no one turned it off) an astonishing spectacle. Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher, concluding a visit to Moscow, was pitted against three of the state-controlled media’s most polished propagandists. For 50 minutes, she tied them in knots, shifting between firm diplomacy and patient, school-marmish exposition, with – whenever one of the interviewers tried to interrupt – flashes of Trunchbull discipline. Even by the standards of the day – Mikhail Gorbachev’s cascade of glasnost, the thawing of the Cold War – the Thatcher interview was amazing. She articulated a peace-through-strength line on nuclear and conventional arms control, disclosing along the way facts that Soviet viewers had never read or heard in their media about their own arsenals. (A transcript is here.) She heaped praise on Gorbachev but, at a time when Gorbachev and President Reagan were indulging in talk about a world without nuclear weapons, Thatcher explained to viewers the European view that only nuclear deterrence had sustained 40 years of peace. The interview ran uncut.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maggie and Gorby', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorbachev, Mikhail S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thatcher, Margaret H', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-08T21:38:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e8e7c78-77f1-5889-8ec7-8af0696eeab4', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e8e7c78-77f1-5889-8ec7-8af0696eeab4'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Pulitzer, the Palm Beach princess of prints, created an enduring fashion uniform for wealthy socialites and jet setters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/04/11/fashion/20130411-PULITZER.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Pulitzer, the Palm Beach princess of prints, created an enduring fashion uniform for wealthy socialites and jet setters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ms. Pulitzer, the Palm Beach princess of prints, created an enduring fashion uniform for wealthy socialites and jet setters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/fashion/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 860, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/11/fashion/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 860}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/fashion/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 715, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/fashion/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE/20130411-PULITZER-slide-ZMQE-superJumbo-v2