'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dd1b364-e139-5cb2-b56c-cb150d3dbb79', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dd1b364-e139-5cb2-b56c-cb150d3dbb79'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (BBC America) SPIES OF WARSAW In the conclusion of this two-part mini-series based on the novel by Alan Furst, Col. Jean-François Mercier (David Tennant, foreground above), a French military attaché in Warsaw, goes undercover in Czechoslovakia as he searches for the man who can lead him into the Nazi war machine. Anna Skarbek (Janet Montgomery), his mistress and a lawyer for the League of Nations, receives devastating news about her former lover, the Russian journalist Maxim Mostov (Piotr Baumann), and flees to Spain on a mission of mercy. The drama “has an almost comic-book adherence to stereotype, from the dashing Polish nobleman to the sallow, pockmarked Nazi border guard,” Alessandra Stanley wrote in The New York Times. “On the other hand, there is nothing more satisfying than a prewar espionage story that shows, up close and told-you-so, how most of Europe slept through Hitler’s rise.” She added, “There are many impediments to true love in ‘Spies of Warsaw,’ but Mercier, solitary and sorrowful, learns to trust his heart — and not the Maginot Line.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-10T05:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlenam