'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/452891e8-4266-5bb9-9276-4fef6ba629a6', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/452891e8-4266-5bb9-9276-4fef6ba629a6'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/the-gun-report-april-10-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reports of drive-by shootings first dominated the headlines in the 1980s, and were largely associated with the infamous Los Angeles gang wars as well as the drug trade in cities such as Chicago. But readers of our daily report will notice that such shootings are not a remnant of the Reagan-Thatcher years: they’re happening all the time, in Texas, Alabama, Tennessee—and now Tupelo, Mississippi. That’s where Carol, the 39-year-old Asian elephant used in the Ringling Bros. and Barnum & Bailey Circus, was shot in a drive-by early Tuesday morning. At around 2:00 a.m., someone fired his gun into the BancorpSouth Arena parking lot where the circus animals were being kept.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: April 10, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mobile (Ala)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montgomery County (Md)', 'rank': 8, 'majo