, 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce35fa93-0965-5f12-bfc2-f42149bfd596', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce35fa93-0965-5f12-bfc2-f42149bfd596'}, {'abstract': 'In Luke Pearson’s graphic novel “Hilda and the Bird Parade,” the blue-haired heroine has to overcome a world of foolish and powerful adults.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/books/hilda-and-the-bird-parade-by-luke-pearson.html', 'snippet': 'In Luke Pearson’s graphic novel “Hilda and the Bird Parade,” the blue-haired heroine has to overcome a world of foolish and powerful adults.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the Broadway stage a heroine named Matilda is showing that magical things can happen to brainy, confident and righteous little girls. Meanwhile, in a new graphic novel, a blue-haired child named Hilda is proving much the same point. In Matilda’s case, what must be overcome is a world of foolish and powerful adults. For Hilda, the obstacle is a new town, Trolberg, where a well-meaning but\xa0insensitive group of peers prove insufficient armor against an unfamiliar and seemingly forbidding environment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/books/10childrens/10childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 282, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/10/books/10childrens/10childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 282}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/books/10childrens/10childrens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo',