 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/420e5c31-c76c-539c-8ad5-d014de706e24', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/420e5c31-c76c-539c-8ad5-d014de706e24'}, {'abstract': 'A big problem with a proposed change in the bankruptcy procedure is its effort to penalize the provider of debtor-in-possession financing if the new financing is used to “overpay” creditors.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/whats-wrong-with-the-chapter-14-proposal/', 'snippet': 'A big problem with a proposed change in the bankruptcy procedure is its effort to penalize the provider of debtor-in-possession financing if the new financing is used to “overpay” creditors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, I am speaking at New York University’s Stern School of Business on a panel on Dodd-Frank’s orderly liquidation authority. It seemed like an appropriate time to discuss why, although I’m no huge fan of the authority, I also have my doubts about its leading alternative.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Wrong With the Chapter 14 Proposal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-10T22:17:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3777f532-f7c3-5d82-a0cf-6ef43a7b30ec', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3777f532-f7c3-5d82-a0cf-6ef43a7b30ec'}, {'abstract': 'Hiroko Takeda’s collection intermingles silk and metal, mohair and linen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/garden/a-new-spirit-in-the-material-world.html', 'snippet': 'Hiroko Takeda’s collection intermingles silk and metal, mohair and linen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trained in Japan, England and Guatemala, Hiroko Takeda, the Brooklyn-based textile artist, is not easily characterized as modern, classic or futurist. Her collection, which intermingles silk and metal, mohair and linen, is all that and more. Whether weaving a throw on a traditional loom or knitting a huge architectural commission in black metallic thread, Ms. Takeda experiments with color, texture and technique. After working for storied textile firms in Tokyo, Kyoto and New York, she struck out on her own in 2010. Since then, Calvin Klein Home, Urban Zen and Christian Dior have commissioned one-of-a-kind pieces as artwork for their stores. Custom fabrics can cost $200 to $600 a yard, not including setup fees, and a 75-by-25-inch panel of her intricate Giant Waffle Cartography series can be priced upward of $1,500. Information: (917) 676-8329 or hirokotakeda.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/garden/11TEXTILES_SPAN/11TEXTILES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/11/garden/11TEXTILES_SPAN/11TEXTILES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/garden/11TEXTILES_SPAN/11TEXTILES_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/garden/11TEXTILES_SPAN/11TEXTILES_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg'