 York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Happiness, Beyond the Data', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Happiness', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T01:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa02c41e-f76e-52c3-9443-536ed8c452a8', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa02c41e-f76e-52c3-9443-536ed8c452a8'}, {'abstract': 'The fresh look at internal practices came after news that a senior partner at KPMG gave confidential information about two clients.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/after-kpmg-audit-firms-reconsider-procedures/', 'snippet': 'The fresh look at internal practices came after news that a senior partner at KPMG gave confidential information about two clients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Accounting firms are being forced to take a fresh look at their practices in the wake of an insider-trading scandal involving one of KPMG’s top auditors, as new rules requiring more transparency by the firms are already being considered.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After KPMG, Audit Firms Reconsider Procedures', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under Scrutiny, Auditors Reconsider Procedures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACCOUNTING AND ACCOUNTANTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'na