}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burkhart, Bryan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh (Pa)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T11:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bryan Burkhart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bryan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burkhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe2989b9-4494-5881-a5e2-6dcadcd5fb55', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe2989b9-4494-5881-a5e2-6dcadcd5fb55'}, {'abstract': 'Supposedly a plan to improve the quality of schooling, Mexico’s education reform is a move to put down the teachers’ union.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/mexico-education-reform-taming-teachers-union/', 'snippet': 'Supposedly a plan to improve the quality of schooling, Mexico’s education reform is a move to put down the teachers’ union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — When the federal policeman approached the strikers, they started to sing the national anthem. “I am Mexican,” the policeman told them. “You are Mexican. But there are thousands of Mexicans behind me who just want to get on their way. So I am asking you to free at least one lane — just one lane.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Those Who Can’t Teach Block', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T11:10:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlos Puig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Puig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0c97211-b441-559b-af6d-b2c86a292ee6', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0c97211-b441-559b-af6d-b2c86a292ee6'}, {'abstract': 'Why I’m bullish about the Japanese experiment.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/monetary-policy-in-a-liquidity-trap/', 'snippet': 'Why I’m bullish about the Japanese experiment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve made it clear that I very much approve of Japan’s new monetary aggressiveness. But I gather that some readers are confused – haven’t I been arguing that monetary policy is ineffective in a liquidity trap? The brief answer is that current policy is ineffective, but that you can still get traction if you can change investors’ beliefs about expected future monetary policy – which was the moral of my original Japan paper, lo these 15 years ago. But I thought it might be worthwhile to go over this again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monetary Policy In A Liquidity Trap', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T11:22:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61f57ea6-4671-52af-9c1d-e388274fff28', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61f57ea6-4671-52af-9c1d-e388274fff28'}, {'abstract': 'Citigroup agreed on Thursday to sell its consumer banking business in Turkey to the local lender DenizBank as it continues to reduce its operations outside of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/citigroup-sells-turkish-operations-as-it-pares-back-internationally/', 'snippet': 'Citigroup agreed on Thursday to sell its consumer banking business in Turkey to the local lender DenizBank as it continues to reduce its operations outside of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Citigroup agreed on Thursday to sell its consumer banking business in Turkey to the local lender DenizBank in its latest move to offload international assets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citigroup Sells Turkish Consumer Unit as It Pares Global Operations', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sberbank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T11:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/033d68a8-2806-501c-8053-d381513a7d36', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/033d68a8-2806-501c-8053-d381513a7d36'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Zao, a Chinese émigré in Paris who merged Eastern and Western aesthetic traditions, helped to shape avant-garde art in postwar Europe and became a commercially successful artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/world/asia/zao-wou-ki-seen-as-modern-art-master-dies-at-92.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Zao, a Chinese émigré in Paris who merged Eastern and Western aesthetic traditions, helped to shape avant-garde art in postwar Europe and became a commercially successful artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zao Wou-ki, a Chinese émigré who merged Eastern and Western aesthetic traditions in his abstract paintings — helping to shape avant-garde art in postwar Europe and attracting a newly wealthy Asian following that made him one of the most commercially successful living artists in either hemisphere — died on April 9 in Nyon, Switzerland. He was 92.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/zao-2-obit/zao-2-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/zao-2-obit/zao-2-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/zao-2-obit/zao-2-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/zao-2-obit/zao-2-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/zao-2-obit/zao-2-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75,