ype': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/85e997de-f8ee-5194-bcb8-1434c0c682ea', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/85e997de-f8ee-5194-bcb8-1434c0c682ea'}, {'abstract': 'The story behind the famous 54th Massachusetts Infantry.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/creating-the-glorious-54th/', 'snippet': 'The story behind the famous 54th Massachusetts Infantry.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Does it not behoove every colored man,” James Henry Gooding, a 26-year-old free black man from New Bedford, Mass., asked the readers of his hometown newspaper in March 1863, “to consider … whether he cannot be one of the glorious 54th?” New Bedford was a wealthy whaling town with a large abolitionist community, but one where many black citizens toiled in poverty. With such conditions likely in mind, Gooding believed that military service provided the key to African-American advancement. “There is more dignity in carrying a musket in defense of liberty and right,” he maintained, “than there is in shaving a man’s face, or waiting on somebody’s table.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Creating ‘The Glorious 54th’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Military)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shaw, Robert Gould', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T16:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn David Brasher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Brasher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fae6e986-17e9-5e96-b38a-1aa95356620f', 'word_count': 1678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fae6e986-17e9-5e96-b38a-1aa95356620f'}, {'abstract': 'Akira Chiba, who summited 100 mountains listed in a popular book, talks about Japan’s spiritual link to mountains and an experience where his “tears wouldn’t stop.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/travel/climbing-100-famous-japanese-mountains.html', 'snippet': 'Akira Chiba, who summited 100 mountains listed in a popular book, talks about Japan’s spiritual link to mountains and an experience where his “tears wouldn’t stop.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Akira Chiba reached the top of Mount Yarigatake a dozen years ago and stared out into the volcanic gas clouds that wreathed its peak, it was the realization of what for some is a long-held dream. He had just summited the last of the 100 mountains listed in a popular book by Kyuya Fukada — a goal held by many Japanese mountaineers, including Crown Prince Naruhito. Reaching the marker, though, he was not overcome with elation. He experienced the normal exhilaration of summiting a mountain and a longing for distant peaks, one that he has continued to indulge.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/travel/14QNA1-sf/14QNA1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/travel/14QNA1-sf/14QNA1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 818}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None