banks are likely to offer investors paltry returns, and they call large banks like their own “un-investable.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – As Jamie Dimon puts the final touches on JPMorgan Chase’s latest earnings report, scheduled to be published on Friday, the bank’s own analysts are raining on his parade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Problem With Investment Banks, as Seen By a Bank', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Bank AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:09:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4b2b3dc-d0b9-5898-b61c-620fcf46a1bc', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4b2b3dc-d0b9-5898-b61c-620fcf46a1bc'}, {'abstract': 'No other generation pays as much income tax to the federal government as baby boomers, but generosity may not be the real explanation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/booming/baby-boomers-pay-biggest-share-of-income-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'No other generation pays as much income tax to the federal government as baby boomers, but generosity may not be the real explanation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Baby boomers, stand tall! This is our moment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 537}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 916, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1282, 'width': 1433, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/booming/11question-booming-taxes/11question-booming-taxes-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Am I Paying All These Taxes?', 'kicker': 'Question Mark', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:11:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c31092c-afe5-504a-a842-e8e063e8260d', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c31092c-afe5-504a-a842-e8e063e8260d'}, {'abstract': 'Data science is the hot new pursuit in higher education, a field spawned by the enormous amounts of data that modern technologies create. Claire Cain Miller writes about this trendy new field in the Education Life section of The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/education-life-teaching-the-techies/', 'snippet': 'Data science is the hot new pursuit in higher education, a field spawned by the enormous amounts of data that modern technologies create. Claire Cain Miller writes about this trendy new field in the Education Life section of The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Data science is the hot new pursuit in higher education, a field spawned by the enormous amounts of data that modern technologies create — be it about the online behavior of Facebook users, tissue samples of cancer patients or crime in cities. Data scientists crunch the data, use mathematical models to analyze it, create narratives or visualizations to explain it, then suggest how to use the information to make decisions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 306}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 522, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1044, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/11/technology/11bits-edlife/11bits-edlife-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Education Life: Teaching the Techies', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:16:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7197b9ed-3a6d-5d1f-a57e-3d23d3994c1d', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7197b9ed-3a6d-5d1f-a57e-3d23d3994c1d'}, {'abstract': 'Warburg Pincus bought the eye care company for $3.7 billion at the peak of the buyout boom and has been reportedly seeking a valuation of up to $10 billion. But a figure like $8 billion makes more sense.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/how-to-value-bausch-lomb/', 'snippet': 'Warburg Pincus bought the eye care company for $3.7 billion at the peak of the buyout boom and has been reportedly seeking a valuation of up to $10 billion. But a figure like $8 billion makes more sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mooted value for Bausch & Lomb needs to be re-examined through fresh lenses. The private equity firm Warburg Pincus bought the eye care company for $3.7 billion at the peak of the buyout boom and has been reportedly seeking a valuation of up to $10 billion. Even after cleaning up problems with recalls and accounting, Bausch & Lomb isn’t worth a big premium to other similar companies. A figure like $8 billion makes more sense.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Value Bausch & Lomb', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BAUSCH & LOMB INCORPORATED', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Warburg Pincus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:18:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Cyran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cyran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c513c6f2-72e8-5034-8bcf-71731bb4e3a1', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c513c6f2-72e8-5034-8bcf-71731bb4e3a1'}, {'abstract': 'In honor of the upcoming Basel watch fair,