orris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bcf8a8c-855a-5363-84cf-6fd7e8c4755a', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bcf8a8c-855a-5363-84cf-6fd7e8c4755a'}, {'abstract': 'Cablevision and its Brooklyn cable installers have not settled on a contract since the workers voted 15 months ago to unionize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/nyregion/cablevision-attacked-again-by-nlrb-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Cablevision and its Brooklyn cable installers have not settled on a contract since the workers voted 15 months ago to unionize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The battle between Cablevision and organized labor has dragged on for months, punctuated by protests and lawsuits, in a feud that has been particularly nasty even by most labor-management standards. Now the National Labor Relations Board has accused the company of acting illegally to avoid reaching an agreement with several hundred unionized cable installers in Brooklyn.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Labor Board Complaining Over Cablevision’s Tactics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Labor Board Complaining Over Cablevision’s Tactics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenhouse, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communications Workers of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cablevision Systems Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:45:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86f7d276-18e9-52b8-8fb2-ed29515bd85d', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86f7d276-18e9-52b8-8fb2-ed29515bd85d'}, {'abstract': 'Only Julia Sweeney would find herself adopting a baby named Mulan — “like the movie.” And she knows it.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/11/julia-sweeney-sees-the-absurd-in-motherhood-and-shares/', 'snippet': 'Only Julia Sweeney would find herself adopting a baby named Mulan — “like the movie.” And she knows it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Within pages of opening “If It’s Not One Thing, It’s Your Mother” by Julia Sweeney (familiar to me, and many, as a former star of “Saturday Night Live” and the creator of the one-woman show and memoir “God Said Ha!”), I felt like I’d made a new friend — a funny, wry, so-cool-she-doesn’t-care-about-being-cool fellow parent with a voice I loved. I asked Julia for permission to share some of the book (it’s below) and I also asked her (among other things) what it’s like to see the world through her eyes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Julia Sweeney Sees the Absurd in Motherhood (and Shares)', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADOPTIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Personal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sweeney, Julia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T17:51:45+0000', 'document_t