, 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/801ecf11-8d48-58f6-9fe8-3294e62b4cf8', 'word_count': 2026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/801ecf11-8d48-58f6-9fe8-3294e62b4cf8'}, {'abstract': 'For writers who inhabit many different worlds, there may be no such thing as a definitive “style.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/voices-inside-their-heads.html', 'snippet': 'For writers who inhabit many different worlds, there may be no such thing as a definitive “style.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mohsin Hamid’s latest novel boasts a startlingly distinctive voice, as commanding and unadorned as its title, “How to Get Filthy Rich in Rising Asia.” But what may be most exciting about that voice — the whole book is delivered in the terse declarations of a mock self-help manual — is that it’s nothing like the voices we met in his two earlier novels. The first, “Moth Smoke,” was a moody, multipart evocation of party kids and S.U.V.’s in Lahore, poetically given life by Hamid as if to the manner born; his second, “The Reluctant Fundamentalist,” was a fast-moving monologue from a young Pakistani, educated at Princeton and once well employed in New York (as Hamid was), who’s returned to his homeland post-9/11 and lures a Daniel Pearl-like American into a confrontation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 820}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 749, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1228, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Iyer/0414-Iyer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Voices Inside Their Heads', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Voices Inside Their Heads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iyer, Pico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:26:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pico Iyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pico', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Iyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87a84098-f7b6-50ec-a169-d256c5bd71c0', 'word_count': 1325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87a84098-f7b6-50ec-a169-d256c5bd71c0'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Savage began writing his syndicated column, “Savage Love,” more than 20 years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Savage began writing his syndicated column, “Savage Love,” more than 20 years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Savage, who reviews Jeff Chu’s “Does Jesus Really Love Me?” this week, is perhaps best known for his syndicated column, “Savage Love.” The column first appeared in The Stranger, a Seattle weekly newspaper, in 1991, and is now published worldwide — quite an accomplishment for something that began as a joke. “A gay guy giving advice to straight people about straight sex?” as Savage put it in an e-mail. “The plan — the joke — was that I was treating straight relationships and straight sex with the same contempt that straight advice columnists had always treated gay sex and gay relationships. I thought I could stretch the joke out for six months at most. But the column took off. It turned out that straight people really enjoyed being treated with contempt — it was a new experience for them. Twenty-plus years later, I’m still writing the column.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-upfront/0414-upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 886, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-upfront/0414-upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 886}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-upfront/0414-upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 694, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-u