s eclectic characters — a chimpanzee, a ghost, two Indian wrestlers in Edwardian London — are entangled in sticky human relationships.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:29:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ac5a06a-9002-5807-905a-91431fd7bcf1', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ac5a06a-9002-5807-905a-91431fd7bcf1'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BETWEEN MAN AND BEAST: An Unlikely Explorer, the Evolution Debates, and the African Adventure That Took the Victorian World by Storm,  by Monte Reel. (Doubleday, $26.95.) In the age of \xadDarwin, the discovery of gorillas caused a sensation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:29:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ba7d439-734e-57d5-9878-1a85b89ef977', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ba7d439-734e-57d5-9878-1a85b89ef977'}, {'abstract': 'Paulo Coelho assembled his novel “Manuscript Found in Accra,” No. 2 on this week’s hardcover list, after soliciting ideas from his 6.3 million Twitter followers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Paulo Coelho assembled his novel “Manuscript Found in Accra,” No. 2 on this week’s hardcover list, after soliciting ideas from his 6.3 million Twitter followers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE LAST KEY: Paulo Coelho, the elfin guru of a novelist who unleashed “The Alchemist” on the world, is at No. 2 on this week’s hardcover list with a new novel, “Manuscript Found in Accra” — although a more forthright title might be “Manuscript Found on Social Media,” since Coelho assembled the book after soliciting ideas from his 6.3 million Twitter followers. The resulting volume of ponderous clichés will surprise nobody familiar with Coelho, or Twitter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 471, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 471, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:31:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba0a2d5f-51ba-5222-a85a-bc75eb7c6e30', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba0a2d5f-51ba-5222-a85a-bc75eb7c6e30'}, {'abstract': 'Journeys of self-discovery await the adventurers of these fantasy tales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/water-castle-and-cats-of-tanglewood-forest.html', 'snippet': 'Journeys of self-discovery await the adventurers of these fantasy tales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my oldest son was 3 and something didn’t go quite the way he had envisioned, he would lament: “I want to go back! I want to go back!” Surely, one of the hardest jobs of childhood is accepting that life moves forward and when something terrible happens, it cannot be undone with either words or wishes. Two new middle-grade fantasy novels feature children on the verge of adolescence who want very much to change the course of life events, and who learn it’s never as simple as it may seem.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hope Springs Eternal', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hope Springs Eternal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blakemore, Megan Frazer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Lint, Charles', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vess, Charles', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:31:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By S. S. Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e29e94b-6297-5043-9d0a-24bfcdaa81fd', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e29e94b-6297-5043-9d0a-24bfcdaa81fd'}, {'abstract': 'Four picture books showcase villains and tough situations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/brief-thief-by-michael-escoffier-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Four picture books showcase villains and tough situations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who knows children knows they are not timid when it comes to pondering ethical conundrums. Rather, as their routine cry of “No fair!” proves, they live for it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1438, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/0414-Handy01/0414-Handy01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bad Guys', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Bad Guys', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handy, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snicket, Lemony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klassen, Jon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willems, Mo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donaldson, Julia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scheffler, Axel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Escoffier, Michael', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:31:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Handy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f6d7200-0177-5a34-b9c3-3d4249110e40', 'word_count': 1079, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f6d7200-0177-5a34-b9c3-3d4249110e40'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage girl in rural New Hampshire grapples with death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/the-center-of-everything-by-linda-urban.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage girl in rural New Hampshire grapples with death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Grandparents often appear in children’s literature just so they can be taken away. At times, death provides a largely unearned “teachable moment,” while at others it conjures a powerful approximation of grief. Linda Urban’s new novel, “The Center of Everything,” uses the premise of a grandparent’s death in a surprising way, exploring not only grief but also its occasional companions, anxiety and guilt. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Her Town', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Her Town', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Urban, Linda', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:31:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef1bc539-a5d0-57e1-b780-7d5ddca14fba', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef1bc539-a5d0-57e1-b780-7d5ddca14fba'}, {'abstract': 'This mirror sends people into the past, but can’t retrieve them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/obsidian-mirror-by-catherine-fisher.html', 'snippet': 'This mirror sends people into the past, but can’t retrieve them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All successful novels for young adults must pass what one might call a narcissistic grandiosity test. Adolescents are, of course, at a developmental stage in which a certain level of passionate self-involvement is the norm. This is both natural and necessary. Good young adult novels must therefore reflect and dramatize the reader’s fantasy of being victimized and misunderstood, yet secretly possessed of powers that allow mastery of one’s destiny. Consequently, the characters in Y.A. fiction tend to live in special worlds, somehow set apart from those of other people. Often, they’re either escaping from or saving their parents; if they discover a way to do both simultaneously, even better.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Get Back', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Get Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Catherine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:32:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophie Gee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ff2c31b-4836-5e8d-ae91-dca6199262de', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ff2c31b-4836-5e8d-ae91-dca6199262de'}, {'abstract': 'The final volume of Jane Gardam’s trilogy looks more deeply at the life of its hero’s nemesis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/last-friends-by-jane-gardam.html', 'snippet': 'The final volume of Jane Gardam’s trilogy looks more deeply at the life of its hero’s nemesis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a list of my top reading interests, the lives of kooky geezers in a fading English village don’t rank high. But all it takes is a page or two of Jane Gardam to force a reconsideration. Her prose is so perceptive and fluid that it feels mentally healthful, exiling the noise and clutter of your mind as efficiently as a Schubert sonata. She could make actuarial tables pleasurable.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14LINDGREN/14LINDGREN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14LINDGREN/14LINDGREN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 317}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14LINDGREN/14LINDGREN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14LINDGREN/14LINDGREN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1081, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran