ory than on myth. His father was a Chicago newspaperman, and so Hainey romanticized the bygone world of Chicago newspapermen. He venerated good reporting. He drank Scotch on the rocks. He sensed secrets in his childhood home. His mother told him his father had died of a heart attack. “I had to pry this out of her,” Hainey writes in “After Visiting Friends.” She was vague about the circumstances. “All my life,” he says, “I’ve felt the story I was told about how my father died did not add up.” An obituary mentioned that his father had been visiting friends: who? It also mentioned a strange address: where had his father gone, and why? So Hainey looked for answers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 811, 'width': 1122, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14THOMAS/14THOMAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unreliable Sources', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unreliable Sources', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hainey, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:34:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louisa Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/faae71c3-294d-5784-bac1-9606edcfc809', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faae71c3-294d-5784-bac1-9606edcfc809'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer Dorothea Lange and her most enduring portrait inspired Marisa Silver’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/mary-coin-by-marisa-silver.html', 'snippet': 'The photographer Dorothea Lange and her most enduring portrait inspired Marisa Silver’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We know the photograph. A seated woman — hand at her jaw, skin etched with worry — looks into the middle distance. Children rest their heads on her shoulders, faces turned away; an infant lies bundled in her lap. By capturing the plight of California’s \xadDepression-era migrant workers, Dorothea Lange’s “Migrant Mother” transformed a nation’s attitudes toward the poor from the moment of its publication in 1936. Today, its immediacy dulled by time and fame, it functions mainly as a visual metonym for the Great Depression. We look at it, but we no longer see it, to borrow a distinction made in Marisa Silver’s phenomenal new novel, “Mary Coin.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Knowing Eye', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Knowing Eye', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Marisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-11T20:34:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antoine Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antoine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6288f6cf-e40c-5705-a297-0cd3e212c8ab', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6288f6cf-e40c-5705-a297-0cd3e212c8ab'}, {'abstract': 'New picture books starring a pair of goslings, animated chickens, lucky ducklings, and a boy and his bluebird.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/books/review/bluebird-by-bob-staake-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New picture books starring a pair of goslings, animated chickens, lucky ducklings, and a boy and his bluebird.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JASPER & JOOP  A Perfect Pair: One Tidy, One Messy.  Written and illustrated by Olivier Dunrea.32 pp. Houghton Mifflin. $9.99. (Picture book; ages 3 to 7) ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/books/review/14bluebird/14bluebird-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 259, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'im