'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a715a79-ee98-5c23-9f27-5f0dc2c9b60d', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a715a79-ee98-5c23-9f27-5f0dc2c9b60d'}, {'abstract': '“John Singer Sargent Watercolors,” a Brooklyn Museum show that also features works from the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, highlights a different side of an artist best known for his flattering oil portraits of the rich.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/arts/design/john-singer-sargent-watercolors-at-brooklyn-museum.html', 'snippet': '“John Singer Sargent Watercolors,” a Brooklyn Museum show that also features works from the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, highlights a different side of an artist best known for his flattering oil portraits of the rich.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly after the turn of the 20th century John Singer Sargent went through a midlife crisis. An American expatriate in London, he was sick of painting flattering portraits of rich people, though he could do it with such seemingly effortless panache that he’d become one of England’s most celebrated artists. He began to retire from that lucrative but repetitious occupation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/arts/12JPSARGENT1_SPAN/JPSARGENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/12/arts/12JPSARGENT1_SPAN/JPSARGENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/