: [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Police in Schools, More Children in Court', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Police in Schools, More Children in Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Discipline (Students)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T04:16:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c83225d-c1aa-51ee-9fd7-b506a15ac238', 'word_count': 1399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c83225d-c1aa-51ee-9fd7-b506a15ac238'}, {'abstract': 'A media theorist says we’ve made bad choices with technology. Instead of using networked technologies to help us use time better, we live in a digital nova of immediate messages, each one demanding our attention. It’s time to rebel, he says, with some revolutionary quiet time.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/one-on-one-douglas-rushkoff-on-everything-happening-now/', 'snippet': 'A media theorist says we’ve made bad choices with technology. Instead of using networked technologies to help us use time better, we live in a digital nova of immediate messages, each one demanding our attention. It’s time to rebel, he says, with ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Douglas Rushkoff hurts the way only a onetime true believer can hurt. Before he taught at New York University and the New School, he was an early fan of the Internet. “I was a slacker, and it seemed like a way to slack,” he says. “We’d all work when we felt like it.” Instead of a contemplative paradise, however, the marriage of networked technology and capitalism tortures our consciousness with an incessant, demanding present. “We’ve attached ourselves to it,” he says; “We respond to things when it wants us to, which is all the time.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/technology/12bits-rushkoff-alt/12bits-rushkoff-alt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/technology/12bits-rushkoff-alt/12bits-rushkoff-alt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/technology/12bits-rushkoff-alt/12bits-rushkoff-alt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/12/technology/12bits-rushkoff-alt/12bits-rushkoff-alt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/technology/12bits-rushkoff-alt/12bits-rushkoff-alt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One on One: Douglas Rushkoff on Everything Happening Now', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushkoff, Douglas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T04:40:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b525c0fb-003d-5143-9e9a-729979b97d7e', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b525c0fb-003d-5143-9e9a-729979b97d7e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Sundance) ADAPTATION (2002) The director Spike Jonze and the writer Charlie Kaufman (along with his imaginary twin, Donald, who is also credited with writing the screenplay) journey into metafiction in this movie about the two Kaufmans (Nicolas Cage, in both roles) and Susan Orlean (Meryl Streep), a writer for The New Yorker whose book “The Orchid Thief” inspired the film. Chris Cooper won an Oscar for best supporting actor for his role as an orchid obsessive; Ms. Streep and Mr. Cage (above) also received Oscar nominations. “\xa0‘Adaptation’ is, most obviously, a movie about itself, as gleefully self-referential an exercise in auto-deconstruction as you could wish,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “But it is also, more deeply, a movie about its own nonexistence — a narrative that confronts both the impossibility and the desperate necessity of storytelling, and that short-circuits our expectations of coherence, plausibility and fidelity to lived reality even as it satisfies them. Common sense suggests that there could never be such a movie, but if there could, it would have to be one of the slipperiest, most fascinating and, by any sane reckoning, best movies of the year.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T05:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/72bef4ae-d4da-542d-868a-5164374ceee2', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72bef4ae-d4da-542d-868a-5164374ceee2'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/news-quiz-april-12-2013/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | April 12, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T05:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anastasia Economides', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anastasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Economides', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29ed37d8-24a0-51f4-b433-2f633e24806f', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29ed37d8-24a0-51f4-b433-2f633e24806f'}, {'abstract': 'The prime minister has vowed to repeal a controversial sorcery act after the latest in a string of brutal public killings of people accused of practicing black magic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/13/world/asia/papua-new-guinea-considers-repealing-sorcery-law.html', 'snippet': 'The prime minister has vowed to repeal a controversial sorcery act after the latest in a string of brutal public killings of people accused of practicing black magic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — Papua New Guinea’s prime minister, Peter O’Neill, has vowed to repeal the country’s controversial Sorcery Act after the latest in a string of brutal public killings of people accused of practicing black magic.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Papua New Guinea Considers Repealing Sorcery Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Papua New Guinea: Prime Minister\r\nSays He Wants to Repeal Sorcery Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Papua New Guinea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Witchcraft', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Targeted Killings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neill, Peter", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T06:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cbd079d-ee65-547c-a061-7a7135318533', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cbd079d-ee65-547c-a061-7a7135318533'}, {'abstract': 'Worries about the H7N9 bird flu; Russia bets on methane-powered cars; deeper debt for Cyprus; and “Django Unchained” is pulled from Chinese theaters.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/iht-quick-read-april-12/', 'snippet': 'Worries about the H7N9 bird flu; Russia bets on methane-powered cars; deeper debt for Cyprus; and “Django Unchained” is pulled from Chinese theaters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWS A report by researchers from China on the first three victims of the H7N9 bird flu was published with commentary from U.S. health officials, who said the disease “raised many urgent questions.” Denise Grady reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12gascars/12gascars-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12gascars/12gascars-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12gascars/12gascars-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12gascars/12gascars-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12gascars/12gascars-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/12/business/energy-environment/12