ingful workout. And it may bring other problems.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/ask-well-ball-chairs/', 'snippet': 'Although you might expect that sitting on an exercise ball would demand extra exertion to keep you upright and stable, it does not provide any meaningful workout. And it may bring other problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Question:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/10/health/10well_desk/10well_desk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ask Well: Do Ball Chairs Offer Benefits?', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Back (Human Body Part)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:13:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/912119a1-8b97-5602-9a8d-d08d1bd09d88', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/912119a1-8b97-5602-9a8d-d08d1bd09d88'}, {'abstract': 'Those beyond retirement age are not the only beneficiaries of Social Security. Children are among those whose payments would be reined in by a proposed change in the inflation formula.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/how-social-security-cuts-could-affect-children/', 'snippet': 'Those beyond retirement age are not the only beneficiaries of Social Security. Children are among those whose payments would be reined in by a proposed change in the inflation formula.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s budget includes a controversial — at least on the left — change in the way Social Security benefits are adjusted for inflation that would save the government about $130 billion over 10 years, but would reduce payments for tens of millions of Americans over time. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Social Security Cuts Could Affect Children', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inflation (Economics)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Security (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center on Budget and Policy Priorities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4702d51f-46cf-5ef5-834b-8eaad1c5bd57', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4702d51f-46cf-5ef5-834b-8eaad1c5bd57'}, {'abstract': 'Did the movement of the stars when I was born say anything about the man I became?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/decoding-a-44-year-old-horoscope.html', 'snippet': 'Did the movement of the stars when I was born say anything about the man I became?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The black folder had a cardboard, cartoon fish stapled to the front cover, indicating the birth sign: Pisces. Inside was an eight-page, all-italics work-up on me — the planets, my innate strengths and weaknesses. Cora Sitrusis of Cora Sitrusis’ School of Astrology Inc. had included instructions that were at times underlined (impress upon him the reason he must master such things as multiplication tables); in all caps (the horoscope shows TENDENCIES); and, very rarely, underlined and in all caps (ONCE HE LEARNS, HE NEVER FORGETS!). The horoscope was dated June 9, 1969. I was nearly 3 months old.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14lives/mag-14Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 413, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14lives/mag-14Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 413, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14lives/mag-14Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14lives/mag-14Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14lives/mag-14Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Decoding a 44-Year-Old Horoscope', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Astral Projection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morfit, Cameron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Astrology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cameron Morfit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cameron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morfit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62e43c8f-2be7-5b00-b0a8-1398e61471d1', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62e43c8f-2be7-5b00-b0a8-1398e61471d1'}, {'abstract': 'Jack Handey’s absurdist affirmations were sharp, surreal and hilarious. Is it possible he influenced a generation of serious poets?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/and-now-deep-thoughts-about-deep-thoughts.html', 'snippet': 'Jack Handey’s absurdist affirmations were sharp, surreal and hilarious. Is it possible he influenced a generation of serious poets?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This past fall, while teaching poetry to undergraduates, I witnessed something of a literary tragedy. Each week, I asked a few students to read a contemporary literary journal of their choosing and present one poem that particularly struck them. An unmistakable pattern began to emerge. In the final week of the quarter, I voiced my discovery: “Have you guys noticed that you gravitate toward poems that are like ‘Deep Thoughts,’ by Jack Handey?”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 102, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 102}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 175, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14riff/mag-14Riff-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'And Now, Deep Thoughts About ‘Deep Thoughts’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And Now, Deep Thoughts About ‘Deep Thoughts’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rooney, Kathleen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handey, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen Rooney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rooney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5eca9d58-2676-5943-a01f-76d0497df539', 'word_count': 1613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5eca9d58-2676-5943-a01f-76d0497df539'}, {'abstract': 'Corporations, alas, are people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/an-ethical-theory-of-relativity.html', 'snippet': 'Corporations, alas, are people.', 'lead_paragraph': ' My wife and I are fairly certain that the rent invoice from our landlord was too low. We believe it was a calculation error and that we should pay the correct amount. But a friend thinks we have a right to take advantage of the error. In discussing why we disagreed, I realized that I would have no problem taking advantage of such an error on my bill from my cable provider (I believe they regularly make “errors” that hurt their customers — but even if that weren’t the case, I still would feel the same way). Is there any difference in the ethics of dealing with, say, Time Warner Cable and our landlord? DAN, NEW YORK CITY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ethical Theory of Relativity', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Ethical Theory of Relativity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landlords', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/e666dfe0-1527-5288-a5b3-2709d28242b8', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e666dfe0-1527-5288-a5b3-2709d28242b8'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/the-3-31-13-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IS GIVING THE SECRET TO GETTING AHEAD?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 3.31.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 3.31.13 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f72bc480-65e9-5f7b-874c-8fc8b7e851f6', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f72bc480-65e9-5f7b-874c-8fc8b7e851f6'}, {'abstract': 'Creating a kiss-proof formula.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/who-made-that-lipstick.html', 'snippet': 'Creating a kiss-proof formula.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1940s, lipstick tended to fade and smear. Women were constantly checking their teeth for red marks and repainting their mouths. Hazel Bishop — a biochemist with a background in petroleum science — set out to solve this problem. Using the tiny kitchen of her Central Park West apartment as a lab, she cooked potions in a double boiler and compounded dyes on a countertop. Sometimes so much dust escaped from her experiments that the butter in her refrigerator turned red. Lipstick had existed for centuries, of course, but Bishop hoped to create a long-lasting version for the modern woman, one that would stand up to the friction of coffee cups, cigarettes and kisses. She hit on a formula made with lanolin and dyes that penetrated deep into the skin. To demonstrate its sticking power, she persuaded her neighborhood druggist to decorate his hand with streaks of her lipstick. As he rang up purchases, the red stripes stayed bright.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/14wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/14wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/14wmt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/14wmt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1179, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Lipstick?', 'kicker': 'Innovation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Lipstick)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Pagan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bishop, Hazel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central Park (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5d8ba50-e103-58a6-8ead-a2485bd01700', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5d8ba50-e103-58a6-8ead-a2485bd01700'}, {'abstract': 'Maria Schneider blends big-band jazz, poetry and chamber music. It’s not as easy as it sounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/how-maria-schneider-reinvented-the-classical-sound.html', 'snippet': 'Maria Schneider blends big-band jazz, poetry and chamber music. It’s not as easy as it sounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago the jazz composer Maria Schneider traveled back to her hometown, Windom, Minn. She climbed a ladder with a childhood friend named Tony Thompson and found herself at the top of a silo. She looked out over the fields: acres of Thompson’s bean plants moving over and past one another in the breeze. Windom, population 4,646, is the seat of Cottonwood County, near the state’s southwest corner. The Des Moines River flows leisurely through it. The land is smooth prairie, in all directions. “The mistake people make,” Schneider told me about this flat place she came from, “is thinking that it’s flat.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/14schneider-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/14schneider-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/14schneider-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/14schneider-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1364, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/mag-14Schneider-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/mag-14Schneider-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14schneider/mag-14Schneider-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prairie Jazz Companion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prairie Jazz Companion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneider, Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T15:16:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1db32ef4-ea0e-5493-a53e-e44bf3e7f958', 'word_count': 2907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1db32ef4-ea0e-5493-a53e-e44bf3e7f958'}, {'abstract': 'Why she chose a life without sex and will not pooh-pooh a link between vaccines and autism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/magazine/temple-grandin-on-autism-death-celibacy-and-cows.html', 'snippet': 'Why she chose a life without sex and will not pooh-pooh a link between vaccines and autism.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In your new book, “The Autistic Brain,” you seriously entertain possible links between vaccines and autism in children, links that scientists have vehemently dismissed.  Well, there’s only one vaccine that could possibly be a problem, and that’s the measles-mumps-rubella vaccine. Now that they’ve changed the vaccine, it has fewer antigens, and that would make it a lot safer. But with the old version of the vaccine, I have not yet come across a study that looked at regressives — when a child had some speech but lost it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14talk/mag-14Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 407, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14talk/mag-14Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1475, 'width': 586, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14talk/14talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14talk/14talk-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/magazine/14talk/14talk-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Temple Grandin on Autism, Death, Celibacy and Cows', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "‘Cats Will Kill You First, But Wolves Just Rip You Open'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldman, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Temple', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cattle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'valu