 into a rhythm of acquiring just the right amount, which I suppose is because what might have worked last week is either too much or not enough this week. And so it goes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yes, We Have Ripe Bananas', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bananas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T19:40:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f242fb9a-d193-5067-ab92-e33c46af745c', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f242fb9a-d193-5067-ab92-e33c46af745c'}, {'abstract': 'When we view a work of art, we add our own brush strokes of memory — finishing a creative process that, until then, is incomplete.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/opinion/sunday/what-the-brain-can-tell-us-about-art.html', 'snippet': 'When we view a work of art, we add our own brush strokes of memory — finishing a creative process that, until then, is incomplete.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIS month, President Obama unveiled a breathtakingly ambitious initiative to map the human brain, the ultimate goal of which is to understand the workings of the human mind in biological terms.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/sunday-review/14GRAYMATTER/14GRAYMATTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/sunday-review/14GRAYMATTE