ubject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:33:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Amy O'Leary", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Leary", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99ba4391-dad3-5e5b-9933-54697ae7013b', 'word_count': 2258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99ba4391-dad3-5e5b-9933-54697ae7013b'}, {'abstract': 'The federal government announced that borrowers with home loans insured by the Federal Housing Administration could suspend mortgage payments for up to one year while they repair their homes.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/a-mortgage-holiday-for-some-borrowers-hurt-by-hurricane/', 'snippet': 'The federal government announced that borrowers with home loans insured by the Federal Housing Administration could suspend mortgage payments for up to one year while they repair their homes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thousands of homeowners whose properties were damaged by Hurricane Sandy\xa0 and cannot make their mortgage payments have gotten another reprieve — \xa0the federal government announced Friday that borrowers\xa0 with home loans insured by the Federal Housing Administration could suspend mortgage payments for up to one year while they repair their homes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mortgage Holiday for Some Borrowers Hurt by Hurricane', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donovan, Shaun L S (1966- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp (Freddie Mac)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Housing Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Assn (Fannie Mae)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63b0eef9-0207-5ef7-b1e8-2803065cad0d', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63b0eef9-0207-5ef7-b1e8-2803065cad0d'}, {'abstract': 'Once a scruffy bar, the River Grille, in Chatham, has changed its mood and menu under new owners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/14/nyregion/a-review-of-the-river-grille-in-chatham-nj.html', 'snippet': 'Once a scruffy bar, the River Grille, in Chatham, has changed its mood and menu under new owners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he was a boy, back in the 1970s, Michael Pryer often accompanied his father to a bar called Flynn’s, in Chatham. Over the years, he mused about turning the scruffy bar into a place where customers could order more than just a liverwurst or Taylor ham, egg and cheese sandwich — the only items the kitchen served back then, Mr. Pryer, 47, recalled over the telephone after my visits.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 418, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 418}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1426, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/14/nyregion/14DINENJ2/14DINENJ2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonhomie, Even if They Don’t Know Your Name', 'kicker': 'New Jersey Dining | Chatham', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bonhomie, Even If They Don’t Know Your Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chatham (NJ)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'River Grille (Chatham, NJ, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:36:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fran Schumer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fran', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schumer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd079c5a-7c0b-5e7d-a05f-ef11d946b615', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd079c5a-7c0b-5e7d-a05f-ef11d946b615'}, {'abstract': 'In this issue we feature images that we hope open your eyes to new ideas of taste and beauty as they have ours.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/the-taste-divide/', 'snippet': 'In this issue we feature images that we hope open your eyes to new ideas of taste and beauty as they have ours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How does an actress design her image? In our celebrity-obsessed culture, she usually dresses in the very latest fashion and has her photographs airbrushed into superhuman oblivion. Julianne Moore, while she indeed looks ravishing in films and on the red carpet, and even in her sweatpants at morning drop off (where I see her from time to time as our children attend the same school), is not such an actress. She is someone who rather pushes the boundaries in her acting in order to inhabit the characters she plays. For this issue she collaborated with the photographers Inez and Vinoodh and the makeup wizard Peter Philips, all artists in their own right, who seek to extend our way of seeing. Here they show us a more real, and even exaggerated side of Moore’s natural beauty. Freckled from birth (and not in a cute way, she explains in the piece), Moore’s face was sprinkled by Philips with even more spots. The resulting image, unlike most cover shots, isn’t retouched to perfection. The power comes from Moore’s striking and honest intensity, and from her scarcely made-up face, remarkable and beautiful at age 52.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Taste Divide', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Taste Divide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:37:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Needleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Needleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bc11e9a-d576-551b-a203-f35c83a3001d', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bc11e9a-d576-551b-a203-f35c83a3001d'}, {'abstract': 'The 33-year-old public economist answers questions about how he chooses his research topics and what he’s working on now.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/a-chat-with-raj-chetty-clark-medalist/', 'snippet': 'The 33-year-old public economist answers questions about how he chooses his research topics and what he’s working on now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This afternoon, we caught up with Raj Chetty, the 33-year-old Harvard economist who is the latest winner of the John Bates Clark Medal, a prestigious award for economists under the age of 40. Mr. Chetty is a public economist whose work has addressed topics including the returns from education, tax policies and savings behavior, often by parsing huge troves of data. This interview was lightly edited for clarity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Chat With Raj Chetty, Clark Medalist', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chetty, Raj', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:37:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de2feba5-94a3-5ffc-bbec-23383b04b827', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de2feba5-94a3-5ffc-bbec-23383b04b827'}, {'abstract': 'Bucks readers tell of their experiences in seeking a mortgage.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/qualifying-for-a-mortgage/', 'snippet': 'Bucks readers tell of their experiences in seeking a mortgage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You probably know someone who has a good story to tell about how difficult it was to get a mortgage in the wake of the housing crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Qualifying for a Mortgage', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-12T22:39:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12e53dfb-f295-5d52-a2a8-150aafe92001', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12e53dfb-f295-5d52-a2a8-150aafe92001'}, {'abstract': 'The problem with mobile payments is it’s usually no more convenient to pay with a phone instead of cash or credit card. A recent trip to Chipotle Mexican Grill was a rare example where paying with a phone actually sped things up.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/12/test-run-what-mobile-payments-companies-can-learn-from-chipotle/', 'snippet': 'The problem with mobile payments is it’s usually no more convenient to pay with a phone instead of cash or credit card. A recent trip to Chipotle Mexican Grill was a rare example where paying with a phone actually sped things up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lingering problem with mobile payments is that it’s usually no more convenient to pay with a phone instead of cash or credit card. Square’s wallet, for example, a feature that promises to let you pay simply by saying your name at the register of participating businesses, is clunky and has rarely worked when we have tried.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/13/business/13bits-app2/13bits-app2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/13/business/13bits-app2/13bits-app2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 494}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/13/business/13bits-app2/13bits-app2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 605, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/13/business/13bits-app2/13bits-app2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 605, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/13/business/13bits-app2/13bits-app2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard