pera that is returning at the Met.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Die Walküre” returned to the Metropolitan Opera on Saturday, and the machine worked without noticeable hitches. Yes, that machine, the 45-ton set consisting of 24 rotating planks that dominates this production from the director Robert Lepage. These days an element of tension hovers over each performance from Wagner’s epic cycle, back for the first run this season at the Met, because you never know when the machine might malfunction, as happened on April 6 during “Das Rheingold.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1240, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/15/arts/15wagner/15wagner-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 