'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T01:06:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hania Mourtada and Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hania', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mourtada', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f38ea8f2-6d5f-5d0d-b838-30138ea4646e', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f38ea8f2-6d5f-5d0d-b838-30138ea4646e'}, {'abstract': 'It is a mistake to view King as merely an ardent proponent of the American dream.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/opinion/dr-kings-righteous-fury.html', 'snippet': 'It is a mistake to view King as merely an ardent proponent of the American dream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHRIS ROCK caused a stir last Fourth of July when he tweeted, “Happy white peoples independence day the slaves weren’t free but I’m sure they enjoyed fireworks.” Mr. Rock’s tweet may not have topped the Rev. Jeremiah A. Wright Jr.’s “God damn America” sermon, but both sentiments are of a piece, and both seem a far cry from the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.’s appeal to the American dream and his embrace of “the magnificent words of the Constitution and the Declaration of Independence.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/opinion/0416OPEDmunday/0416OPEDmunday-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/16/opinion/0416OPE