4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maduro, Nicolas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T13:21:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b258a8c1-1c5d-5756-8258-35bdf19ec773', 'word_count': 10, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b258a8c1-1c5d-5756-8258-35bdf19ec773'}, {'abstract': 'After tough negotiations, a group of eight senators planned to introduce a proposal on Tuesday that contains a 13-year pathway to citizenship for immigrants here illegally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/us/senators-set-to-unveil-immigration-bill.html', 'snippet': 'After tough negotiations, a group of eight senators planned to introduce a proposal on Tuesday that contains a 13-year pathway to citizenship for immigrants here illegally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A sweeping immigration bill that a bipartisan group of eight senators completed on Tuesday seeks not only to fix chronic problems in the system and bring an estimated 11 million illegal immigrants to the right side of the law. It would also reorient future immigration with the goal of bringing foreigners to the country based increasingly on the job skills and personal assets they can offer.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beside a Path to Citizenship, a New Path on Immigration', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beside a Path to Citizenship, a New Course for Immigration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Preston, Julia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizenship and Naturalization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Workers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T13:46:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/62d7e503-9c1d-563f-b6ab-5d29d157029c', 'word_count': 1298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62d7e503-9c1d-563f-b6ab-5d29d157029c'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/what-were-reading-672/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saveur: “In 17th-century Russia, rhubarb was so valuable that much of the country’s treasury was derived from the medicinal sales of the imported rhubarb root,” Dara Moskowitz Grumdahl writes. “When Chinese emperors and Russian czars had a border dispute, withholding rhubarb was China’s biggest weapon.” Playing diplomatic hardball with pie filling? Who knew? — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T13:58:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04184dbf-815d-52cf-a2d9-a987f636e01f', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04184dbf-815d-52cf-a2d9-a987f636e01f'}, {'abstract': 'The actor sings the number “Come Rain or Come Shine” in a scene from the play about the Stonewall uprising.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/in-performance-nathan-lee-graham-of-hit-the-wall/', 'snippet': 'The actor sings the number “Come Rain or Come Shine” in a scene from the play about the Stonewall uprising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ike Holter’s play “Hit the Wall” unfolds over the course of one night during the 1969 Stonewall riots that helped spark the modern gay rights movement. In this scene the actor Nathan Lee Graham, who plays Carson, a drag queen, sings the Harold Arlen-Johnny Mercer song “Come Rain or Come Shine.” The show continues through April 28 at the Barrow Street Theater in Greenwich Village, near where the uprising took place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Nathan Lee Graham of ‘Hit the Wall’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stonewall Riots (1969)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T14:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/754085ed-87c1-5c06-854b-dee1dc23b017', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/754085ed-87c1-5c06-854b-dee1dc23b017'}, {'abstract': 'There was heartache at the Boston Marathon on Monday, and also lessons in a community trying to rally its strength.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/sports/in-boston-tragedy-and-triumph-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'There was heartache at the Boston Marathon on Monday, and also lessons in a community trying to rally its strength.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Acts of terror typically rip apart ordinary days, turn routine trips to work or school into pits of pain and heartache. That is what made the shock of Monday’s blasts at the Boston Marathon finish line so different. Patriots’ Day in Boston is no ordinary day. It’s a day to celebrate America’s essential character. The marathon itself is a stream of celebration, a parade of people defying limits, championing their determination and resolve. And then two bombs went off and rocked it all. The shock waves did not stop before reaching every last bit of our country, and beyond.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17leadingpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17leadingpic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1383, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17leadingpic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/17leadingpic/17lea