s of more than 7,000 samples turned up horse DNA in about 5 percent of samples and traces of an equine painkiller not approved for human consumption.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/business/global/european-study-affirms-role-of-fraud-in-horsemeat-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'Analyses of more than 7,000 samples turned up horse DNA in about 5 percent of samples and traces of an equine painkiller not approved for human consumption.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Two months after a horse meat scandal first gripped Europe, tests conducted by European Union countries showed on Tuesday that France had found the most beef products containing horse DNA, while Britain had detected the most traces of a painkiller banned from the human food chain.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/business/17-HORSE/17-HORSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe Says Tests Show Horse Meat Scandal Is ‘Food Fraud’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europe Says Tests Show Horse Meat Scandal Is ‘Food Fraud’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castle, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Product Tests', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterinary Medicine', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T19:54:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b335b1ae-c9b7-54bc-91d2-c2a5fc25fc67', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b335b1ae-c9b7-54bc-91d2-c2a5fc25fc67'}, {'abstract': 'In Mr. Lytal’s novel, a young man from Oklahoma is torn between his hometown and New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/returning-home-benjamin-lytal-talks-about-a-map-of-tulsa/', 'snippet': 'In Mr. Lytal’s novel, a young man from Oklahoma is torn between his hometown and New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benjamin Lytal’s novel “A Map of Tulsa” is the story of Jim Praley, a young man back in his hometown for a summer during college who falls in love with an artist named Adrienne. Five years later, living in New York, Jim returns to Tulsa when he learns that Adrienne has been injured in an accident. In The New York Times Book Review, Gary Sernovitz writes, “Lytal asks the essential questions: how to be good; how to be an adult; how to live outside one’s head; how to love unselfishly; how to understand if this girl, this town — any of it, anything at all — are indispensable, and if they’re meaningful enough to turn into art.” In a recent e-mail interview, Mr. Lytal discussed the elements of autobiography in his book, what he’s learned from being a book critic and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/books/16lytal-2/16lytal-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Returning Home: Benjamin Lytal Talks About ‘A Map of Tulsa’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lytal, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-16T20:04:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f559a162-dffb-5897-8baf-ce0ff90567c7', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f559a162-dffb-5897-8baf-ce0ff90567c7'}, {'abstract': 'Coors Field employees — and Mets General Manager Sandy Alderson — helped remove snow from the ballpark before a doubleheader scheduled for Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/in-denver-no-business-like-snow-business/', 'snippet': 'Coors Field employees — and Mets General Manager Sandy Alderson — helped remove snow from the b