ligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Constitution Project, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hutchinson, Asa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, James R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T01:17:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/37fc9bdb-b3f5-5be3-976d-b38f7e8edc8a', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37fc9bdb-b3f5-5be3-976d-b38f7e8edc8a'}, {'abstract': 'The University of Oregon agreed that violations occurred in its football program, but the N.C.A.A.’s unwieldy enforcement process offers little disincentive for any school to cheat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/sports/ncaafootball/ncaas-case-against-oregon-moves-slowly.html', 'snippet': 'The University of Oregon agreed that violations occurred in its football program, but the N.C.A.A.’s unwieldy enforcement process offers little disincentive for any school to cheat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news this week came from the University of Oregon, but it could have come from anywhere. Two local outlets, the newspaper The Oregonian and the television station KATU, obtained a report through public records that detailed the interplay between Oregon’s athletic department and N.C.A.A. investigators.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/OREGON/OREGON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/17/sports/OREGON/OREGON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600,