e': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T04:28:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/723ba998-cb06-57ff-8472-8feb92d931cf', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/723ba998-cb06-57ff-8472-8feb92d931cf'}, {'abstract': 'Seven Florida teachers have brought a federal lawsuit to protest job evaluation policies that tether individual performance ratings to the test scores of students who are not even in their classes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/education/florida-teachers-sue-over-evaluation-system.html', 'snippet': 'Seven Florida teachers have brought a federal lawsuit to protest job evaluation policies that tether individual performance ratings to the test scores of students who are not even in their classes.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Seven Florida teachers have brought a federal lawsuit to protest job evaluation policies that tether individual performance ratings to the test scores of students who are not even in their classes. The suit, which was filed Tuesday in conjunction with three local affiliates of the National Education Association in Federal District Court for the Northern District of Florida in Gainesville, says Florida’s two-year-old evaluation system violates teachers’ rights of due process and equal protection. Under a 2011 law, schools and districts must evaluate teachers in part based on how much their students learn, as measured by standardized tests. But since Florida, like most states, administers only math and reading tests and only in selected grades, many teachers do not teach tested subjects. One of the plaintiffs, a first-grade teacher, was rated on the basis of test scores of students in a different school in her district, and another, who teaches vocational classes to aspiring health care workers, was rated based on test scores of students in grades and subjects she had never taught. “This lawsuit highlights the absurdity of the current evaluation system,” said Andy Ford, president of the Florida Education Association. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Florida: Teachers Sue Over Evaluation System', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Florida: Teachers Sue Over Evaluation System', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Evaluations (Labor)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Education Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gainesville (Fla)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T04:47:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3444801-c4c7-5a36-aee9-18408e2cd9f2', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3444801-c4c7-5a36-aee9-18408e2cd9f2'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (13, 49) THE MYSTERY OF EELS Snakelike and slimy, with a row of jagged teeth and, in some cases, a life span of more than 100 years, the eel is one of the natural world’s few enigmas, with little known about where it goes, what it does and how it dies. In this “Nature” episode the naturalist James Prosek tries to shed light on this creature, above, christened “the North Sea siren” and “love’s arrow on earth” by the Italian poet Eugenio Montale. In “Australia’s First 4 Billion Years: Life Explodes,” on “Nova” at 9, Richard Smith investigates fossils for clues as to how arthropods invaded that barren continent from the ocean and spawned life on land. And “Guts With Michael Mosley,” at 10, explores the human digestive tract.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T05:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/b66a69d3-5d0f-56c2-919e-3b3ce35f4962', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b66a69d3-5d0f-56c2-919e-3b3ce35f4962'}, {'abstract': 'Besides Charles E. Schumer of New York, a Democrat, and John McCain of Arizona, a Republican, three other Democrats and three other Republicans were part of the group that shaped the new immigration bill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/04/17/us/politics/17senatebox.html', 'snippet': 'Besides Charl