dline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T17:47:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad80f97c-95ad-5ecb-b677-640f8f71de03', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad80f97c-95ad-5ecb-b677-640f8f71de03'}, {'abstract': 'A shoe designer known for creating platforms for the glam rock generation, opens a pop-up boutique in London.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/terry-de-havilland/', 'snippet': 'A shoe designer known for creating platforms for the glam rock generation, opens a pop-up boutique in London.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A lot of my stuff terrifies buyers,” Terry de Havilland\xa0says, chuckling. A desire to show the shoe-buying public what they’re missing spurred the designer, who made some of the most memorable footwear of the 1970s, to open a three-month-long pop-up boutique in London’s Carnaby Street area beginning on April 20. It’s his first stand-alone retail venture since his Kings Road shop, Cobblers to the World — where Led Zeppelin, Anita Pallenberg and Bianca Jagger bought their platforms — closed in 1979. Though he makes other styles, including a fetching Cuban-heeled boot for men, de Havilland will forever be associated with the platform, which he learned to make at his father’s knee; his parents had a shoe business that catered to showgirls in postwar London, and it was those original designs that prompted him to resuscitate the platform for the glam rock generation and again more recently for customers like Kate Moss. “I think they empower women,” he says. “They give you your own little stage to stand on.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Terry de Havilland', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T18:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ede0f675-25b7-5254-a534-aa295d895f3f', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ede0f675-25b7-5254-a534-aa295d895f3f'}, {'abstract': 'Three possible explanations for the relatively rarity of domestic terror campaigns.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/why-so-little-terrorism/', 'snippet': 'Three possible explanations for the relatively rarity of domestic terror campaigns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With ricin-laced letters suddenly showing up in Washington D.C., I worry a little that I’m tempting fate by writing about the rarity of organized terrorism in early 21st century America. But hopefully one more post won’t hurt. So, picking up where my last one left off, here are three theories about why our culture seems to be producing relatively few people with the combination of ideological commitment, organizational competence and zeal that has sparked and sustained terrorist campaigns in other times and places:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why So Little Terrorism?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 