middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/39fda79c-785f-5e6e-b523-35b252c237f0', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39fda79c-785f-5e6e-b523-35b252c237f0'}, {'abstract': 'Amaryllis Knight opens ALTAI, a new retail store and online platform for her consultancy and furniture studio in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/now-opening-altai/', 'snippet': 'Amaryllis Knight opens ALTAI, a new retail store and online platform for her consultancy and furniture studio in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amaryllis Knight, a globe-trotting, British-born designer, has left her aesthetic fingerprints all over her adopted home of Los Angeles: in the homes, gardens and offices of private design clients; in her own house; and soon, in the smartly appointed restaurant that chef Josef Centeno is opening this summer (Orsa & Winston). Now, with the opening of Altai — the retail store (which opens April 18) and online platform for her consultancy and furniture studio — Knight’s eclectic vision is, at last, shoppable. “It’s a lot more than a store,” she says. “It’s a way to further explore my work and interests while contributing to a conversation in Los Angeles about varied worlds of design and how they intersect.” Situated on a stretch of 3rd Street that’s a retail desert — but a quick skip to LACMA and Regen Projects — the light-drenched, vaulted-ceilinged space sells everything from $14 Santa Maria Novella lozenges to a $600,000 1948 Vincent 1000cc Rapide Series B motorcycle with a legendary racing pedigree. (“It’s a wild machine,” Knight says.)', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Opening | Altai', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heavy Shopping', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T21:19:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36e6cb31-9077-5d62-b89e-bd6f0f50b78e', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36e6cb31-9077-5d62-b89e-bd6f0f50b78e'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen A. Feinberg, owner of Cerberus, is said to be considering buying a gun maker that the firm promised to sell in the wake of the slaying in Newtown, Conn.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/feinberg-of-cerberus-considers-bid-for-its-gun-maker/', 'snippet': 'Stephen A. Feinberg, owner of Cerberus, is said to be considering buying a gun maker that the firm promised to sell in the wake of the slaying in Newtown, Conn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four months after the investment firm Cerberus put the country’s largest gun company up for sale in the wake of the school shootings in Connecticut, it has found a possible buyer in Cerberus’s owner, Stephen A. Feinberg.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cerberus Owner Might Buy Its Gun-Making Group', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cerberus Owner Might Buy Its Gun-Making Group ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinberg, Stephen A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cerberus Capital Management', '