wsgaming”: projects at the intersection of games and journalism. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Lab at The New York Times', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T21:40:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marci Windsheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marci', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Windsheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69f3d6b9-3a45-5e06-9dca-08102e8533f2', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69f3d6b9-3a45-5e06-9dca-08102e8533f2'}, {'abstract': 'Jean-Claude Mas, of Poly Implants Prothèses, is accused of selling hundreds of thousands of defective implants to more than 65 countries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/18/world/europe/company-founder-makes-court-appearance-in-breast-implant-case.html', 'snippet': 'Jean-Claude Mas, of Poly Implants Prothèses, is accused of selling hundreds of thousands of defective implants to more than 65 countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The founder of a French company accused of selling hundreds of thousands of defective breast implants to more than 65 countries appeared on Wednesday in a courthouse in Marseille, with more than 300 victims in attendance.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Company Founder Makes Court Appearance in Breast-Implant Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France: Trial on Flawed Implants Opens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Implants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erlanger, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breasts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defective Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mas, Jean-Claude', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Poly Implants Protheses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marseille (France)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T21:41:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maïa de la Baume', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maïa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la Baume', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/059006ec-ef56-53ce-bfbf-6ade929ce77a', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/059006ec-ef56-53ce-bfbf-6ade929ce77a'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s briefs: finding philosophy in H.P. Lovecraft, a philosophy teacher leaves prison, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/17/the-weird-realism-of-h-p-lovecraft/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s briefs: finding philosophy in H.P. Lovecraft, a philosophy teacher leaves prison, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Stone’s weekly briefing of notable philosophy-related issues and ideas from around the Web.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘Weird Realism’ of H.P. Lovecraft', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-17T21:42:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.c. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c70990e9-351e-5b09-8c37-e1dd8b50717d', 'word_count': 389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c70990e9-351e-5b09-8c37-e1dd8b50717d'}, {'abstract': 'Brooks Brothers creates a capsule collection for “The Great Gatsby” premiere; Holst + Lee joins Plum Alley, a new luxury e-commerce platform and other shopping news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/18/fashion/scouting-report.html', 'snippet': 'Brooks Brothers creates a capsule collection for “The Great Gatsby” premiere; Holst + Lee joins Plum Alley, a new luxury e-commerce platform and other shopping news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn-based jewelry line Holst + Lee, created by Natalie Holst and Rochelle Lee, is the latest addition to the roster at Plum Alley, a new luxury e-commerce platform that curates products exclusively from companies founded by women. In honor of the occasion, Ms. Holst and Ms. Lee have designed three pieces exclusively for the site: glass crystal and nylon rope earrings ($175), a Swarovski crystal-studded necklace ($275) and a multistrand necklace made from nylon rope wrapped with pearl cotton thread ($225). This week, 10 percent of the proceeds on these items will go to Somaly Mam Foundation, an organization that works to end human trafficking.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/18/fashion/18SCOUT2/18SCOUT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/18/fashion/18SCOUT2/18SCOUT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/18/fashion/18SCOUT2/18SCOUT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType':