tion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e3c07ec-a159-5dca-9a4a-89ece5ce5dc9', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e3c07ec-a159-5dca-9a4a-89ece5ce5dc9'}, {'abstract': 'Greece’s economic free fall is reshaping the lives of families with children, many of whom arrive at school hungry, underfed or even malnourished.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/18/world/europe/more-children-in-greece-start-to-go-hungry.html', 'snippet': 'Greece’s economic free fall is reshaping the lives of families with children, many of whom arrive at school hungry, underfed or even malnourished.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATHENS — As an elementary school principal, Leonidas Nikas is used to seeing children play, laugh and dream about the future. But recently he has seen something altogether different, something he thought was impossible in Greece: children picking through school trash cans for food; needy youngsters asking playmates for leftovers; and an 11-year-old boy, Pantelis Petrakis, bent over with hunger pains.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/18/world/jp-greece1/jp-greece1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/18/world/jp-greece1/jp-greece1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/18/world/jp-greece1/jp-greece1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/18/world/jp-greece1/jp-greece1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {