'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37edc4aa-a910-51c3-8615-eef2f9dbbcd7', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37edc4aa-a910-51c3-8615-eef2f9dbbcd7'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/18/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:30 P.M. (FX) SALT (2010) Who is Salt? In this action extravaganza from Phillip Noyce, Evelyn Salt (Angelina Jolie, above) is a Russian mole or a C.I.A. assassin, or maybe both. Shedding her shoes and her underwear, she goes on the run from suspicious colleagues (Liev Schreiber and Chiwetel Ejiofor) after she is accused of being a double agent by a Russian defector. What follows is a symphony of ingenious fights and escapes as she tries to prove her innocence. “Perhaps the most ridiculous scene in ‘Salt’ has Ms. Jolie walking away unnoticed from the aftermath of a multivehicle smash-up,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “I suspect this was meant as a joke, since her magnetism is the film’s foundation and reason for being (even though her role was originally conceived as a vehicle for Tom Cruise). She is the prime special effect, and a reminder that even in an era of technological overkill, movie stars matter.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-18T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported