ne, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 919, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 919}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 669, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1338, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-Upfront/0421-bks-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-18T20:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e7f0d40-197c-5a8a-8916-cb8eabd0898c', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e7f0d40-197c-5a8a-8916-cb8eabd0898c'}, {'abstract': 'Three new books offer different takes on the Windy City, from personal and historical perspectives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/the-third-coast-by-thomas-dyja-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three new books offer different takes on the Windy City, from personal and historical perspectives.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Poor Chicago,” a friend of mine recently said. Given the number of urban apocalypses here, I couldn’t tell which problem she was referring to. Was it the Cubs never winning? The abominable weather? Meter parking costing more than anywhere else in America — up to $6.50 an hour — with the money flowing to a private company, thanks to the ex-mayor Richard M. Daley’s shortsighted 2008 deal? Or was it the fact that in 2012, of the largest American cities, Chicago had the second-highest murder rate and the \xadsecond-highest combined sales tax, as well as the ninth-highest metro foreclosure rate in the country? That it’s the third-most racially segregated city and is located in the state with the most underfunded public-employee pension debt? Was my friend talking about how a real estate investor bought The Chicago Tribune and drove it into bankruptcy? Or how 15-year-old Hadiya Pendleton, who performed at the festivities for Barack Obama’s inauguration, was shot dead near the president’s Kenwood home?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHTIER/21SCHTIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHTIER/21SCHTIER-articleLarge.jpg', 