, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-18T22:12:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ce48616-091b-5581-9036-9d05eae43ec7', 'word_count': 4748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ce48616-091b-5581-9036-9d05eae43ec7'}, {'abstract': 'In “Oblivion,” Tom Cruise is a repairman on a post-apocalyptic Earth who finds himself confronting a stranger-than-usual work order.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/movies/oblivion-with-tom-cruise.html', 'snippet': 'In “Oblivion,” Tom Cruise is a repairman on a post-apocalyptic Earth who finds himself confronting a stranger-than-usual work order.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If only it were less easy to laugh at “Oblivion,” a lackluster science-fiction adventure with Tom Cruise that, even before its opening, was groaning under the weight of its hard-working, slowly fading star and a title that invites mockery of him and it both. The agony of being a longtime Tom Cruise fan has always been a burden, but now it’s just, well, dispiriting. You not only have to ignore the din of the tabloids and swat away the buzzing generated by his multiple headline-ready dramas, you also have to come to grips with the harsh truth that it no longer actually matters why and how Tom Terrific became less so. No one else much cares.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After the Apocalypse, Things Go Downhill', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After the Apocalypse, Things Go Downhill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_wo