t’s at.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Liberals are furious, but the gun issue will not significantly damage the Republican Party. Sure, it looks bad to oppose background checks, which have overwhelming popular support. Sure, the Republican position will further taint the party’s image in places like the suburbs of Philadelphia and Northern Virginia. Sure, the party looks extreme when it can’t accept a bill sponsored by the conservative Senator Joe Manchin and the very conservative Senator Pat Toomey.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Second Wave', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Second Wave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T00:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f525cc4-80f8-5e88-beff-447cb4f2506d', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f525cc4-80f8-5e88-beff-447cb4f2506d'}, {'abstract': 'China’s newest white paper on defense does not mention its “no-first-use” pledge in terms of nuclear weapons, as past versions have.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/opinion/is-china-changing-its-position-on-nuclear-weapons.html', 'snippet': 'China’s newest white paper on defense does not mention its “no-first-use” pledge in terms of nuclear weapons, as past versions have.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERPRETING any country’s pronouncements about its nuclear weapons can be a study in fine distinctions, but occasionally a state says — or fails to say — something in a clear break from the past. A Chinese white paper on defense, released on Tuesday, falls into this category and now demands our attention, because it omits a promise that China will never use nuclear weapons first. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is China Changing Its Position on Nuclear Weapons?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is China Changing Its Position on Nuclear Weapons?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T00:55:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By James M. Acton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f8042db-a887-5c1b-941f-c4749a6325c6', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f8042db-a887-5c1b-941f-c4749a6325c6'}, {'abstract': 'Inside 8 Bit and Up, cluttered shelves hold video games of yesterday, catering to the aficionados of 8- and 16-bit gaming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/nyregion/at-8-bit-and-up-the-enduring-romance-of-retro-video-games.html', 'snippet': 'Inside 8 Bit and Up, cluttered shelves hold video games of yesterday, catering to the aficionados of 8- and 16-bit gaming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Tarczon, a law student, recently visited St. Marks Place to indulge in a somewhat specific hobby. He climbed stairs to a small, discreetly located shop where he knew he would find what he was looking for: video games with great graphics and challenging gameplay — and compatibility with the extinct Nintendo Entertainment System. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21JOINT2/21JOINT2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Enduring Romance of Super Mario', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Enduring Romance of Super Mario', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vadukul, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'