ubject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T01:10:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28484916-2280-5846-a01a-1e4310840523', 'word_count': 1241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28484916-2280-5846-a01a-1e4310840523'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court was right to block a policy that allowed the police to order a blood test of a driver without obtaining a warrant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/opinion/the-constitution-and-blood-testing.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court was right to block a policy that allowed the police to order a blood test of a driver without obtaining a warrant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drunken driving kills someone every 53 minutes — 9,878 times in the United States in 2011. But the problem, however grave, should not be solved by policies that violate constitutional rights. The Supreme Court was correct when it ruled Wednesday that a Missouri policy requiring a blood test, even without a search warrant, of anyone arrested on charges of driving under the influence of alcohol violated the Constitution’s Fourth Amendment ban on unreasonable searches — unless circumstances demand immediate action and justify a warrantless test.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Constitution and Blood Testing', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Constitution and Blood Testing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Sobriety)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drunken and Reckless Driving', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T01:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d478efc9-cbf4-52fe-ba76-01905582348a', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d478efc9-cbf4-52fe-ba76-01905582348a'}, {'abstract': 'A town coping with tragedy assimilates the Senate vote against a gun control bill even as it grapples with holding a Labor Day parade and other attempts to return to normal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/nyregion/reality-encroaching-on-grief-in-newtown.html', 'snippet': 'A town coping with tragedy assimilates the Senate vote against a gun control bill even as it grapples with holding a Labor Day parade and other attempts to return to normal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWTOWN, Conn. — Mark Barden is a talented musician and composer who played in Nashville for years before settling in this bucolic New England town to raise his three children.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/19/nyregion/newtown-1/newtown-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/19/nyregion/newtown-1/newtown-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewid