'original': 'By Peter Applebome', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Applebome', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/970a493f-5d19-5dc9-b13a-e164ab30d144', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/970a493f-5d19-5dc9-b13a-e164ab30d144'}, {'abstract': 'The United States must not allow the government in Seoul to go down the road to uranium enrichment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/opinion/south-koreas-nuclear-ambitions.html', 'snippet': 'The United States must not allow the government in Seoul to go down the road to uranium enrichment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States and South Korea are negotiating a new nuclear cooperation agreement that will have consequences for the conflict with North Korea and President Obama’s broader nonproliferation agenda. As part of the deal, the South Korean government has requested American approval to enrich uranium and to reprocess nuclear fuel originally bought from the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toward a Nuclear Deal With South Korea', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Toward a Nuclear Deal With South Korea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T01:13:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4a26194-af5b-51e5-bdfd-4316474eea5a', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4a26194-af5b-51e5-bdfd-4316474eea5a'}, {'abstract': 'Districts should think twice before deploying more cops in schools because it might hurt students more than it helps them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/opinion/criminalizing-children-at-school.html', 'snippet': 'Districts should think twice before deploying more cops in schools because it might hurt students more than it helps them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The National Rifle Association and President Obama responded to the Newtown, Conn., shootings by recommending that more police officers be placed in the nation’s schools. But a growing body of research suggests that, contrary to popular wisdom, a larger police presence in schools generally does little to improve safety. It can also create a repressive environment in which children are arrested or issued summonses for minor misdeeds — like cutting class or talking back — that once would have been dealt with by the principal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Criminalizing Children at School', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Criminalizing Children at School', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Discipline (Students)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T01:13:50+0