lead_paragraph': 'Like many of us, Rebecca Bond was furious on Wednesday after the Senate voted down a series of far-from-radical measures—including universal background checks—that could have helped reduce gun violence, and might even have prevented future Newtowns. Bond, you may recall, is the co-founder, with her husband Jon, of a new organization, Evolve, that hopes to change the cultural norms around gun behavior, just as Mothers Against Drunk Driving changed the norms around drinking and driving. So she asked her friend Milton Glaser, one of the great men of American design, to craft a response. Here is what they came up with:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: April 19, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALBANY (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boise (Idaho)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bucks County (Pa)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisville (Ky)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phoenix (Ariz)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rialto (Calif)', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sanford (Fla)', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45268216-1290-5b51-b9ff-1e9ee70dd1b6', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45268216-1290-5b51-b9ff-1e9ee70dd1b6'}, {'abstract': 'Meg Wolitzer’s characters meet at an arts camp and stay connected as their life paths diverge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/the-interestings-by-meg-wolitzer.html', 'snippet': 'Meg Wolitzer’s characters meet at an arts camp and stay connected as their life paths diverge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That misanthropic wag H. L. Mencken once wrote that his definition of happiness included “a comfortable feeling of superiority to the masses of one’s fellow men” — something he suggested was more easily achieved in this country than elsewhere. And yet his quest to be exceptional (in which he inarguably succeeded) didn’t appear to make him all that happy, judging from his prodigious, grumbling output. But does the compulsion to excel make anybody happy? Or is it, rather, a prescription for disappointment in oneself and in the “circumscribed world”?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 813}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 756, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2025, 'width': 1494, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SCHILLINGER/21SCHILLINGER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Friends Forever', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Best Friends Forever', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:32:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0134b5e-3a0c-5d5d-b132-f8f440321524', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0134b5e-3a0c-5d5d-b132-f8f440321524'}, {'abstract': 'The life of a 19th-century public intellectual, writer and feminist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/margaret-fuller-by-megan-marshall.html', 'snippet': 'The life of a 19th-century public intellectual, writer and feminist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Margaret Fuller died on July 19, 1850, in a shipwreck off Fire Island. In her intellectual prime and at the height of her influence as a social reformer, she was returning home from Europe with her Italian husband and their child. A major advocate for the rights of women, Fuller had left the United States in 1846, just a year after the publication of her influential book, “Woman in the Nineteenth Century,” which both Susan B. Anthony and Elizabeth Cady Stanton cited as an inspiration. Having accomplished her mission, as Megan Marshall puts it in this new biography, “to meet the writers and radicals whose work she’d admired from afar and test their minds in conversation,” Fuller was coming home to the United States, having flagrantly acted out the freedoms she demanded as every woman’s right.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 783}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1771, 'width': 1357, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HARRISON/21HARRISON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vindication', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vindication', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marshall, Megan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Margaret', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:32:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/021f5f78-2857-5da1-9a21-cebfcc94a1ec', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/021f5f78-2857-5da1-9a21-cebfcc94a1ec'}, {'abstract': 'Blake Bailey traces the rise and fall of Charles Jackson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/farther-and-wilder-blake-baileys-bio-of-charles-jackson.html', 'snippet': 'Blake Bailey traces the rise and fall of Charles Jackson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Jackson, one of the most successful failures in American letters, wished as fiercely as any writer for literary immortality. This he was denied, though for a brief moment in the mid-1940s it seemed all but certain. His first novel, “The Lost Weekend,” sold nearly half a million copies within five years of its publication, was translated into 14 languages, inspired a hit film that swept the 1945 Academy Awards, and helped cause a major shift in public perceptions about alcoholism. After this taste of fame, Jackson’s career tumbled into a long decline, made more agonizing by periodic relapses of hope. Even before he died, in 1968, his reputation had blurred into obscurity. Today, except for vague memories of his connection to the classic movie, it has all but disappeared.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 612}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1004, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1722, 'width': 1687, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21RIFKIND/21RIFKIND-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lost Novelist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lost Novelist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bailey, Blake', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Charles (1903-68)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:33:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donna Rifkind', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rifkind', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7da934e9-ae84-54c7-8d40-0e0a6e6f8469', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7da934e9-ae84-54c7-8d40-0e0a6e6f8469'}, {'abstract': 'Clive James takes on a verse translation of Dante’s “Divine Comedy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/dantes-divine-comedy-translated-by-clive-james.html', 'snippet': 'Clive James takes on a verse translation of Dante’s “Divine Comedy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The perfect translation of Dante’s “Divine Comedy” remains one of literature’s holiest grails. Some translators have captured facets of the poem’s magic, but always at a cost: Charles Singleton conveys Dante’s erudition but flattens his poetry; John Ciardi recreates his music but takes mammoth liberties with the original; and John Sinclair’s “thees” and “thous” date his otherwise deft rendering. If the translator’s task is “to liberate the language imprisoned in a work,” as Walter Benjamin writes, then few literary strongholds come as heavily fortified as “The Divine Comedy.” Written in Dante’s native Tuscan instead of the more prestigious Latin, the poem and its earthy idiom, copious allusions and otherworldy precision burden translators, especially in rhyme-poor English, which struggles to match the momentum of Dante’s terza rima and internal rhymes. No wonder that Dante’s latest translator, the eminent Australian poet and critic Clive James, feared his task would be “thankless.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1926, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21LUZZI/21LUZZI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Could Be ‘Heaven,’ or This Could Be ‘Hell’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Could Be ‘Heaven,’ or This Could Be ‘Hell’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dante Alighieri (1265-1321)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Clive', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Luzzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Luzzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/22842663-e0b5-5246-a1cf-dafeaaacdaac', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22842663-e0b5-5246-a1cf-dafeaaacdaac'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/magazine/the-4-713-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 4.7.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reply All | The 4.7.13 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:33:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d15d3c2-a28e-5280-b53b-553922b6b3f9', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d15d3c2-a28e-5280-b53b-553922b6b3f9'}, {'abstract': 'It’s only April, but sportswriters began to analyze next season’s football matchups upon the release of the 2013 schedule.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/20/sports/obsessing-over-the-nfl-schedule.html', 'snippet': 'It’s only April, but sportswriters began to analyze next season’s football matchups upon the release of the 2013 schedule.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you are looking at sports as a mindless exercise, the relentless march of awful real-world news this week has put you in good company. You suddenly have people crowding around the virtual water cooler of the Internet, fascinated by the release of the N.F.L. schedule. The Ravens at Broncos to start the season? Holy cow, we could talk about that for weeks. In fact, let’s psychoanalyze the whole not-yet-played season just based on who is playing who when. Anyone have some tea leaves? Tarot cards, maybe? Ouija board?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obsessing Over the N.F.L. Schedule', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Lawrence', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griner, Brittney', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Lindsey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:34:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ef102d-3774-53da-99cd-ddbd0459bdc1', 'word_count': 683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ef102d-3774-53da-99cd-ddbd0459bdc1'}, {'abstract': 'A bloody valentine to the riotous ’90s in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/magazine/the-last-gasp-of-the-east-village.html', 'snippet': 'A bloody valentine to the riotous ’90s in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ’90s was not a golden era in New York City, by almost anybody’s reckoning. It had neither the nothing-left-to-lose freedom of the ’70s nor the everything-to-gain hedonism of the aughts. History will record it as a neutered, amorphous interregnum. But I can attest to the fact that the East Village was a good place to live then, filled with coffee shops, secondhand stores, Kim’s Video and lots of seedy bars. It still felt like downtown, and for a kid raised in the suburb known as the Upper East Side, there was nothing more I wanted from a neighborhood. One of my favorite places was a rock club called Brownies, on Avenue A, and that was largely because of a single night in 1996 that kind of represents the whole decade for me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21lives/21lives-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21lives/21lives-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21lives/21lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21lives/21lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21lives/21lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Last Gasp of the East Village', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Riotous ’90s: A bloody valentine to a scuffed-up New York decade.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lindgren, Hugo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Nineties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:34:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a6f60f4-cdbf-5271-bb80-6c6999a14daf', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a6f60f4-cdbf-5271-bb80-6c6999a14daf'}, {'abstract': 'In his late-career action movies, Tom Cruise manages to be the one thing he has never been in real life: Relatable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/magazine/learning-to-re-love-tom-cruise.html', 'snippet': 'In his late-career action movies, Tom Cruise manages to be the one thing he has never been in real life: Relatable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a scene in 2011’s “Ghost Protocol,” the most recent installment of the “Mission Impossible” franchise, in which Tom Cruise hangs from the side of Dubai’s Burj Khalifa, the world’s tallest building. He’s wearing special “Mission Impossible”-style magnetic gloves that allow him to stick to the building with his hands — if the gloves work properly! And Jeremy Renner has already warned him that if he doesn’t break into the server on the 130th floor soon, they’ll never be able to do, I don’t know, something important. (I have a hard time following the plots of action movies.)', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 86, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 86}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 147, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 293, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21riff/21riff-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning to (Re)Love Tom Cruise', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Jumping Out of Buildings,Less Jumping on Sofas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruise, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stunts and Stuntmen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:34:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taffy Brodesser-Akner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taffy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brodesser-Akner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85e590e7-2c60-5b27-ad4c-657224fc94f6', 'word_count': 1853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85e590e7-2c60-5b27-ad4c-657224fc94f6'}, {'abstract': 'And who would pay more than $70,000 for a miniature hot rod?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/magazine/who-made-those-hot-wheels.html', 'snippet': 'And who would pay more than $70,000 for a miniature hot rod?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Here was this car you always dreamed about, and you can buy it for a dollar,” says Larry Wood, who began designing Hot Wheels toys in 1969. Wood arrived at Mattel just a few years after Elliot Handler, the co-founder of the company, who died in 2011, decided to sell a miniature customized muscle car to kids. At that time, gearheads and backyard mechanics paraded around in hot rods that looked as if they’d come from a child’s fevered imagination. Painted in lollipop colors, the cars sprouted chrome-plated wings made out of exhaust pipes; they belched and roared and reared up on their haunches.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21wmt/21wmt-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made Those Hot Wheels?', 'kicker': 'Innovation'