me': 'subject', 'value': 'Chaplains', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coogan, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saltillo (Mexico)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:35:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1e1ca6e3-9b20-5733-a340-f1ef0a709722', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1e1ca6e3-9b20-5733-a340-f1ef0a709722'}, {'abstract': 'In a Mexican prison run by the country’s most feared criminal syndicate, a Brooklyn-born priest struggles to save the most hardened souls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/magazine/even-violent-drug-cartels-fear-god.html', 'snippet': 'In a Mexican prison run by the country’s most feared criminal syndicate, a Brooklyn-born priest struggles to save the most hardened souls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on a December morning, Robert Coogan pulled his red Chevy hatchback into the parking lot of the state prison in Saltillo, Mexico. It was frigid outside, the sun had not yet cleared the reddish mountains, and Coogan lingered, staring at the tall black letters on the prison’s high walls: “CERESO” — Centro de Reinserción Social, the place where criminals are supposed to be reformed. Coogan, who has served as chaplain at the prison for a decade, slowly pulled himself from the warm car. In dark jeans, brown boots and a thick gray sweater, he looked more like a factory foreman than a Brooklyn-born priest. He wore no clerical collar, just a necklace of pendants with images of the Virgin Mary and Christ on the cross.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/magazine/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7/21Mag-priest-slide-VRM7-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Even Violent Drug Cartels Fear God', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Salvation Army\r\nof\r\nOne', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coogan, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cave, Damien', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Cartels', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zetas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saltillo (Mexico)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chaplains', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:35:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90428bf1-03fa-5060-9f1f-2c05bfb5dc69', 'word_count': 5100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90428bf1-03fa-5060-9f1f-2c05bfb5dc69'}, {'abstract': 'Humanity’s experience has been marked by cooperation as much as conflict, a historian argues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/the-undivided-past-by-david-cannadine.html', 'snippet': 'Humanity’s experience has been marked by cooperation as much as conflict, a historian argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Undivided Past,” David Cannadine challenges those who believe that all history is the history of conflict, whether over class, as Marx and Engels proclaimed, or over religion, nationality, race, gender or civilization. The fact is, mankind’s divisions may not be the most important part of the story. As Cannadine succinctly puts it, “humanity is still here.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 651}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 944, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1529, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/0421-bks-WOLFE/0421-bks-WOLFE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Common Struggle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Common Struggle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolfe, Alan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cannadine, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:35:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Wolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6b97208-75d8-53cc-8a20-1e8b1b104754', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6b97208-75d8-53cc-8a20-1e8b1b104754'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Carson returns to the Greek myth she first explored in “Autobiography of Red.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/red-doc-by-anne-carson.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Carson returns to the Greek myth she first explored in “Autobiography of Red.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Carson has a history of doing unpredictable and genre-crossing things. A classical scholar who came late to poetry, she rose, in the ’90s and ’00s, quickly and deservedly, to prominence. Many readers (including me) first knew her through “The Glass Essay,” a 38-page multipart lyric narrative in 1995’s “Glass, Irony and God.” The poem is an inspired mash-up: a confessional-style “I” recounts a breakup with a lover and a visit to an aged mother while considering the life and writings of Emily Brontë and reporting on her surrealist visions of nudes. The yoking of disparates, the old and the new, continues to be a Carson strategy. She’s generally claimed by the poetry world, but her books often contain swaths of lyric, critical or essayistic prose, as well as translation, dramatic dialogue and visual art. Many toil in the interstices of genre; Carson’s palatable, popular, sophisticated and who-cares approach may have done the most and best work in the last two decades to stop people worrying so much about what’s poetry and what’s not. At her best, she’s among our most exciting poets. At her less-than-best she’s reliably ingenious, full of charisma and surprise. Lesser poets who behave more predictably and risk less are easier to praise — and not as important.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 690}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 891, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1782, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21FRIED/21FRIED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Other Labyrinths', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Other Labyrinths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Anne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Red Doc (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:36:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daisy Fried', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daisy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fried', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9553d98-7616-520c-b343-cdf7eb55ac75', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9553d98-7616-520c-b343-cdf7eb55ac75'}, {'abstract': 'Elizabeth Becker’s exposé considers the global travel industry’s growing toll on the planet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/overbooked-by-elizabeth-becker.html', 'snippet': 'Elizabeth Becker’s exposé considers the global travel industry’s growing toll on the planet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1980, armed with a backpack and a dog-eared copy of the Lonely Planet guidebook, I set out on a yearlong post-university trip through Asia. I hired a fishing boat to take me to a near-deserted island in the Gulf of Thailand and trekked for weeks through roadless villages in the Nepalese Himalayas. The highlight of the trip was a one-week tour of Myanmar, then almost completely cut off from the outside world. Three decades later, Jet Skiers and beach resorts have taken over my Thai island, and busloads of day-trippers flock to those Nepalese villages on a newly built trans-Himalayan highway. Even once-hermetic Myanmar is grabbing a piece of the global tourism trade; during the high season, the medieval temples of Bagan now face a crush of foreign invaders.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 524, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 524}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 895, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1790, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21HAMMMER/21HAMMMER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '100 Places to Visit Before They Die', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '100 Places to Visit Before They Die', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammer, Joshua', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Becker, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:36:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec495a4f-8c4f-5ddb-9d44-41f8972fcc0f', 'word_count': 1165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec495a4f-8c4f-5ddb-9d44-41f8972fcc0f'}, {'abstract': 'David Sheff’s message to policy makers and others is that drug addiction is primarily a health problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/clean-by-david-sheff.html', 'snippet': 'David Sheff’s message to policy makers and others is that drug addiction is primarily a health problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It must be the purest agony to be the parent of a child succumbing to drug addiction. David Sheff’s previous book was an account of his son Nic’s descent from a thoughtful boy to a sullen pothead to a self-destructive methamphetamine fiend, and of his own tormented and bewildered reaction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 708}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 868, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1231, 'width': 1043, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21SUSSMAN/21SUSSMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75,