 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:38:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/29dd145e-5a70-59cd-86cb-39846f3e140b', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29dd145e-5a70-59cd-86cb-39846f3e140b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'READING FOR MY LIFE: Writings, 1958-2008,  by John Leonard. Edited by Sue Leonard. (Penguin, $18.) These are exuberant essays and reviews by the cultural critic (and onetime Book Review editor), who died in 2008 — from his earliest columns for the Harvard Crimson to his final writings for The New York Review of Books. It includes reflections on Joan Didion, Don DeLillo and Toni Morrison, as well as tributes to Leonard by friends and colleagues.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:38:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/99652f4d-7cb3-51a5-b204-3cf75bd2a25c', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99652f4d-7cb3-51a5-b204-3cf75bd2a25c'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics on “Oblivion,” “The Lords of Salem” and “In the House.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/this-weeks-movies-oblivion-the-lords-of-salem-and-in-the-house/', 'snippet': 'Times critics on “Oblivion,” “The Lords of Salem” and “In the House.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s video includes reviews of “In the House,” “The Lords of Salem” and “Oblivion.” See all of this week’s reviews here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: ‘Oblivion,’ ‘The Lords of Salem’ and ‘In the House’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:38:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9fc0a10-44b2-521f-a772-d6d777154907', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9fc0a10-44b2-521f-a772-d6d777154907'}, {'abstract': 'Meg Wolitzer says looking at the cover of her novel “The Interestings,” at No. 11 on the hardcover fiction list, is “like walking into a painting done at summer camp by a very young Mark Rothko.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Meg Wolitzer says looking at the cover of her novel “The Interestings,” at No. 11 on the hardcover fiction list, is “like walking into a painting done at summer camp by a very young Mark Rothko.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROTHKO AT CAMP: The cover of Meg Wolitzer’s new novel, “The Interestings” — which hits the hardcover list at No. 11 — features bold lettering against a backdrop of bright stripes, like a TV test pattern from the disco era. What it does not feature, significantly, is any of the following: “Laundry hanging on a line. A little girl in a field of wildflowers. A pair of shoes on a beach. An empty swing on the porch of an old yellow house.” If that list sounds familiar, it’s because it appeared in the Book Review last year in Wolitzer’s essay “The Second Shelf,” about gender bias in publishing and the hurdles (including jacket art) facing any woman who hopes to be taken seriously as a literary novelist. Interviewing Wolitzer at Slate recently, her editor at Riverhead Books, Sarah McGrath, acknowledged treading carefully as a result: “We approached the art for this book with particular sensitivity,” she said, “and the art department was given a copy of your essay along with the manuscript. How did we do?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21list-img/21list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21list-img/21list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21list-img/21list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21list-img/21list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/books/review/21list-img/21list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'le