her new novel, “The Interestings”; Rachel Shteir discusses three new books about Chicago; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/04/19/books/review/21books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Meg Wolitzer talks about her new novel, “The Interestings”; Rachel Shteir discusses three new books about Chicago; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Meg Wolitzer talks about her new novel, “The Interestings”; Rachel Shteir discusses three new books about Chicago; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/92518487-f034-58c6-863e-93821e233a54', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/92518487-f034-58c6-863e-93821e233a54'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Martins is marking 30 years at the reins of New York City Ballet — a time of achievements, challenges and the shadows of Balanchine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/arts/dance/peter-martins-on-30-years-with-city-ballet.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Martins is marking 30 years at the reins of New York City Ballet — a time of achievements, challenges and the shadows of Balanchine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COPENHAGEN — On April 30, 1983, the co-founder and celebrated choreographer of New York City Ballet, George Balanchine, died. Balanchine was his form’s Picasso, the greatest choreographer of the 20th century, who changed the techniques and perceptions of his art. A successor seemed impossible, but a successor there had to be. It was Peter Martins, the tall, blond Danish dancer who had joined City Ballet 13 years earlier.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21MARTINS4_SPAN/21MARTINS4_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'City Ballet’s Leader, 30 Years In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Ballet’s Leader, 30 Years In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sulcas, Roslyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martins, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:54:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6be4db66-2612-543d-86b7-c72104960ff0', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6be4db66-2612-543d-86b7-c72104960ff0'}, {'abstract': 'Gabriel Kahane based his “Gabriel’s Guide to the 48 States” on writings from the Depression-era American Guide series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/arts/music/orpheus-to-play-kahanes-gabriels-guide-to-48-states.html', 'snippet': 'Gabriel Kahane based his “Gabriel’s Guide to the 48 States” on writings from the Depression-era American Guide series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there had been room in his camper, John Steinbeck once wrote, he would have packed all 48 volumes of the American Guide series. Compiled in the 1930s by the Federal Writers’ Project, part of the artistic wing of the Works Progress Administration, the guides provided state-by-state accounts of local history geared toward tourists in cars. In the middle of the Depression, the Writers’ Project retained more than 6,600 unemployed workers, ranging from up-and-coming luminaries like Saul Bellow and Richard Wright to schoolteachers and ditch diggers, who gathered information about their hometowns and spun it into legend.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21KAHANE1_SPAN/KAHANE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Traveling 48 States, by Orchestra', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Traveling 48 States, by Orchestra', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robin, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orpheus Chamber Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kahane, Gabriel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T14:56:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Robin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5f499ef-6dbc-5874-a2d8-5fafa75925e7', 'word_count': 1653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5f499ef-6dbc-5874-a2d8-5fafa75925e7'}, {'abstract': 'A midday stroll near the author’s office tells a story of a city defined by water.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/travel/quirks-of-amsterdam-revealed-during-lunch.html', 'snippet': 'A midday stroll near the author’s office tells a story of a city defined by water.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Haarlemmerstraat in Amsterdam is a narrow enough thoroughfare that from my office window I can easily see into the shops across the street. There is the olive oil boutique, with its rows of metal barrels and its sign inside saying “Check Your Oil,” and the coffee shop that young, nattily dressed tourists wander into to get licitly high. (Most visitors know that in Amsterdam a cafe is for coffee, and a coffee shop is for marijuana.) Looking up, I have to crane my neck to take in the succession of gable types on the brick facades — step, bell, spout — that signal the changing fashions among real estate developers during the city’s golden age in the 17th century. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/travel/21AMSTERDAM1_SPAN/21AMSTERDAM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/travel/21AMSTERDAM1_SPAN/21