13-04-19T21:21:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5acbf7a9-1177-5442-b3c4-9583147cdf3c', 'word_count': 32, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5acbf7a9-1177-5442-b3c4-9583147cdf3c'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Seimetz, who stars in the TV series “The Killing” and the movie “Upstream Color,” has written, produced, directed and coedited the noir “Sun Don’t Shine.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/movies/amy-seimetz-goes-behind-the-camera-with-sun-dont-shine.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Seimetz, who stars in the TV series “The Killing” and the movie “Upstream Color,” has written, produced, directed and coedited the noir “Sun Don’t Shine.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amy Seimetz has this recurring nightmare. She’s had it since she was a child. It’s a dream about guilt — maybe even about death. On a recent Sunday, on Skype, Ms. Seimetz described the dream. She was in Vancouver, British Columbia, where she’s been shooting new episodes of “The Killing,” and this was her first day off in a while. She ran her hands through her thick blond hair and began to explain.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1147, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/arts/21SEIMETZ1_SPAN/21SEIMETZ1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fulfilling Dreams and Nightmares', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fulfilling Dreams and Nightmares', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baron, Zach', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Seimetz, Amy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T21:22:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zach Baron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zach', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28a3614d-81cf-5e05-b387-7a940ff6038f', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28a3614d-81cf-5e05-b387-7a940ff6038f'}, {'abstract': 'Thoughts from Elizabeth Rubin on her 2001 magazine article on the conflict in Chechnya.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/19/revisiting-chechnya/', 'snippet': 'Thoughts from Elizabeth Rubin on her 2001 magazine article on the conflict in Chechnya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like everyone else, I woke this morning to news of the shootout and the manhunt in Boston, which was soon followed by the revelation that the two suspects were Chechen — or born to Chechen parents — and that they were raised, before coming to the United States, in Dagestan, which borders Chechnya and was attacked in the late ’90s by Chechen rebels bent on creating a single Islamist state. What I thought of almost immediately was an article that Elizabeth Rubin wrote for this magazine back in the summer of 2001, which I was lucky enough to edit. “Only You Can Save Your Sons” is an extraordinary feat of reporting and writing, as powerful and haunting as any magazine article I’ve ever read. It turned out that other editors here at the magazine were thinking about Elizabeth’s story, too, and as the discussion on Twitter and elsewhere throughout the day turned to questions of whether the Tsarnaev brothers were influenced by the Chechen war — and as the Chechen leader, Ramzan Kadyrov, announced that the root of the Tsarnaev’s “evil” lay in America and not in Chechnya — it seemed like a good idea to reach out to Elizabeth and get her thoughts, which follow below, on the Chechnya she experienced then and its connection, or lack of connection, to the men thought to have bombed the Boston Marathon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 156, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 156, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbLarge-v3.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Chechnya', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-19T21:24:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joel Lovell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c79054de-fff0-545c-99a6-fb7252b0c6c0', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c79054de-fff0-545c-99a6-fb7252b0c6c0'}, {'abstract': 'Delicacy was a theme of the evening when the Israeli new-music Meitar Ensemble played new works by Bryan Jacobs, Enda Bates, Ofer Pelz, Marcin Stanczyk and Christopher Bailey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/20/arts/music/meitar-ensemble-at-roulette.html', 'snippet': 'Delicacy was a theme of the evening when the Israeli new-music Meitar Ensemble played new works by Bryan Jacobs, Enda Bates, Ofer Pelz, Marcin Stanczyk and Christopher Bailey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is normal to be asked to turn off your cellphone before a concert begins. But Yotam Haber, the artistic director of the MATA Festival, went further