A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Reform = Public Financing', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Reform = Public Financing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-20T02:20:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/714b5697-ee8c-5eef-8102-afe6d9d1eeab', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/714b5697-ee8c-5eef-8102-afe6d9d1eeab'}, {'abstract': 'A compensation-check problem highlights the need for independent monitoring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/20/opinion/more-problems-for-a-mortgage-deal.html', 'snippet': 'A compensation-check problem highlights the need for independent monitoring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A troubled and delayed federal effort to hold big banks accountable for illegal foreclosure practices got off to a poor start this month when some borrowers were unable to cash checks sent to them as part of a $9.3 billion settlement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Problems for a Mortgage Deal', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Problems for a Mortgage Deal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rust Consulting Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Comptroller of the Currency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-20T02:20:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d00b0ab-569f-5401-aeae-cc227d7b3fe8', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d00b0ab-569f-5401-aeae-cc227d7b3fe8'}, {'abstract': 'What ice once protected, it is now up to humans to protect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/20/opinion/reaching-an-arctic-accord.html', 'snippet': 'What ice once protected, it is now up to humans to protect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The central Arctic Ocean has been covered in ice for eons, but under the influence of global warming, nearly half of it is now open water for part of the year. Commercial fishing has not yet begun there, but the urge to begin fishing is almost overwhelming. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaching an Arctic Accord', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reaching an Arctic Accord', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC OCEAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-20T02:20:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/503856bb-e94d-598f-a91b-e8b5e56af6ac', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/503856bb-e94d-598f-a91b-e8b5e56af6ac'}, {'abstract': 'The 175-acre community of Forest Hills Gardens was inspired by British “garden cities”; it has quaint Norman turrets but also prefabricated wall slabs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/nyregion/forest-hills-gardens-a-british-experiment-in-queens.html', 'snippet': 'The 175-acre community of Forest Hills Gardens was inspired by British “garden cities”; it has quaint Norman turrets but also prefabricated wall slabs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Outside is the Queens you know, with all the amenities and hubbub of contemporary city life. But inside, Forest Hills Gardens is another world. Just through an arched underpass, the community’s expansive entrance plaza, Station Square, unfolds with arcaded sidewalks, a domed tower and what could be some sort of Anglo-German manor house in subtly colored and intricately patterned brickwork. It is a disorienting transition, like stepping into a blurred fantasy of the past. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21LOCAL3/21LOCAL3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/nyregion/21LOCAL3/21LOCAL3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit