 the Future of Downtown Detroit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-20T19:41:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/51fddc5e-80a7-5962-8c21-e1277150388f', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51fddc5e-80a7-5962-8c21-e1277150388f'}, {'abstract': 'A eulogy for Philip Roth’s homeroom teacher, his legendary and noble friend Bob Lowenstein.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/opinion/sunday/in-memory-of-a-friend-teacher-and-mentor.html', 'snippet': 'A eulogy for Philip Roth’s homeroom teacher, his legendary and noble friend Bob Lowenstein.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I WAS 12 years old when I entered the Hawthorne Avenue Annex in February 1946. The Annex, a 15-minute bus ride for me from the main high school, was where you went in those days as a freshman at Newark’s Weequahic High. The first teacher I had to face on the first hour of my first day at the Annex was Bob Lowenstein. Dr. Lowenstein. Doc Lowenstein. He was fresh from serving in World War II, unlike most high school teachers he was unassumingly in possession of a Ph.D., and what was recognizable even to a 12-year-old was that this was a formidable man who did not gladly suffer fools.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/sunday-review/21ROTH/21ROTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/sunday-review/21ROTH/21ROTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 510