 None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-21T14:53:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marci Windsheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marci', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Windsheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d907c347-1fa4-537c-aa30-013258c2be16', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d907c347-1fa4-537c-aa30-013258c2be16'}, {'abstract': 'The performance group Poets in Unexpected Places has been using its ties to a thriving poetry slam community to encourage others to deliver their works in public.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/pop-up-poets/', 'snippet': 'The performance group Poets in Unexpected Places has been using its ties to a thriving poetry slam community to encourage others to deliver their works in public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City is invited to a poetry reading.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Train, or at the Laundromat, Your Poem Begins … Now', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Adding Verse And Meter To the Drone Of the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-21T15:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dusica Sue Malesevic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dusica', 'middlename': 'Sue', 'lastname': 'Malesevic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98c8136c-9898-5ad2-9f04-d19d65b21385', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98c8136c-9898-5ad2-9f04-d19d65b21385'}, {'abstract': 'Companies are increasingly using data-driven testing and measurement in the hiring and evaluation of employees — a field called work force science. The enthusiasm for worker measurement and testing is not new, but the ability to collect and mine so much data on worker behavior is. It could open the door to new insights into what makes workers productive, innovative and happy on the job.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/scientific-management-redux-the-difference-is-in-the-data/', 'snippet': 'Companies are increasingly using data-driven testing and measurement in the hiring and evaluation of employees — a field called work force science. The enthusiasm for worker measurement and testing is not new, but the ability to collect and mine s...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wrote a Sunday column about the rise of what is being called “work force science.” Lots of companies are embracing the trend, but anyone familiar with business history might reasonably ask, What’s really new here?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 632, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1263, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/21/business/21-UNBOX/21-UNBOX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scientific Management Redux: The Difference Is in the Data', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Productivity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burks, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'TAYLOR, FREDERICK WINSLOW', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-21T15:29:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8db79256-61a4-5c15-b024-1454f60d12ab', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8db79256-61a4-5c15-b024-1454f60d12ab'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/21/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (13, 49) THE BLETCHLEY CIRCLE Nine years after putting their extraordinary talents to use at Bletchley Park, Britain’s code-breaking headquarters during World War II, four seemingly ordinary women piece together clues they hope will solve a series of murders. Leading the charge is Susan (Anna Maxwell Martin, above), now a homemaker and mother of two, who wangles her way into Scotland Yard when she thinks she has detected a pattern to the killings. But when the police dismiss her theory, she turns for assistance to her former colleagues — Millie (Rachael Stirling), conversant in 14 languages and good with maps; Lucy (Sophie Rundle), with her photographic memory; and Jean (Julie Graham), their onetime supervisor — who, like Susan, have returned to civilian life and kept their intelligence work secret from even those closest to them. “While the solving of the mystery is fairly conventional, these amateur detectives aren’t,” Alessandra Stanley wrote in The New York Times. The three-part mystery “doesn’t have a preachy tone, but it does have a message, and it’s similar to the one in ‘Call the Midwife,’\xa0“ the British series, leading in at 8, “that follows young nurses who after the war helped women in the poorest sections of London deliver babies, usually in tenement homes, on top of rags and newspapers,” she said, adding that “both series find a clever, entertaining way to pay tribute to women who in their time were often overlooked and underestimated, and nevertheless found ways to never be ordinary.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-21T15:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstnam