ence? Or is she Louise Brooks, the woman who takes what she wants and destroys everything? Or is she Nancy Drew caught up in Kafka?” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1005, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tales From the Prison of International Notoriety', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tales From the Prison of International Notoriety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knox, Amanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-21T21:44:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/48f518cb-217c-5ea0-896d-9ecb7291a080', 'word_count': 1330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48f518cb-217c-5ea0-896d-9ecb7291a080'}, {'abstract': 'The Japanese avant-garde musician Keiji Haino brought his loud but tightly controlled explorations for guitar and voice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/22/arts/music/keiji-haino-at-issue-project-room-and-whitney-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Japanese avant-garde musician Keiji Haino brought his loud but tightly controlled explorations for guitar and voice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keiji Haino lives among the class of improvisers who roam so far from what we usually call form — meaning, basically, rules — that they make you question whether what they’re doing is music. When you arrive at that question, generally, the joke’s on you. If you feel nothing for the music, you can walk away. If you feel something for it, you may start to notice that it possesses its own unity, its own rules. Anyway, it’s not Mr. Haino’s problem. “I am not an anarchist,” he has said. “I am anarchy.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/22/arts/22HAINO/22HAINO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'image