hed a $139 million settlement with a group of United States shareholders.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Corp. in $139 Million Settlement With Shareholders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'News Corp. in $139 Million Settlement With Shareholders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Amalgamated Bank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shine Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shareholder Rights and Activism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-22T15:28:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c78c4bf-b74c-5371-9581-fccf3511a003', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c78c4bf-b74c-5371-9581-fccf3511a003'}, {'abstract': 'Michou, the irrepressible showman and 81-year-old owner of a transvestite cabaret in Paris, has become an unofficial ambassador of Montmartre.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/22/lumiere-the-king-of-gay-paree/', 'snippet': 'Michou, the irrepressible showman and 81-year-old owner of a transvestite cabaret in Paris, has become an unofficial ambassador of Montmartre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 57 years, Michel Georges Alfred Catty, known to all as Michou, has run the transvestite Cabaret Michou on the Rue des Martyrs at the base of Montmartre in Paris. With his\xa0 white demi-bouffant, oversize sunglasses and eccentric ways (he dresses only in blue), he is so recognizable that passers-by stop him on the street — to chat, perhaps, or to ask to take his photo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lumière | The King of Gay Paree', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-22T15:35:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0823ff6-87e2-557c-9aaa-62d7572443de', 'word_count': 1165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0823ff6-87e2-557c-9aaa-62d7572443de'}, {'abstract': 'It may be tempting to “invest in what you know,” but Carl Richards explains why that isn’t necessarily the best strategy for average investors.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/22/the-perils-of-investing-in-what-you-know/', 'snippet': 'It may be tempting to “invest in what you know,” but Carl Richards explains why that isn’t necessarily the best strategy for average investors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah, and is the director of investor education at The BAM Alliance. His book, “The Behavior Gap,” was published this year. His sketches are archived\xa0on the Bucks blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Perils of Investing in What You Know', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-22T15:36:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7b60550-8b0b-5825-b41f-fbb0f2a2790a', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7b60550-8b0b-5825-b41f-fbb0f2a2790a'}, {'abstract': 'In this animated Op-Doc, a ghost mourns the failed history of gun control in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/20/opinion/the-ghost-of-gun-control.html', 'snippet': 'In this animated Op-Doc, a ghost mourns the failed history of gun control in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years now I have been fascinated by the story of Sarah Winchester, heiress to the Winchester rifle fortune. In the late 19th century, after early death claimed her family members one by one, she became convinced that her family was cursed, haunted by the ghosts of those killed by the Winchester repeating rifle. Sarah decided that she had to move out West and begin building a house, which would confuse and trap the ghosts that were haunting her and her family name. She settled in San Jose, Calif., and once construction on her home began, it continued until her death in 1922. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/16/opinion/video-opdoc-ghostgun/video-opdoc-ghostgun-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/16/opinion/video-opdoc-ghostgun/video-opdoc-ghostgun-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xl