ral money to combat AIDS abroad may be required to adopt policies opposing prostitution. The answer, judging from the justices’ comments in the first half of the argument, seemed to be that the First Amendment bars attaching that kind of condition to federal grants.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1529, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/us/23SCOTUS/23SCOTUS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices Weigh Conditions in Awarding U.S. Grants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justices Weigh Conditions In Awarding U.S. Grants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T01:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f31098b-74a4-506e-bc23-fedd917875c8', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f31098b-74a4-506e-bc23-fedd917875c8'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court should make it clear that the American government cannot compel organizations to espouse its views in exchange for federal financing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/opinion/free-speech-and-an-anti-prostitution-pledge-at-the-supreme-court.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court should make it clear that the American government cannot compel organizations to espouse its views in exchange for federal financing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 2003 federal statute, called the United States Leadership Against HIV/AIDS, Tuberculosis, and Malaria Act, finances a wide range of public health initiatives to treat and prevent disease around the world. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Speech and an Anti-Prostitution Pledge', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Free Speech and an Anti-Prostitution Pledge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T01:14:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/85689bbd-1ca8-589a-bd35-163a503b07b9', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85689bbd-1ca8-589a-bd35-163a503b07b9'}, {'abstract': 'Hotels around the world are using technology in new ways, sometimes as a substitute for human hospitality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/business/electronic-smarts-at-hotels-attract-guests.html', 'snippet': 'Hotels around the world are using technology in new ways, sometimes as a substitute for human hospitality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the InterContinental Miami hotel, guests in the lobby can call up the drink menu and flag down a server to order using a touch screen on the coffee tables. The Park Hyatt Tokyo and Park Hyatt Seoul give guests free access to over 2,300 international newspapers on their smartphones or tablets using the hotel’s Wi-Fi network and an app called PressReader. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/business/Personal/Personal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/23/business/Personal/Personal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo'