d today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reed Krakoff to Go Solo, Leaving Coach Next Year', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T15:09:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca49c5eb-7e7d-5e39-ae6d-c461f7fd176f', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca49c5eb-7e7d-5e39-ae6d-c461f7fd176f'}, {'abstract': 'At the Los Angeles MOCA, a new exhibition features a collection of more than 10 years of the artist’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/now-showing-urs-fischer/', 'snippet': 'At the Los Angeles MOCA, a new exhibition features a collection of more than 10 years of the artist’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artist Urs Fischer may humbly refer to his ambitious exhibition occupying 65,000 square feet of Los Angeles MOCA’s two venues as “pretty much a sculpture show,” but it’s more than that. Fischer and Jessica Morgan, the Tate Modern’s curator, have designed a stunning visual presentation collecting more than 10 years of Fischer’s artworks into a formal exercise in high contrasts — order built from disarray, precision from destruction — presented through radical scale shifts, disharmonious color schemes and surprisingly cohesive textural oppositions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Showing | Urs Fischer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Urs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T15:15:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trinie Dalton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trinie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dalton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c38a5b11-5ff6-590f-bf21-1c86482d2675', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c38a5b11-5ff6-590f-bf21-1c86482d2675'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators’ early portrait of the two brothers accused in the marathon bombings suggests they were driven by extremist Islamic beliefs and may have learned to build bombs on the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/24/us/boston-marathon-bombing-developments.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators’ early portrait of the two brothers accused in the marathon bombings suggests they were driven by extremist Islamic beliefs and may have learned to build bombs on the Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The portrait investigators have begun to piece together of the two brothers suspected of the Boston Marathon bombings suggests that they were motivated by extremist Islamic beliefs but were not acting with known terrorist groups — and that they may have learned to build bombs simply by logging onto the online English-language magazine of the affiliate of Al Qaeda in Yemen, law enforcement officials said Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/24/us/BOSTON-1/BOSTON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name':