ional', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Hurdle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurdle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bad6c19-c111-5672-851a-24f300b3f9cc', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bad6c19-c111-5672-851a-24f300b3f9cc'}, {'abstract': 'Post-Boston, New York’s mayor said that “our laws and our interpretation of the Constitution” may “have to change.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/security-privacy-and-bloomberg/', 'snippet': 'Post-Boston, New York’s mayor said that “our laws and our interpretation of the Constitution” may “have to change.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael Bloomberg of New York has become a national leader on many important issues — showing real courage on gun regulation, for example.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Security, Privacy and Bloomberg', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T17:52:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9df00476-14c6-593f-a100-141218e1116f', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9df00476-14c6-593f-a100-141218e1116f'}, {'abstract': 'Primary Source suggests three tech tools matched to three current events topics that, together, can help you teach both Common Core skills and digital literacy.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/guest-post-primary-sources-meet-free-web-2-0-tools-for-common-core-learning/', 'snippet': 'Primary Source suggests three tech tools matched to three current events topics that, together, can help you teach both Common Core skills and digital literacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We invited staffers at Primary Source to do a guest-blog version of a presentation we saw them make at the International Society for Technology in Education conference last summer.Because the organization specializes in helping educators use primary sources, like newspapers, to teach about world histories and cultures, its writers chose three well-known tech tools — Google Docs, QR codes and LiveBinders — and showed how they might be matched creatively with Times articles and other resources to teach three topics that draw on both current and historical Times reporting.What tech tools do you use to teach with primary resources? We’d love to hear more ideas, especially if, like these tools, they’re free.— Katherine Schulten', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guest Post | Primary Sources Meet Free Web 2.0 Tools for Common Core Learning', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T17:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Hanson and Elizabeth Howald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Howald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c496aed7-013e-5d53-b1b2-456526dc48a5', 'word_count': 1047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c496aed7-013e-5d53-b1b2-456526dc48a5'}, {'abstract': 'Contest entries will be compiled on an album of raps about financial literacy.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/learning-and-rapping-about-401ks/', 'snippet': 'Contest entries will be compiled on an album of raps about financial literacy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My future’s holding a jobWorking hard all dayThrowing money in the bankToward my 401(k).”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning (and Rapping) About 401(k)s', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boys and Girls Clubs of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charles Schwab Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indianapolis (Ind)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T17:59:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/604123bf-4624-57fb-9115-ce17d274c145', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/604123bf-4624-57fb-9115-ce17d274c145'}, {'abstract': 'Fong Qi Wei’s photographs of disassembled flowers seem apt for the type of spring we’re having.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/the-exploding-floral-inevitable/', 'snippet': 'Fong Qi Wei’s photographs of disassembled flowers seem apt for the type of spring we’re having.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seemed like a never-ending winter this year in the Northeast. Every time warm weather emerged in the past few weeks, another blast of cold quickly followed, leaving us skeptical as to whether spring would ever really come. Perhaps that’s one of the reasons why Fong Qi Wei’s series “Exploded Flowers” seemed so apt when I came upon it on one one of my favorite art and design blogs, Colossal. Wei disassembled and photographed 20 varieties of flowers, arranging each blossom’s pieces to emphasize its radial symmetry. It’s a graphic and colorful way of looking at one of nature’s most recognizable symbols of spring. Wei started the series in 2011 and finished last November; the images have been making the rounds on various design blogs recently because five of the pictures won second place as a series in the 2012 International Photography Awards. For more of Wei’s images, check out the entire series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Exploding Floral Inevitable', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T18:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gail Bichler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bichler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff81b653-ed36-5f73-aaab-457e36af223b', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff81b653-ed36-5f73-aaab-457e36af223b'}, {'abstract': 'After City Room reported last week on the accuracy of a plot element in the TV drama, a reader’s comment on his real-life experience required our detectives to dig deeper.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/23/maybe-mad-men-lawyers-knew-more-than-lindsays/', 'snippet': 'After City Room reported last week on the accuracy of a plot element in the TV drama, a reader’s comment on his real-life experience required our detectives to dig deeper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, we reported that the “Mad Men” character Henry Francis’s new job as a deputy mayor of New York City did not put him at odds with residency laws, because the city had no such laws in 1967 and 1968, when the new season of “Mad Men” is set.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/nyregion/00cityroom-grossman/00cityroom-grossman-jumbo.png', 'height': 420, 'width': 505, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/nyregion/00cityroom-grossman/00cityroom-grossman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 420, 'width': 505, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/nyregion/00cityroom-grossman/00cityroom-grossman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/23/nyregion/00cityroom-grossman/00cityroom-grossman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/23/nyregion/00cityroom-grossman/00cityroom-grossman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Maybe ‘Mad Men’ Lawyers Knew More Than Lindsay’s', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis, Henry', 