'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-23T21:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston and Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86567746-2433-5c5d-8ff4-5caf81b90019', 'word_count': 4304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86567746-2433-5c5d-8ff4-5caf81b90019'}, {'abstract': 'The three-day Tectonics Festival of contemporary music made imaginative use of an equally imaginative new performance complex in Reykjavik.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/24/arts/music/ilan-volkovs-tectonics-festival-in-reykjavik-iceland.html', 'snippet': 'The three-day Tectonics Festival of contemporary music made imaginative use of an equally imaginative new performance complex in Reykjavik.', 'lead_paragraph': 'REYKJAVIK, Iceland — Visitors to Thingvellir National Park, just over 30 miles outside of Reykjavik, can stand on the North American continental plate one minute, and Eurasia the next, by simply crossing a rocky gorge: it’s a divergent boundary where the world’s face is ripping apart slowly. In Reykjavik a different kind of upheaval was evident last weekend, as the Iceland Symphony Orchestra presented its second annual Tectonics Festival at the Harpa Concert Hall and Conference Center here on Thursday, Friday and Saturday. The festival also engaged in shifting borders, but convergence, rather than separation, was the chief process in play.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '