he New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Full Speed', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-24T02:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df6d1bdc-96b9-5793-83b2-7c6248271826', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df6d1bdc-96b9-5793-83b2-7c6248271826'}, {'abstract': 'Juan Lagares gets the call in the absence of Matt den Dekker, who is recovering from a broken wrist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/24/sports/baseball/mets-outfielder-juan-lagares-surprised-to-be-called-up.html', 'snippet': 'Juan Lagares gets the call in the absence of Matt den Dekker, who is recovering from a broken wrist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Juan Lagares stood in front of his new locker at Citi Field on Tuesday with a huge smile on his face. He had arrived in New York only a few hours earlier after catching a red-eye flight from Colorado.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets Summon an Outfielder From the Minor Leagues, and Even He Is Surprised', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside Pitch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Terry L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'den Dekker, Matt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagares, Juan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-24T02:06:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce7f4ad4-fec4-509e-ad9c-ff2dcffcfdcf', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce7f4ad4-fec4-509e-ad9c-ff2dcffcfdcf'}, {'abstract': 'The experience of a health system in Chicago shows how hard it will be to expand “accountable care” and keep costs from rising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/24/business/accountable-care-helping-hospitals-keep-medical-costs-down.html', 'snippet': 'The experience of a health system in Chicago shows how hard it will be to expand “accountable care” and keep costs from rising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — On a stormy evening this spring, nurses at Dr. Gary Stuck’s family practice were on the phone with patients with heart ailments, asking them not to shovel snow. The idea was to keep them out of the hospital, and that effort — combined with dozens more like it — is starting to make a difference: across the city, doctors are providing less, but not worse, health care.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/24/business/ADVOCATE/ADVOCATE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/24/business/ADVOCATE/ADVOCATE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/24/business/ADVOCATE/ADVOCATE-ju