d States, is just steps from the Legoland theme park. It is designed with a shorter clientele in mind: its rooms, decorated according to themes like adventure, pirate and kingdom, feature bunk beds and a trundle bed with night lights, and its restaurant’s buffet is low enough for children to serve themselves.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Design a Little Blocky?', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hotels: Is the Design a Little Blocky?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-25T17:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Brennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d77dad0-0e1a-59a3-98f2-56593ca9d6d8', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d77dad0-0e1a-59a3-98f2-56593ca9d6d8'}, {'abstract': 'The musical “The Last Five Years” has a huge following, despite mixed reviews.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/theater/the-last-five-years-by-jason-robert-brown-heading-to-film.html', 'snippet': 'The musical “The Last Five Years” has a huge following, despite mixed reviews.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Last Five Years” is a modest musical with a huge following. Jason Robert Brown’s two-person show follows the doomed marriage of Jamie, an arrogant Jewish novelist, and Cathy, an insecure gentile actress. The story of their troubled courtship is told chronologically — backward (hers) and forward (his). Their scenes intersect in the middle, when Jamie proposes to Cathy during a boat ride in Central Park. It’s an intimate, melancholy show that has confused and alienated some audience members and enchanted others since its 2001 premiere at the Northlight Theater in Skokie, Ill.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28FIVEFAN1_SPAN/28FIVEFAN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Strange Pull of a Simple Love Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Strange Pull of a Simple Love Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piepenburg, Erik', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2