friends with the same names and occupations as those in her life off the page. This is a relatively extreme case in autobiographical fiction, more experimental than confessional, in which the niceties of inventing characters are dispensed with entirely. It’s an intriguing and sometimes unsettling decision, resulting in a sort of hybrid: not quite fiction, not quite memoir. Sitcoms have used this device for years, with stars from Ozzie and Harriet Nelson to Louis C. K. creating irreverent alternative realities modeled closely on their lives. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 664}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 926, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1559, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-bks-RINGWALD/0428-bks-RINGWALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}},