ber a time when Bruckner and Mahler seemed inevitably, eternally conjoined. Probably this was just a matter of their unusually long symphonies and inscrutable mysteries contained therein. Otherwise, little of substance seemed to link Bruckner, the peasant priest of Austrian Romanticism, and Mahler, his more cosmopolitan admirer and successor.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2038, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/26/arts/26PHIL_SPAN/PHIL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Glories in Bruckner', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding Glories in Bruckner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Alan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bruckner, Anton', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-25T21:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9abf1c01-05da-5a74-877c-5aeff3e47184', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9abf1c01-05da-5a74-877c-5aeff3e47184'}, {'abstract': 'Performing at 54 Below, Barbara Cook sings an earthy, direct mix of pop, jazz and blues that has the feel of a conversation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/26/arts/music/barbara-cook-at-54-below.html', 'snippet': 'Performing at 54 Below, Barbara Cook sings an earthy, direct mix of pop, jazz and blues that has the feel of a conversation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That Barbara Cook’s new show at 54 Below is, in fact, an old one that she performed last year at two locations, including Carnegie Hall, is no reason not to return and discover how she has grown into the material, which she described on Wednesday evening as “swinging, sassy and torchy.” This formerly demure Broadway soprano, now 85, began shucking off her ingénue persona four decades ago and is now comfortable in a pop-jazz-blues realm that was almost unthinkable territory for her to explore way back when.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank':