rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79e8001f-d629-59bd-abf8-cc374485b650', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79e8001f-d629-59bd-abf8-cc374485b650'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/26/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13, 49) RODGERS & HAMMERSTEIN’S CAROUSEL The Broadway baby Kelli O’Hara and the opera singer Nathan Gunn, above with Ms. O’Hara, star in this semistaged New York Philharmonic production of the musical about an ill-fated romance between a carousel barker and a millworker in coastal Maine, featuring classics like “If I Loved You,” “June Is Bustin’ Out All Over” and “You’ll Never Walk Alone.” Stephanie Blythe, Shuler Hensley, Jason Danieley, Jessie Mueller, Kate Burton and John Cullum round out the cast of this “Live From Lincoln Center” presentation, directed by John Rando and conducted by Rob Fisher. The New York City Ballet principals Robert Fairchild and Tiler Peck perform the pas de deux in Act II. “There’s a sly fitness to the pairing” of Ms. O’Hara and Mr. Gunn, Charles Isherwood wrote in The New York Times. “You might say these gifted singers come from different worlds, just as do their characters, Julie Jordan and Billy Bigelow.” He added: “Happily, and perhaps obviously, things work out better for Ms. O’Hara and Mr. Gunn than they do for the loving but trouble-plagued characters they portray. Things come out pretty swell for the audience too: from top to bottom this is as gorgeously sung a production of this sublime 1945 Broadway musical as you are ever likely to hear.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T05:00:10+0000',