rt Hunter and his wife, Mary Leong, set out along historic Route 66 in their 1971 Rolls-Royce Silver Shadow. In contrast to the hordes of impoverished Okie farmers who headed to California in overloaded jalopies to escape the Dust Bowl during the Great Depression, Mr. Hunter and Ms. Leong would be journeying in reverse, headed east from Santa Monica, Calif., in a car that is the opposite of a jalopy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cruising Route 66 in a Rolls-Royce Station Wagon', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rolls-Royce Motor Cars', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Monica (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T11:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald Ahrens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahrens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1344430-26ec-506b-b51e-3f3f17e6c588', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1344430-26ec-506b-b51e-3f3f17e6c588'}, {'abstract': 'The doctors could address my symptoms. But they didn’t much care about my vanishing sense of self.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/magazine/the-problem-with-how-we-treat-bipolar-disorder.html', 'snippet': 'The doctors could address my symptoms. But they didn’t much care about my vanishing sense of self.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last time I saw my old self, I was 27 years old and living in Boston. I was doing well in graduate school, had a tight circle of friends and was a prolific creative writer. Married to my high-school sweetheart, I had just had my first child. Back then, my best times were twirling my baby girl under the gloaming sky on a Florida beach and flopping on the bed with my husband — feet propped against the wall — and talking. The future seemed wide open.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 766}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 803, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1175, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28depression1/28depression1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Problem With How We Treat Bipolar Disorder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Selfless', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Logan, Linda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bipolar Disorder', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T11:01:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Logan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Logan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7cdeab9-9aa3-5126-b19e-ab33d42f88c0', 'word_count': 4115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7cdeab9-9aa3-5126-b19e-ab33d42f88c0'}, {'abstract': 'Diederik Stapel, a Dutch social psychologist, perpetrated an audacious academic fraud by making up studies that told the world what it wanted to hear about human nature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/magazine/diederik-stapels-audacious-academic-fraud.html', 'snippet': 'Diederik Stapel, a Dutch social psychologist, perpetrated an audacious academic fraud by making up studies that told the world what it wanted to hear about human nature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One summer night in 2011, a tall, 40-something professor named Diederik Stapel stepped out of his elegant brick house in the Dutch city of Tilburg to visit a friend around the corner. It was close to midnight, but his colleague Marcel Zeelenberg had called and texted Stapel that evening to say that he wanted to see him about an urgent matter. The two had known each other since the early ’90s, when they were Ph.D. students at the University of Amsterdam; now both were psychologists at Tilburg University. In 2010, Stapel became dean of the university’s School of Social and Behavioral Sciences and Zeelenberg head of the social psychology department. Stapel and his wife, Marcelle, had supported Zeelenberg through a difficult divorce a few years earlier. As he approached Zeelenberg’s door, Stapel wondered if his colleague was having problems with his new girlfriend.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 515, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 515}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 880, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1065, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/magazine/28stapel1/28stapel1-