t have taken.', 'lead_paragraph': '“After the first death, there is no other,” Dylan Thomas wrote. How obvious, one might think. But the one-time-only nature of death is anything but self-evident in Kate Atkinson’s new novel, “Life After Life.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 677}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1816, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28PROSE/28PROSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Subject to Revision', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Subject to Revision', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Life After Life (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prose, Francine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atkinson, Kate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:12:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francine Prose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed98790f-846d-5ff3-ad76-1a38b7893894', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed98790f-846d-5ff3-ad76-1a38b7893894'}, {'abstract': 'In Rachel Kushner’s second novel, an impressionable artist navigates the volatile worlds of New York and Rome in the 1970s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/rachel-kushners-flamethrowers.html', 'snippet': 'In Rachel Kushner’s second novel, an impressionable artist navigates the volatile worlds of New York and Rome in the 1970s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Flamethrowers,” her frequently dazzling second novel, Rachel Kushner thrusts us into the white-hot center of the 1970s conceptual art world, motorcycle racing, upper-class Italy and the rampant kidnappings and terrorism that plagued it. It’s an irresistible, high-octane mix — and a departure from the steamier pleasures of her critically acclaimed first novel, “Telex From Cuba.” The language is equally gorgeous, however, and Kushner’s insights into place, society and the complicated rules of belonging, and unbelonging, can be mordantly brilliant. None of the characters in “The Flamethrowers” are quite what they seem, fabricating pasts as nonchalantly as they throw together their art. Above all, they hunger to be seen, to distinguish themselves from the ordinary. One artist, responding to the question of why he invents, defends his florid lies as “a form of discretion.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 577}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1969, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28GARCIA/28GARCIA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revolutions Per Minute', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revolutions Per Minute', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:12:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cristina García', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cristina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'García', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e9e0c8a-48cf-5f59-8711-b926160da804', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e9e0c8a-48cf-5f59-8711-b926160da804'}, {'abstract': 'Marci Shore examines the psyche of Eastern Europe in the wake of the revolutions of 1989.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/marci-shores-taste-of-ashes.html', 'snippet': 'Marci Shore examines the psyche of Eastern Europe in the wake of the revolutions of 1989.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When thinking about the fall of any dictatorship, one should have no illusions that the whole system comes to an end like a bad dream,” Ryszard Kapuscinski wrote 30 years ago. A Polish journalist, Kapuscinski was ostensibly reporting on the fall of the shah of Iran, but his devoted Polish readers knew that everything he said applied to their part of the world as well. “A dictatorship . . . leaves behind itself an empty, sour field on which the tree of thought won’t grow quickly. It is not always the best people who emerge from hiding.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1538, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28HOCHSCHILD/28HOCHSCHILD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shadowlands', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shadowlands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochschild, Adam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shore, Marci', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9356fa92-da62-584b-9862-7e48cb7961f7', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9356fa92-da62-584b-9862-7e48cb7961f7'}, {'abstract': 'It was love at first sight, 40 years after they had met, for Vilma Kracun and João Crisóstomo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/absence-40-years-worth-makes-hearts-grow-very-fond-vows.html', 'snippet': 'It was love at first sight, 40 years after they had met, for Vilma Kracun and João Crisóstomo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'João Crisóstomo, a butler to members of New York society, long ago mastered the art of decorum and the stiff upper lip. Yet in his private life he is as ebullient as they come, turning strangers into friends with a quicksilver smile and an ardent embrace. The result is a Christmas greetings list that has grown exponentially, even though instead of sending cards, he calls on the telephone.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/28VOWS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/28VOWS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/28VOWS1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/28VOWS1_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1345, 'width': 1798, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/20VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/20VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/weddings/28VOWS1_SPAN/20VOWS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Absence (40 Years’ Worth) Makes 2 Hearts Grow Fonder', 'kicker': 'Vilma Kracun and JoÃo CrisÓstomo', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Absence (40 Years’ Worth) Makes 2 Hearts Grow Fonder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shattuck, Kathryn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:12:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbda338e-5f47-5843-978b-b7e883abd5ae', 'word_count': 1369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbda338e-5f47-5843-978b-b7e883abd5ae'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Pollan on how to prepare your own food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/cooked-by-michael-pollan.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Pollan on how to prepare your own food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the second page of “Cooked,” his paean to home cooking, Michael Pollan makes a statement that may baffle his many fans (I am one). “I seldom made time for cooking or gave it much consideration,” he confesses. Later on, he tells us that before he wrote this book his regular method of preparing a meal was to “throw expensive fillets” of meat on the grill.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 162, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 162}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 276, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 552, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815/0428-BKS-Wilson-1366064213815-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of the Oven . . .', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of the Oven . . .', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Bee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollan, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:14:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bee Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b0316f8-4548-5806-a83d-4e3fbd056190', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b0316f8-4548-5806-a83d-4e3fbd056190'}, {'abstract': 'Gish Jen discusses the differences between Asian and Western approaches to identity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/tiger-writing-by-gish-jen.html', 'snippet': 'Gish Jen discusses the differences between Asian and Western approaches to identity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of students at Cornell, born in Asia but raised in the United States by immigrant parents, were instructed to keep a diary. They struggled to recall the events of their own daily lives when they were later quizzed about them, remembering fewer details about their experiences than their Euro-American counterparts. Qi Wang, the Cornell scholar of “cross-cultural” cognition who conducted the experiment, speculated that Asians were not more forgetful but that they had, perhaps, filtered out the contents of their own stories, deeming them unworthy of being encoded as memories in the first place. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 577}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1969, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28YANG/28YANG-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Selves', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many Selves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yang, Wesley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jen, Gish', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:14:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wesley Yang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wesley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3211a5c-ffd9-5a2e-bc6f-0e4db8244c27', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3211a5c-ffd9-5a2e-bc6f-0e4db8244c27'}, {'abstract': 'Mary Roach on what happens after you eat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/gulp-by-mary-roach.html', 'snippet': 'Mary Roach on what happens after you eat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An Italian saliva expert named Erika Silletti recently addressed a dental conference. History doesn’t record which aspects of saliva she extolled from the stage that day. Perhaps she described how art conservators are so enamored with its cleaning power they use it to dab fragile artworks. (Dishwashing detergent is a kind of simulated saliva, digesting the food you couldn’t stomach.) Or maybe she praised the people of Greece — who spit on everything as a sign of good luck — for their saliva-positive attitudes. What we do know is that her speech was a bit of a disaster. The assembled dentists regarded her blankly. She returned to her hotel room and burst into tears. “They think of it as lubricating, and that’s it!” she complained to her boyfriend.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/0428-bks-RONSON-1366064243822-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/0428-bks-RONSON-1366064243822-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 805}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/0428-bks-RONSON-1366064243822-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 763, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/0428-bks-RONSON-1366064243822-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1526, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/28ronson-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/28ronson-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28ronson/28ronson-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '. . . And Down the Gullet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '. . . And Down the Gullet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronson, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roach, Mary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Digestive Tract', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:14:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Ronson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ronson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8990f7d-70c0-56fc-9609-03888c8f6ac9', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8990f7d-70c0-56fc-9609-03888c8f6ac9'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time, once again, to quote David Winner’s “Brilliant Orange: The Neurotic Genius of Dutch Soccer.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/26/high-trees-tall-poppies-egalitarian-winds/', 'snippet': 'It’s time, once again, to quote David Winner’s “Brilliant Orange: The Neurotic Genius of Dutch Soccer.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been nearly two years now that I’ve gone without quoting David Winner’s “Brilliant Orange: The Neurotic Genius of Dutch Soccer” in this space. I’m going to fix that now. Not on account of this week’s performances by two voetbal stars, Robin van Persie and Arjen Robben — but did you see those performances?! — but because an article in this weekend’s magazine includes a reference to “a Dutch proverb about high trees catching a lot of wind.” It’s the first defense Diederik Stapel, a prominent social psychologist in the Netherlands, employs to counter accusations of academic fraud. People envy a standout.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 156, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 156, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/17/magazine/17-dailydogear/17-dailydogear-thumbLarge-v3.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'High Trees, Tall Poppies, Egalitarian Winds', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:14:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62922ba6-b2f6-5197-80cd-bcaa5ac99a50', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62922ba6-b2f6-5197-80cd-bcaa5ac99a50'}, {'abstract': 'For 43 years “Li’l Abner” entertained millions, but the comic strip’s creator was a complicated, often unpleasant man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/al-capp-by-michael-schumacher-and-denis-kitchen.html', 'snippet': 'For 43 years “Li’l Abner” entertained millions, but the comic strip’s creator was a complicated, often unpleasant man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cartoonist Al Capp, John Steinbeck wrote in 1952, “may very possibly be the best writer in the world today.” As Michael Schumacher and Denis Kitchen document in “Al Capp: A Life to the Contrary,” he could also be one of the most contentious. Capp (1909-79), whose comic strip “Li’l Abner” ran for 43 years and reached 90 million readers at its peak, was a celebrity: a radio personality, magazine essayist, talk-show regular and college speaker. Among his strip’s contributions to the cultural landscape were the expression “double whammy” and the invention of Sadie Hawkins Day, a mock holiday (and national craze in its time) that inspired Sadie Hawkins dances, to which women could invite men. Underlying Capp’s antic humor and prodigious productivity, however, was a toxic chip on his shoulder. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 854}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 716, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 716, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28SUBWEBSTER/28SUBWEBSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despot of Dogpatch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despot of Dogpatch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumacher, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitchen, Denis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T17:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fe9ebfc-0fd1-5618-975a-276e8362c9cb', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fe9ebfc-0fd1-5618-975a-276e8362c9cb'}, {'abstract': 'T. D. Allman traces five centuries of Florida history, from discovery to settlement to tourist destination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/books/review/finding-florida-by-t-d-allman.html', 'snippet': 'T. D. Allman traces five centuries of Florida history, from discovery to settlement to tourist destination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who has commuted to a Fort Lauderdale beach will be familiar with the journey T. D. Allman describes in “Finding Florida: The True History of the Sunshine State.”Because drawbridges that lead to the ocean’s edge are raised to allow large boats up the inland waterways, highway passengers are almost invariably subjected to long waits. This imposition — and the fact that the people behind steering wheels don’t protest — drives Allman to distraction. “Not one person demands to know: Why is it that the people with boats take precedence over us?” he writes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1266, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/books/review/28STARR/28STARR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Contested State', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Contested State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allman, T D', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T1