'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/052108b9-920c-5b1f-a4de-ba38e8e737a9', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/052108b9-920c-5b1f-a4de-ba38e8e737a9'}, {'abstract': '“What Maisie Knew,” a new film updating the Henry James novel, raises anew the issue of just how hard it is to adapt James’s work to the screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/movies/translating-henry-james-for-the-screen.html', 'snippet': '“What Maisie Knew,” a new film updating the Henry James novel, raises anew the issue of just how hard it is to adapt James’s work to the screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Henry James’s novel “What Maisie Knew” was published in 1897, the movies were in their infancy — the brothers Lumière had exhibited their first motion pictures just two years earlier — and there’s no evidence that in the 19 years of life that remained to James he ever cared a jot for the new medium; it wouldn’t have met his standards. The movies have since avenged that slight in a way that seems at least arguably Jamesian: They have paid his work the ambiguous tribute of their attention. Dozens of adaptations of his difficult, delicate fictions have been produced since his death, all trying (and most failing) to capture something of his essence, the elusive beast in the verbal jungle.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28JAMES1_SPAN/28JAMES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28JAMES1_SPAN/28JAMES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/arts/28JAMES1_SPAN/28JAMES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, '