uist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5b3b077-eaff-5e8f-968b-bd3d54f51f0b', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5b3b077-eaff-5e8f-968b-bd3d54f51f0b'}, {'abstract': 'TV shows, guest appearances, books by (yes) and about cats, and a new Monopoly piece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/fashion/the-year-of-the-cat-books-of-style.html', 'snippet': 'TV shows, guest appearances, books by (yes) and about cats, and a new Monopoly piece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to the Chinese lunar calendar, the Year of the Snake began on Feb. 10. But by most other standards, 2013 is shaping up to be the Year of the Cat. In the first week of February, Hasbro, makers of the Monopoly board game, announced they were replacing one of the traditional tokens, the flat iron, with a curvaceous silver pussycat. Ailurophobes howled, blaming the “all-powerful cat lobby” (so teased a reporter on NPR), but the evidence suggests that public taste alone explains the switch. There are 86 million cats in American households, and in a Facebook survey, the nimble cat instantly leapt ahead of the regular Monopoly retinue to become players’ piece of choice — nearly twice as popular as the Scottie Dog, and three times more popular than the Car and Battleship. Take that, cat detractors! ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1010, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28BOOKS_SPAN/28BOOKS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year of the Cat', 'kicker': 'Books Of Style', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Year of the Cat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trachtenberg, Peter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Caroline', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nagelschneider, Mieshelle', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T22:46:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/318ab3b6-0a2a-570c-bb98-ba1bccae44b2', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/318ab3b6-0a2a-570c-bb98-ba1bccae44b2'}, {'abstract': 'The Israeli military said on Friday that it planned to remove shells containing phosphorus from active use within about a year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/27/world/middleeast/israel-military-to-stop-using-shells-containing-phosphorus.html', 'snippet': 'The Israeli military said on Friday that it planned to remove shells containing phosphorus from active use within about a year.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Israeli military said on Friday that it planned to remove shells containing phosphorus from active use within about a year. The munitions, typically used to illuminate a battlefield or to create a smokescreen to mask troop movements, will be replaced with a new type of shell that uses gas, the military said. Israeli and international human rights organizations accused Israel of using white phosphorus munitions improperly during Israel’s three-week military offensive against Hamas and other militant groups in Gaza in the winter of 2008-9. Such munitions are not prohibited under international law, but they are not supposed to be used in civilian areas, because white phosphorus is highly flammable and, like napalm, it can burn flesh. Israel maintained that its use of shells containing phosphorus did not violate international law. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel: Military to Stop Using Shells Containing Phosphorus', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israel: Military to Stop Using Shells Containing Phosphorus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'White Phosphorus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-26T22:48:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3546a3ec-1d6f-51b9-a1dd-288f5509c1d5', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3546a3ec-1d6f-51b9-a1dd-288f5509c1d5'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Pasternak, president and artistic director of Creative Time, works to help artists stage free spectacles in public spaces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/fashion/a-den-mother-for-art-spectacles.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Pasternak, president and artistic director of Creative Time, works to help artists stage free spectacles in public spaces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The children were too close to the stanchions, and Anne Pasternak wasn’t having it. “You can’t touch the ropes or the horses won’t come visit you,” she trilled.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28PASTERNAK_SPAN/28PASTERNAK_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/fashion/28PASTERNAK_SPAN/28PASTERNAK_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '