_paragraph': 'The story of Ulysses S. Grant is an American epic practically without equal. Hollywood couldn’t dream it up. Even today, Grant’s complicated persona and unquenchable will to victory enthrall historians and fiction writers alike. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grant in Pictures', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Ulysses S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Robert E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T02:40:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Traub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Traub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efb102ae-1168-5203-a4ab-bc79f2cf939e', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efb102ae-1168-5203-a4ab-bc79f2cf939e'}, {'abstract': 'After the novelist Meg Wolitzer walks the dog and visits with family, she goes back to work, either at home or at the library.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/nyregion/meg-wolitzer-writes-on-weekends-with-time-to-loll.html', 'snippet': 'After the novelist Meg Wolitzer walks the dog and visits with family, she goes back to work, either at home or at the library.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In novels like “The Wife"and “The Ten-Year Nap,” Meg Wolitzer has explored themes of marriage, sex, gender politics and family. Her real domestic life is spent on the Upper East Side, where she lives with her husband, Richard Panek, 54, who is also a writer; their younger son, Charlie, who is 18; and the family’s dog, Jet, a 5-year-old Havanese. The couple’s older son, Gabriel, 22, is a senior at the University of Chicago. Ms. Wolitzer, 53, said she usually treated Sundays as a lighter writing day, carving out time for rest, Mexican food and an occasional cheap massage. Her newest book, “The Interestings,” was published this month by Riverhead Books.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/nyregion/28ROUTINE3_SPAN/28ROUTINE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bit of Writing, and Various States of Loll', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bit of Writing, and Various States of Loll', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T02:53:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4815fa72-c33d-5ad2-9e20-2d241f0bdb5c', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4815fa72-c33d-5ad2-9e20-2d241f0bdb5c'}, {'abstract': 'Justin Pugh, an intense offensive tackle from Syracuse, had to overcome personal trials, including the death of his father, on his way to the N.F.L.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/27/sports/football/justin-pugh-giants-top-pick-has-overcome-obstacles.html', 'snippet': 'Justin Pugh, an intense offensive tackle from Syracuse, had to overcome personal trials, including the death of his father, on his way to the N.F.L.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — Justin Pugh, the Syracuse offensive tackle taken by the Giants in the first round, is a bruising, 6-foot-4, 305-pound all-American whom one of his college coaches on Friday called fiery, hard-nosed and eager to knock people down.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/27/sports/giants/