 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d06dc1b-bb63-506d-a67b-3c83c8c559ca', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d06dc1b-bb63-506d-a67b-3c83c8c559ca'}, {'abstract': 'Women are being removed from the list of “American Novelists” and put into their own category. Moves like that make it harder and slower for women to gain equality in the literary world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/wikipedias-sexism.html', 'snippet': 'Women are being removed from the list of “American Novelists” and put into their own category. Moves like that make it harder and slower for women to gain equality in the literary world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early last week I noticed something strange on Wikipedia. It appeared that, gradually, over time, the volunteer editors who create the site had begun moving women, one by one, from the “American Novelists” category to the “American Women Novelists” subcategory. Female authors whose last names began with A or B had been most affected. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/28wikipedia/28wikipedia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/28wikipedia/28wikipedia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/28wikipedia/28wikipedia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 825, 'subT