of video games, Ubisoft’s Montreal studio has aimed to maintain an old-fashioned attention to artistic detail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Tchae Measroch leaves work, his hands usually bear a fresh cut or bruise. He works, often on his knees, in a small room crowded with an odd mix of items: a dried-grass hula skirt, a car door, baseball bats, swords and knives of varying length, a camouflage net typically used to disguise military equipment from enemy eyes.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 459}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1434, 'width': 1876, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/business/28-UBISOFT/28-UBISOFT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where the Artists Are the Superheroes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where the Artists Are the Superheroes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Caitlin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ubisoft Entertainment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T17:58:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Kelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41bcbb55-459c-558f-b8df-983c0fd9a0dc', 'word_count': 1902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41bcbb55-459c-558f-b8df-983c0fd9a0dc'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t forget the lies.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/27/the-great-degrader/', 'snippet': 'Don’t forget the lies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been focused on economic policy lately, so I sort of missed the big push to rehabilitate Bush’s image; also, as a premature anti-Bushist who pointed out how terrible a president he was back when everyone else was praising him as a Great Leader, I’m kind of worn out on the subject.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great Degrader', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T18:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f4a5d1a-bd13-5588-a5f7-d53dffe1c04d', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f4a5d1a-bd13-5588-a5f7-d53dffe1c04d'}, {'abstract': 'It’s mainly men who have glorified themselves by amassing large collections of valuable work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/the-art-of-the-hunt.html', 'snippet': 'It’s mainly men who have glorified themselves by amassing large collections of valuable work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE next time you visit an art museum, look around — not at the paintings, but at the people in the galleries. It’s a fair bet that women will outnumber men; even government statistics say so. More women than men study art, too, at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Art appreciation is a female predilection and so, you would think, is collecting. To buy art, you have to shop. Need I say anything about which sex has a proclivity for browsing — an essential element of buying, um, visual art?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1469, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28MUSEUM/28MUSEUM-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of the Hunt', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of the Hunt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dobrzynski, Judith H', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T18:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith H. Dobrzynski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Dobrzynski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b718e7e-3f9e-5952-8c0d-53d59239663f', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b718e7e-3f9e-5952-8c0d-53d59239663f'}, {'abstract': 'We have become suspicious of those who take a stand against the abuses of power or dogma.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/whither-moral-courage.html', 'snippet': 'We have become suspicious of those who take a stand against the abuses of power or dogma.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE find it easier, in these confused times, to admire physical bravery than moral courage — the courage of the life of the mind, or of public figures. A man in a cowboy hat vaults a fence to help Boston bomb victims while others flee the scene: we salute his bravery, as we do that of servicemen returning from the battlefront, or men and women struggling to overcome debilitating illnesses or injuries.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 939}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 655, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1309, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/28/sunday-review/28RUSHDIE/28RUSHDIE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Whither Moral Courage?', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whither Moral Courage?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-27T18:44:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Rushdie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rushdie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9782f04-67df-54d7-92a0-014cfa5d36fc', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9782f04-67df-54d7-92a0-014cfa5d36fc'}, {'abstract': 'The current energy revolution will be a boon to America’s economy, but it will in no way allow the United States to turn its back on the rest of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/opinion/sunday/the-dark-side-of-energy-independence.html', 'snippet': 'The current energy revolution will be a boon to America’s economy, but it will in no way allow the United States to turn its back on the rest of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JUST as the world was writing off America as a declining power, the country now finds itself on the cusp of realizing one of its longstanding goals: energy independence. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimed