o': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALL NIPPON AIRWAYS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boeing Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ethiopian Airlines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-28T05:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea20d56c-95eb-5003-8c7c-2eb285f99842', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea20d56c-95eb-5003-8c7c-2eb285f99842'}, {'abstract': 'Best of long-form journalism this week.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/newswallah-long-reads-edition-60/', 'snippet': 'Best of long-form journalism this week.', 'lead_paragraph': '“What explains this increasing perversion in society, where even children aren’t spared?” Debarshi Dasgupta and Prachi Pinglay-Plumber ask in Outlook magazine (print only). The authors survey the increasing number of reports of child sex abuse and ask what it is about Indian society that is causing this phenomenon. According to the National Crime Records Bureau between 2001 and 2011 there has been a three-fold increase in registered cases of child sex abuse, from 2,113 in 2001 to 7,112 a decade later. In speaking with experts in the field and those who deal with victims and perpetrators of child sex abuse, the authors find that it is not easy to determine a direct cause for the increase in sex abuse among minors. They find that offenders vary in behavior and psychology and therefore it is both hard to draw any generalizations and create effective means of prevention.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Long Reads Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bharatiya Janata Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Open (Magazine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Outlook (Magazine)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tehelka', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-28T08:35:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neha Thirani Bagri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neha', 'middlename': 'Thirani', 'lastname': 'Bagri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69ced940-e9fa-5666-9064-a4d30e03c08a', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69ced940-e9fa-5666-9064-a4d30e03c08a'}, {'abstract': 'What if Real Madrid and Barcelona refuse to roll over in the Champions League semifinals, and instead rewrite a week of commentary about German dominance?', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/contrary-to-reports-the-champions-league-final-isnt-set-yet/', 'snippet': 'What if Real Madrid and Barcelona refuse to roll over in the Champions League semifinals, and instead rewrite a week of commentary about German dominance?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Will you be watching the second legs of the Champions League semifinals this week — Borussia Dortmund’s visit to Real Madrid on Tuesday, and Bayern Munich’s journey to Barcelona a day later? We ask because, if you haven’t heard, the outcome is a foregone conclusion, what with last week’s blowouts and the era of Spanish dominance being over and German soccer ascendant and plates shifting and all that. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/sports/soccer/ucl2/ucl2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/sports/soccer/ucl2/ucl2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/02/sports/soccer/ucl2/ucl2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/02/sports/soccer/ucl2/ucl2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Contrary to Reports, the Champions League Final Isn’t Set Yet', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'UEFA Champions League (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-28T10:20:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f78e449-4b8a-50be-bcba-e7167a69c4de', 'word_count': 981, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f78e449-4b8a-50be-bcba-e7167a69c4de'}, {'abstract': 'The Fed versus fiscal policy.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/monetarism-falls-short-somewhat-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'The Fed versus fiscal policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The central debate over macroeconomic policy is, of course, between Keynesians and Austerians. And at this point the Keynesians have overwhelmingly won the debate everywhere except where it matters – the intellectual basis for austerity economics has collapsed, but actual austerity continues apace on both sides of the Atlantic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monetarism Falls Short (Somewhat Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-28T11:41:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/388b08a7-85c1-56af-9f57-d4aac2246de9', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/388b08a7-85c1-56af-9f57-d4aac2246de9'}, {'abstract': 'Cherry-picking issues, not facts.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/28/knaves-fools-and-me-meta/', 'snippet': 'Cherry-picking issues, not facts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One criticism I face fairly often is the assertion that I must be dishonest — I must be cherry-picking my evidence, or something — because the way I describe it, I’m always right while the people who disagree with me are always wrong. And not just wrong, they’re often knaves or fools. How likely is that?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knaves, Fools, and Me (Meta)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-28T12:01:43+0000', 'document_