ns', 'value': 'Curry, Stephen', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Dwight', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T14:47:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75844e5b-f891-5412-b240-e874f3683858', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75844e5b-f891-5412-b240-e874f3683858'}, {'abstract': 'Why it’s easy to imagine a comprehensive reform blowing up in the Republican Party’s face.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/29/immigration-reform-and-the-lessons-of-2012/', 'snippet': 'Why it’s easy to imagine a comprehensive reform blowing up in the Republican Party’s face.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column sounded some skeptical notes about the immigration reform being considered in the Senate, raising the question of whether an America whose native-born working class is facing a slow-burning socieconomic crisis is really in an ideal position to assimilate low-skilled immigrants at an increasing clip. What I didn’t say is that these intertwined issues\xa0— working class dysfunction and the hard path to assimilation\xa0— are part of why the Republican elite’s political theory of immigration reform looks so dubious.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Immigration Reform and the Lessons of 2012', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 3, 'major':