ne, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T15:23:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ed32647-d6de-5de5-90fd-c37830084a83', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ed32647-d6de-5de5-90fd-c37830084a83'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court’s decision leaves in place a ruling that blocks a law making it a crime to harbor or transport immigrants not authorized to be in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/us/supreme-court-declines-case-on-alabama-immigration-law.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court’s decision leaves in place a ruling that blocks a law making it a crime to harbor or transport immigrants not authorized to be in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Monday declined to consider a case concerning a tough 2011 Alabama immigration law that makes it a crime to harbor or transport immigrants not authorized to be in the United States or to induce them to enter or live in the state. The move left in place an appeals court ruling blocking the law.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices Decline to Take Case on Alabama Immigration Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T15:24:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ec35209-15ec-5be4-9cc7-cce764d01099', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ec35209-15ec-5be4-9cc7-cce764d01099'}, {'abstract': 'Asking ourselves what we truly value about money can lead to smarter, more fulfilling financial decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/29/whats-important-about-money-to-you/', 'snippet': 'Asking ourselves what we truly value about money can lead to smarter, more fulfilling financial decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah, and is the director of investor education at The BAM Alliance. His book, “The Behavior Gap,” was published this year. His sketches are archived\xa0on the Bucks blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Important About Money to You?', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T15:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5521d6dc-066d-5bc7-9387-a059718d1520', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5521d6dc-066d-5bc7-9387-a059718d1520'}, {'abstract': 'An excerpt from a new book by David Sheff on America’s drug problem and why we’ve failed in our efforts to combat it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/science/clean-overcoming-addiction-and-ending-americas-greatest-tragedy.html', 'snippet': 'An excerpt from a new book by David Sheff on America’s drug problem and why we’ve failed in our efforts to combat it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The view that drug use is a moral choice is pervasive, pernicious, and wrong. So are the corresponding beliefs about the addicted — that they’re weak, selfish, and dissolute; if they weren’t, when their excessive drug taking and drinking began to harm them, they’d stop. The reality is far different. Using drugs or not isn’t about willpower or character. Most problematic drug use is related to stress, trauma, genetic predisposition, mild or serious mental illness, use at an early age, or some combination of those. Even in their relentless destruction and self-destruction, the addicted aren’t bad people. They’re gravely ill, afflicted with a chronic, progressive, and often terminal disease.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1367, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30scibooks-cover/30scibooks-cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Clean: Overcoming Addiction and Ending America’s Greatest Tragedy’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Methamphetamines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T15:33:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Sheff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbf00084-1d16-5b19-847c-3194c2f97f1e', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbf00084-1d16-5b19-847c-3194c2f97f1e'}, {'abstract': 'In his new book, David Sheff changes perspective on dealing with addiction, writing as an advocate and journalist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/science/clean-book-review-once-an-addicts-father-now-an-advocate.html', 'snippet': 'In his new book, David Sheff changes perspective on dealing with addiction, writing as an advocate and journalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Addiction swallows lives whole, and not only with overdose, illness and concentric cycles of rehab and relapse. A less onerous but still tenacious kind of post-traumatic stress disorder may develop as well, with recovered addicts and their families compulsively reliving the past in private — or, like David Sheff and his son Nic, in public.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/science/30SCIB_SPAN/30SCIB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Addict’s Father, Now Advocate', 'kicker': 'Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Addict’s Father, Now Advocate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheff, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T15:33:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abigail Zuger, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abigail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zuger', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/18419c15-3356-5542-b883-53a8cf4aba48', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18419c15-3356-5542-b883-53a8cf4aba48'}, {'abstract': 'For a writer, racing was all he did, all he wanted to do until he got old enough to think about it.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/29/a-lifes-work-interwoven-with-horse-racing/', 'snippet': 'For a writer, racing was all he did, all he wanted to do until he got old enough to think about it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shecky Greene. My family used to have a hat pool for the Kentucky Derby. Someone writes the horses’ names on slips of paper and puts the slips in a hat. Then you pay $5 and pull a name. That’s your horse. If he wins, you get the money.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Life’s Work, Interwoven With Horse Racing', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank':