sk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Friedman, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7325563-5da1-51e6-a5e9-aaed0aee3027', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7325563-5da1-51e6-a5e9-aaed0aee3027'}, {'abstract': 'Sergio Marchionne has long envisioned a fully integrated Fiat-Chrysler as the first step in a continuing consolidation designed to squeeze out the excess capacity that plagues the car industry, particularly in Fiat’s home market.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/04/29/how-a-fiat-chrysler-merger-could-work/', 'snippet': 'Sergio Marchionne has long envisioned a fully integrated Fiat-Chrysler as the first step in a continuing consolidation designed to squeeze out the excess capacity that plagues the car industry, particularly in Fiat’s home market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took about a generation before Italian immigrants to the United States adopted the ways of their new home and shook off those of the old country. Italy’s premier industrial enterprise may do it in a matter of years. A spat over Fiat’s plans to fully acquire Chrysler, Detroit’s No. 3 car maker, could hasten the process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Fiat-Chrysler Merger Could Work', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marchionne, Sergio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'ran