Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T20:17:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0661a84c-86e7-50cf-9d6c-71b9be81f3e9', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0661a84c-86e7-50cf-9d6c-71b9be81f3e9'}, {'abstract': '“I’ve got to eat where the people I cover go.”', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/29/a-reporter-eats-and-drinks-the-news/', 'snippet': '“I’ve got to eat where the people I cover go.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charlie Gasparino, a senior correspondent with the Fox Business Network and Fox News, is known (some might say notorious) for his dogged, determined pursuit of scoops on the titans of Wall Street. He is known, too, for his unusual habit of going on the air and chatting about what he had to eat (and drink) the night before. Mr. Gasparino, 51, met for a late lunch at San Pietro, one of his Midtown haunts. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reporter Eats and Drinks the News', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Relationship With Food', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-29T20:20:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d769d9f8-d5dc-5b6d-95a0-8b36f2ce6598', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d769d9f8-d5dc-5b6d-95a0-8b36f2ce6598'}, {'abstract': 'The composer and pianist Vijay Iyer performed solo and with his trio and a sextet at Carnegie Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/arts/music/vijay-iyer-alone-and-with-others-at-zankel-hall.html', 'snippet': 'The composer and pianist Vijay Iyer performed solo and with his trio and a sextet at Carnegie Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were many possible points of entry to Vijay Iyer’s immersive three-part concert at Zankel Hall on Saturday night, but the most illuminating was also the most deceptively simple. “Maybe I’ll play a standard,” Mr. Iyer said casually, introducing a solo piano mini-set near the program’s midpoint. Then he began a murky, unsettled, slow-rumbling prelude, only gradually recognizable as the songbook ballad “Body and Soul.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/vijay/vijay-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/vijay/vijay-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/vijay/vijay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im