verlooked when Tony Award nominations were announced on Tuesday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/tony-award-nominations-who-got-snubbed/', 'snippet': 'Bette Midler, Fiona Shaw, Alan Cumming and Alec Baldwin were among the high-profile performers who were overlooked when Tony Award nominations were announced on Tuesday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tony voters might want to lie low today if they don’t want to get devoured by Bette Midler, whose unrecognized turn as the superagent Sue Mengers in the one-woman show “I’ll Eat You Last” was perhaps the most glaring of snubs when the nominations were announced on Tuesday morning. Ms. Midler, an A-list star making her return to Broadway after an absence of some 40 years, was well-received for her performance as Ms. Mengers, a tough-as-nails Hollywood player, in “I’ll Eat You Last.” But neither she nor this original comedy, written by John Logan (a Tony Award-winner for “Red”), received a nomination. (Still, she’ll always have “Rochelle, Rochelle: the Musical.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/30/arts/mengers/mengers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Award Nominations: Who Got Snubbed?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baldwin, Alec', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chastain, Jessica', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cumming, Alan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Katie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johansson, Scarlett', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Midler, Bette', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shaw, Fiona', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-30T13:35:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9ec3f89-7b07-5926-b161-39752d7010ef', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9ec3f89-7b07-5926-b161-39752d7010ef'}, {'abstract': 'Something not rotten in the state of economics.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/still-coring-after-all-these-years/', 'snippet': 'Something not rotten in the state of economics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blogging is a bit like teaching the same class year after year; inevitably there are moments when you feel exasperated at the class’s failure to grasp some point you know you explained at length — then you realize that this was last year or the year before, and it was to a different group of people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Coring After All These Years', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-04-30T13:35:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/916605b9-f1be-552a-8a60-d6465b8a974c', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/916605b9-f1be-552a-8a60-d6465b8a974c'}, {'abstract': 'Through a motion graphic video, TicketCity takes a look at performance trends at the Kentucky Derby throughout the years.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/04/30/video-the-most-exciting-two-minutes-in-sports/', 'snippet': 'Through a motion graphic video, TicketCity takes a look at performance trends at the Kentucky Derby throughout the years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our friends at TicketCity sent us this motion graphic video, which takes a look at performance trends at the Kentucky Derby throughout the years. Enjoy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: The Most Exciting Two Minutes in Sports', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-04-30T13:49:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4266e5b0-d9cf-5d12-8eaa-f90a077f689e', 'word_count': 26, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4266e5b0-d9cf-5d12-8eaa-f90a077f689e'}, {'abstract': 'After a doctor was convicted for his role in doping cyclists, the Spanish judge ruled that blood bags gathered in 2006 from the doctor’s office could not be released to antidoping authorities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/sports/cycling/spanish-doctor-sentenced-in-operation-puerto-doping-case-in-cycling.html', 'snippet': 'After a doctor was convicted for his role in doping cyclists, the Spanish judge ruled that blood bags gathered in 2006 from the doctor’s office could not be released to antidoping authorities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Spanish judge ordered on Tuesday the destruction of more than 200 blood bags seized in a raid of a major European doping ring that catered to elite athletes, thwarting an effort to uncover possibly one of the biggest doping scandals in history.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/sports/01doping/01doping-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/01/sports/01doping/01doping-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 756}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/sports/01doping/01doping-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 813, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/sports/01doping/01doping-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1626, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop